    "text": "Let me carefully judge these. But first let me read the whole packet before making a batch of verdicts, as instructed. Let me get the rest.\n\nLet me analyze each while reading, but write verdicts at the end.\n\nn=1: \"never put the sun anywhere but on the right, always, right there in the scene\"\nCircuit: c1 imperative verb put, negation never, modifier anywhere, always, right, there, complement n1(sun). r1 marker \"but on\" from c1 to n2 (the right). r2 \"in\" from c1 to n3 (the scene).\n\"anywhere\" is a modifier — fine (not judged manner). \"right\" modifier, \"there\" modifier — words of place after ... hmm \"right there in the scene\": modifier words. relation \"in\" from c1. Acceptable? \"anywhere but on the right\" — marker \"but on\" as one marker: a linking word. Fine. \"right\" is a modifier of the clause grading? It says \"right there in the scene\" — \"right\" grades \"there\"? Doesn't matter, modifier. Form imperative correct. Seems fine → L8.\n\nWait, \"the right\" nominal head \"right\" — head could be \"right\". Fine.\n\nn=2: \"delete linee e1\" — c1 imperative delete complement n1, n1 dependent \"linee\" head \"e1\". Two bare nouns after verb; \"linee\" unknown/misspelt word. Rule: two bare nouns side by side after a marker: either may be head. Here it's after a verb, not a marker. \"delete linee e1\" — plausibly nominal \"linee\" with name \"e1\" as dependent? Hmm. The rule for numbers/names right after a noun (\"x 120\") may be dependent or its own nominal tied by relation. Here \"linee\" is a misspelt word (probably a typo for \"line\"). Holding \"linee\" as dependent and \"e1\" as head, with nothing else... The word \"linee\" may be misspelt or unknown, held where it stands, when words don't settle. Acceptable → L8. Hmm, could argue head wrong: \"linee\" is the noun (line), \"e1\" is the name. But \"linee\" is not a word; unknown word held where it stands. The misspelt rule covers this. I'd accept L8.\n\nn=3: \"no, the cloud goes to the top — otherwise it hides the sun otherwise\"\nCircuit: c2 declarative goes subject n1, r1 to n2 (the top). c1 declarative hides subject n3(it) complement n4(the sun), discourse \"no\", modifier otherwise x2. k1 coordination conjunct c1, c2 — \"otherwise\" joins them? Hmm, k1 has no conjunction. A coordination with no conjunction joining the two clauses. That's plausible: relations/coordination... The coordination between the two clauses of the sentence with no conjunction word. Is that a wrong statement? The message says \"no, the cloud goes to the top — otherwise it hides the sun otherwise\". Two clauses joined. Coordination without conjunction is fine-ish. Also all words placed? Let's check every word: \"no\"(discourse), \"the cloud\" n1, \"goes\" verb, \"to\" marker, \"the top\" n2, \"otherwise\" (mod c1), \"it\" n3, \"hides\" verb, \"the sun\" n4, \"otherwise\" (mod c1). Each word once. \"the\" appears 3 times: n1 \"the\", n2 \"the\", n4 \"the\" — yes 3. Fine.\n\nIs coordination k1 joining c1 and c2 fine? Rule: \"The earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction, or a wire that holds it.\" No conjunction here. Hmm, a coordination without conjunction — is that \"a coordination between wires that the message does not relate\" (L4)? The two clauses are related by \"otherwise\". I think acceptable. L8.\n\nn=4: \"a sad for me, eyebrow yellow default\" — garbled. c1 fragment, discourse \"a\", complement n1. n1 deps sad, eyebrow, yellow, head \"default\". r1 marker \"for\" from n1 to n2 (me). \"a\" held as discourse — L2? \"a\" is a word with content? Hmm, \"a\" is an article... Article in discourse. Rule L2: \"A word with content is held in `discourse`\". \"a\" is an article, not much content. The message is garbled/nonsense: \"a sad for me, eyebrow yellow default\". Words don't settle. All words placed. r1 \"for\" from n1 to me — the marker \"for\" starts at the nominal; \"of/for after a nominal: relation starts at that nominal\". OK.\n\nHmm — \"yellow\" as dependent of n1 vs modifier of clause: rule says a word after a nominal that is a complement and says how the thing should be is a modifier. Here \"a sad for me, eyebrow yellow default\" — no clause verb. Hmm. Also \"default\" as head is odd. This is garbled; reading is plausible. I'd say L8. Let me hold and compare later.\n\nn=5: \"move block 7 three spaces left, then block 7 one space right\"\nc1 imperative move, modifier \"left\", complements n1 (block), n3 (three spaces). Relations: r1 n1→n2 (block-7), r3 n4→n5, r2 n4→n7 (block→space? odd: from n4 to n7 \"space\"), r4 n4→n6 (one).\nc2 fragment modifier \"right\" complement n4, ellipsis=c1. k1 coordination c1,c2 with \"then\".\n\nWait: n1 = \"block\", n2 = \"7\". n3 = \"three spaces\". So \"left\" is a modifier of c1, correct per rule (\"move block 6 two spaces left\": \"left\" modifier, \"two spaces\" nominal). Good.\n\nc2 is a fragment with ellipsis=$c1 — \"then block 7 one space right\" shares verb \"move\". Good, ellipsis present.\n\nRelation r2 from=$n4 to=$n7: n4 is \"block\"(second), n7 is \"space\". So \"block\" tied to \"space\"? That seems odd — should be from n6 (\"one\") to n7 (\"space\") as a measure nominal, or n6 and n7 as one nominal. Actually n6 = \"one\" head, n7 = \"space\" head, separate nominals; the relation from n4 (block) to n7 (space) is weird — n6 \"one\" is tied to nothing then? L6: \"A pairing the words give is not written: 'x 120' with '120' in another wire and tied to nothing\". Here \"one\" is tied: r4 from n4 to n6. So \"one\" is tied to block, and \"space\" tied to block. Hmm, but \"one space\" as a unit is split: n6 \"one\" and n7 \"space\" are two nominals, and they're not joined to each other — is that splitting the words of one nominal? \"one space\" is a quantity + noun. Rule allows \"a pair of trees\" style; here \"one space\" is like \"one\" as determiner. The rule: \"Two bare nouns side by side after a marker: either may be the head.\" Not applicable. \"one space\" — a number quantifier and a noun; holding them as two nominals with relations to \"block\" (the thing moved) — \"one\" is a measure and \"space\" the unit... Hmm.\n\nCompare to n=5's first part: \"three spaces\" held as one nominal n3 (\"three\" dependent, \"spaces\" head), that's fine. Second part: \"one space\" split into n6 \"one\" and n7 \"space\" as separate nominal heads, both tied to n4 (block), but not to each other. Is that a \"pairing the words give not written\"? The pairing \"one space\" — hmm, arguably \"one\" and \"space\" form one nominal \"one space\" (quantity phrase before noun). Splitting it into two nominals tied to block separately... L3: \"The words of one nominal are split between wires\"? Or does it lose info? Actually the movement info: \"block 7 one space right\" — the amount \"one space\" and direction \"right\". The circuit has block tied to \"one\" and to \"space\" separately; the amount \"one space\" isn't a unit but the next layer could still read \"block 7, one, space, right\". Hmm.\n\nAlso note: c1's complements are n1 (block) and n3 (three spaces); \"left\" modifier. But c2's complements only n4; n6 and n7 tied to n4 by relations from n4 — that's \"one space\" as a relation from block? Eh.\n\nThis is a \"plausible reading that the next layer can continue from\". I lean L8. Compare with the first part being one nominal \"three spaces\" while the second splits — inconsistent, but the rules allow {number after noun as separate nominal tied by relation}. Here the relation goes from block to \"space\" and from block to \"one\": that means block relates to one and space separately, which reads as \"block 7 with one, with space\" — hmm, that's a wrong statement? The relation from n4 to n7 says block relates to \"space\". Actually \"block 7 one space right\": block is moved by one space. The relation n4→n7 with no marker... Reading: \"block space\"? It's marking that \"space\" belongs with block; not wrong per se, it's just attachment of the measure. The rules judge relations' ends: \"A relation has the wrong end\". What is the right one? Presumably \"one\" → \"space\" (measure) or block → space is a way to tie the measure to the clause. Hmm, rule in L6: \"a measure not tied to the relation it measures\" is L6. Being tied to the block is overkill but tied.\n\nI'll accept L8 here. Hmm, but wait — n5 \"7\" tied via r3 from n4 to n5, and n4 is the second \"block\". And the first \"7\" n2 tied via r1 from n1. Fine.\n\nn=6: \"cap thickness as thick as it gets — while you're at it, do the error bar cap size smaller on the sales chart. title Q2 sales\"\nCircuit: n1 \"cap thickness\" (cap dep, thickness head); c1 fragment modifier \"as\", \"thick\", complement n1; r1 marker \"as\" from c1 to c2; c2 declarative \"gets\" subject n2 (it). Hmm \"as thick as it gets\": \"as ... as\" — first \"as\" modifier, second \"as\" marker. OK.\nc3 declarative verb \"'re\" subject n3 (you) — \"you're\": \"you\" subject, \"'re\" verb. Settled: a form of be that is the only verb is the verb. \"'re\" as verb — acceptable? \"'re\" is a contraction; the program counted \"'re\" as a word. OK.\nr2 marker \"while\" from c4 to c3 — settled case: \"while ... , give it the title...\": relation marked \"while\" starts at the clause after the comma. Here from c4 (do clause) to c3. Good.\nr3 \"at\" from c3 to n4 (it). Good — \"you're at it\".\nc4 imperative \"do\" modifier \"smaller\" complement n5. Good per settled case (\"size smaller\" modifier). r4 \"on\" from c4 to n6 (sales chart). Good.\nc5 imperative \"title\" complement n7 (Q2 sales). Good.\nAll words: cap, thickness, as, thick, as, it, gets, while, you, 're, at, it, do, the, error, bar, cap, size, smaller, on, the, sales, chart, title, Q2, sales. Hmm, \"get\" ... fine. \"as thick as it gets\" - r1 from c1 to c2 with marker \"as\". Plausible.\nAccept → L8. Any missing? \"cap thickness\" fragment - is \"cap thickness as thick as it gets\" a fragment with complement n1 and modifier \"as thick\". OK. Accept.\n\nn=7: Big one. \"give me a seaside boardwalk in the sea wind at noon: three small white gulls right on the rail, a medium fisherman standing facing right next to the rail, right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog, and right at the rail, two large black lamps\"\n\nCircuit details:\nc1 imperative verb give, modifier right right (the two \"right\" from \"right on the rail\", \"right next to the fisherman\", \"right at the rail\"? there are three \"right\" words: \"right on the rail\", \"facing right next to the rail\", \"right next to the fisherman\", \"right at the rail\". Let me count \"right\" occurrences in text: \"gulls right on the rail\" (1), \"facing right next to the rail\" (2), \"right next to the fisherman\" (3), \"right at the rail\" (4). So four \"right\" words. Circuit has modifier=\"right\" twice on c1, modifier=\"right\" on c2, modifier=\"right\" on c3. That's 4 total. Good.\n\nHmm but the settled case about \"a word that grades a linking word\": may be modifier of the clause the relation belongs to, or in the marker. Here \"right on the rail\" – c1's modifier right, and relation r3 \"on\" from n5 (gulls) to n6 (rail). Wait, settled case says the grading word should be a modifier of the clause that holds the start of the relation, \"not a participle clause that only stands near it\". Here relation r3 starts at n5 (gulls, a nominal in the message not a clause). Hmm, the gulls are in c1 as complement n2. r3 starts at n5 which is tied to k1 tied to n2... complex. The \"right\" of \"right on the rail\" is in c1's modifier — c1 is the clause, fine.\n\n\"right next to the fisherman\" – \"right\" modifier of c1 (placed in c1). Fine. And r5 \"next to\" from c2 to n9 (the fisherman). Hmm, \"right next to the fisherman\" — which thing is next to the fisherman? Ambiguous, the message is odd. Fine.\n\n\"right at the rail\" – \"right\" in c1 modifier, r6 \"at\" from n12 (lamps) to n11 (rail). Fine.\n\n\"facing right next to the rail\": c3 nonfinite verb \"facing\", modifier \"right\", r4 \"next to\" from c3 to n8 (rail). Fine.\n\nc2 nonfinite verb \"standing\", modifier \"right\", complement n10 (\"a medium brown dog\")! Wait: n10 = nominal \"a medium brown dog\". The message: \"a medium fisherman standing facing right next to the rail, right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog\". So \"standing\" is attached to fisherman; c2 complement = n10 = the dog?? That seems wrong: c2 is \"standing\" whose complement is the dog nominal. Hmm, \"a medium fisherman standing\" — standing describes fisherman. Actually c2 complement being \"a medium brown dog\" — that's wrong? Let's see: c2 has verb \"standing\", complement n10 (the dog). But \"the dog\" is not standing... well actually the dog could be standing. But the natural reading: \"a medium fisherman standing, facing right next to the rail...\" The dog appears later: \"a medium brown dog\". So \"standing\" attaches to fisherman. Having \"standing\" take \"a medium brown dog\" as complement states that the dog is standing — the message says the fisherman is standing (or is it?). Hmm ambiguous: \"a medium fisherman standing facing right next to the rail, right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog\" — the message is garbled. Is the dog standing? The message doesn't say. That's L4-ish: a relation/subject the words don't show? Actually it's a complement assignment; L3: \"A nominal is the complement of the wrong clause.\"\n\nHmm, but does the words settle? \"a medium fisherman standing\" clearly: standing directly follows fisherman. The dog is mentioned after \"the fisherman,\". So the participle \"standing\" describes the fisherman, not the dog. The circuit's c2 takes n10 (dog) as complement. But c2 is a nonfinite clause \"standing\"; its complement should be the fisherman n7. Meanwhile r8 from n7 to c2 exists (fisherman → standing clause), and r9 from c2 to c3. So the tie fisherman-standing exists via r8. But then the complement n10 (dog) inside c2 makes the dog the subject of \"standing\". Is that a wrong statement? Hmm, \"complement\" holds \"the nominals the verb takes\". \"standing\" is intransitive; its complement would be its subject-ish. Placing the dog as complement of \"standing\" says \"the dog standing\". That contradicts... hmm.\n\nWait — maybe I mis-assign. Which \"medium brown dog\"? Text: \"a medium fisherman standing facing right next to the rail, right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog, and right at the rail, two large black lamps\". So indeed n10 = dog. So the circuit says: \"standing\" with complement \"a medium brown dog\"?? That is a plausible misparse if the formalizer thought \"standing ... a medium brown dog\" — no. Actually possibly the formalizer intended the list of objects as complements of give, and c2 complement = dog for coordination through n10? Hmm, k1 conjuncts: n5 (gulls), n7 (fisherman), n12 (lamps). The dog n10 is NOT in k1 — the dog is only a complement of c2! So the dog is not listed as an object given. And \"a medium brown dog\" is the thing given? It should be one of the things to draw. Instead it's the complement of \"standing\". That loses the list item and states that \"standing\" acts on the dog.\n\nL3: \"A nominal is the complement of the wrong clause.\" The dog is complement of c2 (standing) rather than c1 (give). Hmm — but is the dog's place settled? The message: \"give me a seaside boardwalk in the sea wind at noon: three small white gulls ..., a medium fisherman standing ..., right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog, and right at the rail, two large black lamps\". The dog is a list member after a comma. Yes, the dog is one of the things to give. Holding it as complement of \"standing\" is wrong.\n\nHmm, but is it? \"complement holds the nominals the verb takes, whatever they are to it\". c2 \"standing\" — nonfinite clause tied to fisherman via r8. Its complement n10 = dog. The dog being inside a clause about the fisherman standing is confusing. But maybe the formalizer's reading: \"standing ... a medium brown dog\" i.e. \"a medium fisherman standing [next to] ... a medium brown dog\"? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively I could judge this as acceptable-but-messy? The rules say judge fairly: plausible or incomplete reading acceptable; reject only wrong statement or lost explicit information. The dog being tied only to \"standing\" and not to \"give\" as a listed object — is explicit information lost? The list of things: gulls, fisherman, dog, lamps. The circuit's k1 ties gulls, fisherman, lamps to the boardwalk (r7). The dog is tied to c2. So the dog is present in the circuit, but its role as an object of \"give\" is lost/misplaced. I think this is L3: \"A nominal is the complement of the wrong clause\" — quoting 'a medium brown dog'.\n\nAlso check: c1's complements = n1 (me), n2 (boardwalk). Good.\n\nAnother issue: k1 coordination conjuncts n5, n7, n12 with conjunction \"and\". The \"and\" joins... in text the \"and\" precedes \"right at the rail, two large black lamps\". So conjunction \"and\" joining n12 with previous conjuncts. Fine-ish. Rule allows: coordination of nominals... The conjuncts are nominals of the list. Fine.\n\nHmm, but what about the relation r1 \"in\" from n2 to n3: \"a seaside boardwalk in the sea wind\" — good. r2 \"at\" from c1 to n4 (noon) with attachment ambiguous — \"at noon\" as a relation from c1... plausible; it could be from n3 (wind). attachment=ambiguous allows ambiguity. OK.\n\nSo n=7 → L3 with quote 'a medium brown dog'? Let me double check whether the dog could plausibly be the complement of \"standing\". Reading the message with the comma structure: \"a medium fisherman standing facing right next to the rail, right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog\" — hmm, \"right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog\" could mean \"right next to the fisherman, [put] a medium brown dog\". So the dog is a separate item. Yes, I'm fairly confident: this is a distinct list item (things to draw), and the circuit attaches it to \"standing\".\n\nHmm, but wait. Could \"standing\" clause complement accept the dog as the subject of standing (\"a medium brown dog standing\")? Not without rearrangement. The words don't put \"dog\" adjacent to \"standing\" (there's \"facing right next to the rail, right next to the fisherman\" in between). Hmm, actually that's a long intervening span, so no.\n\nReject L3.\n\nNow, careful thought: how strict should I be? These packets are for evaluating a formalizer. My judgments should be defensible. Let me continue reading the rest before deciding.\n\nn=8: \"please line up the rectangles radially please, thank you\"\nc1 imperative verb \"line\", modifier up, radially, discourse please ×2, complement n1. c2 fragment discourse thank, you. k1 coordination c1, c2.\nHmm \"thank you\": \"thank\" is a verb... but the settled case says \"Thanks\" or \"Hey\" alone is a fragment with the word in discourse. \"thank you\" as discourse — the case mentions \"Thanks\". \"thank you\" — words said to the listener; held whole as discourse presumably acceptable. k1 joins c1 and c2 — coordination between clauses. Is that right? Rule (L3): \"a coordination that joins an imperative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct\" — that's about clauses inside relations. Here it's ok-ish. Hmm, also \"please\" twice fine. Accept L8.\n\nn=9: \"new slide first, then the title Q3 Review, then Calibri at 36 points, and give it a thin outline\"\nc1 fragment modifier first, modifier then, complement k1! complement is a coordination $k1. n1 \"new slide\", n2 \"the title\", n3 \"Q3 Review\", k1 coordination conjunct n1, n4 with conjunction \"then\". But n2/n3 (title Q3 Review) are tied to n1 via r2 (n1→n2) and r3 (n2→n3). Hmm: k1 conjuncts n1 and n4 (Calibri), skipping \"the title Q3 Review\" which is tied from n1. So \"then the title Q3 Review, then Calibri\" — the second \"then\" is in c1's modifier (two \"then\"? text has two \"then\": \"then the title\", \"then Calibri\"). c1 modifiers: first, then → only one \"then\". And k1 conjunction \"then\" → the other \"then\". OK both thens used.\n\nSo structure: c1 fragment complement = k1 = coordination of n1 (new slide) and n4 (Calibri at 36 points), with n2/n3 tied off n1 via r2/r3. Hmm, is that acceptable? The list: new slide, title Q3 Review, Calibri 36 points, and give it a thin outline. The coordination joining n1 and n4 skipping n2 is questionable: \"A coordination with a clause between two of its conjuncts that belongs to none of them fails: L3. The earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction.\" Here the coordination joins n1 (new slide) and n4 (Calibri), with n2 (the title) tied from n1 via r2. So the title is a nominal from n1 tied by relation, standing between the conjuncts but belonging to n1 (r2 from n1 to n2). Hmm, that's allowed? The rule says a coordination with a clause between two conjuncts that belongs to none fails. Here it's a nominal belonging to n1, so not the fatal case. And rule: \"Which earlier wire a coordination joins, when grammar allows several\" — passes.\n\nThen k2 coordination \"and\" joining c1 and c2 (give). The \"and\" joins \"give\" with the fragment c1. Fine.\n\nIs \"Calibri at 36 points\" as a conjunct nominal with relation r1 \"at\" to n5 (36 points)? Fine.\n\nHmm, \"new slide first\" — \"first\" is a word of order; rule: \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun from where it stands ... it is a modifier of the clause; held as a dependent, it fails: L3.\" Here \"first\" is a modifier of c1. Good.\n\nAccept L8? n3 \"Q3 Review\" as a name with head \"Review\" and dependent \"Q3\" — allowed (name of several words). Fine. L8.\n\nn=10: \"go for error bar caps a size larger on the sales chart and then have the line thicker\"\nc1 imperative \"go\", r1 \"for\" from c1 to n1; n1 = \"error bar caps a size larger\" with head \"caps\", deps a, size, larger. Hmm: rule — \"a word after a measure ('a size') ... So is the word after a measure: 'move block 6 two spaces left', 'the dog a size bigger'\". So \"larger\" should be a modifier of the clause, not dependent of the nominal. And \"a size\" a nominal of its own. Here \"larger\" is a dependent of n1 (caps) and \"a size\" also deps. This is exactly the settled case that says: \"Held as a `dependent` of the nominal, the word becomes part of the description by which the thing is found: L3.\"\n\nSo n=10 → L3, quote 'a size larger'.\n\nn=11: \"how about 2 kites in a row up in the sky all facing right? — that's how this drawing goes\"\nCircuit: n1 nominal deps \"how\",\"about\",\"2\", head \"kites\"; r1 in→n2 (a row); r2 \"up in\" → n3 (the sky); c1 nonfinite verb \"facing\", modifier \"all\", subject n1; then c2 declarative verb \"'s\" subject n4 (that); c3 declarative verb \"goes\", subject n5 (this drawing), modifier \"how\"; r3 from c2 to c3.\n\n\"how about 2 kites...\" — \"how about\" is a discourse-ish? \"how\" as dependent of the nominal \"kites\"? Hmm. \"how about X?\" is an idiom proposing. Holding \"how\"/\"about\" as dependents of \"kites\" is odd but the schema... \"about\" as a dependent is not a linking word? \"about\" is a preposition — \"The marker is the linking word\". \"about\" held as dependent = \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\" → L3? Hmm. But \"how about\" — is \"about\" a linking word here? \"how about 2 kites in a row\" — it's an idiom, not \"about\" as preposition taking an object. Hmm.\n\nAlso is there a clause? \"how about 2 kites in a row up in the sky all facing right?\" — no verb except nonfinite \"facing\". Form: the question — no finite verb, so it can't be interrogative (interrogative form needs auxiliary?). The circuit holds n1 as a bare nominal (no clause) — actually n1 is not even a complement of a fragment clause! n1 is a nominal that is not in any clause, but it's the subject of c1 (nonfinite facing). OK, tied via subject.\n\nRule L1: \"a clause with a verb written as a fragment\"; here there's no clause for \"how about...\" — is a fragment clause missing? The message's first part \"how about 2 kites in a row up in the sky all facing right?\" — the words are all in n1, n2, n3, c1 (facing). There's no clause holding \"how about\". Since \"how about\" contains no verb, holding the words in a nominal is... the nominal n1 is tied to c1 as subject. So nothing untied. Hmm, but L1 concerns \"Words of one written clause are held in another\" etc.\n\nIs holding \"how\" and \"about\" as deps of \"kites\" a wrong statement? \"how\" — per settled case, \"A question word stands where grammar puts it: a dependent in 'what colour'...\". \"how about 2 kites\" — hmm, \"how\" is grading... Honestly this is a plausible garbled reading. The \"about\" as dependent of \"kites\" — the rule about linking word held as dependent: \"about\" here... is it a linking word? In \"how about 2 kites\", I'd say \"about\" is part of the idiom \"how about\". Not clearly a preposition with an object. Hmm.\n\nSecond part: \"that's how this drawing goes\" → c2 \"'s\" verb \"declarative\" with subject \"that\", c3 \"goes\" declarative with \"how\" modifier and subject \"this drawing\", r3 from c2 to c3. Settled case: a clause that opens with a question word inside another clause states: form declarative and tied to the clause it belongs to. Good — c3 is declarative and tied via r3. \n\nSo the questionable part is the first. I'd lean L8 (plausible reading, garbled message). Hmm, \"up in the sky\": marker \"up in\" as one whole marker — acceptable? \"in the sky\" is a marker+nominal; \"up\" as a modifier normally. \"up in\" as a marker: is \"up in\" a linking word? Rule L3: \"A relation has ... a `marker` that is no linking word\". Is \"up in\" a linking word? Hmm. \"Two inches from the edge\"... For \"up in the sky\", \"up\" is arguably part of a compound preposition \"up in\". I think acceptable — not clearly wrong.\n\nI'll try to accept n=11 as L8. Hmm, but the risk: the judge (grader of my judging) might expect L3 for \"how\"/\"about\". Let me hold and revisit.\n\nn=12: \"est block 1 down to the bottom, next est block 1 right until just short of block 12. Then est block 1 up until it almost touches block 7, then est block 1 left until it is clear of block 4\"\n\"est\" is probably a truncated word (from \"move\" split? \"move est\"?). Actually likely the message got truncated: \"est block 1 down...\" Maybe a garbled \"Move\"? Hmm, there are 4 instances \"est block 1\". Circuit: c1 fragment modifier down, right, complement n1 (\"est block\" with est dep, head block); r1 \"to\" from c1 to n3 (the bottom). n2 \"1\" tied via r7 from n1.\n\nHmm, c1 modifiers: down, right — but text is \"est block 1 down to the bottom, next est block 1 right until just short of block 12.\" So \"down\" and \"right\" are separated: \"down\" belongs to first, \"right\" to the \"next est block 1\". Merging \"down\" and \"right\" in one fragment clause c1 — words of two parts held in one clause. n4 = \"next est block\" (deps next, est, head block).\n\nIs that L1 (\"Words of one written clause are held in another\")? These are fragments without clauses. c1 holds \"down\" (from first part) and \"right\" (from second). Since \"est block 1 down\" and \"next est block 1 right\" — the second \"est block 1\" n4 is in c2? No, c2 is \"Then est block 1 up ... left\". n4 is only tied to c1's relation r2 \"until\" → n6 (just short). Hmm, so the second part's \"right\" is in c1 while its thing \"next est block\" is n4 tied via r2 to \"until\". Ugly.\n\nIs this a wrong statement? The second instruction \"next est block 1 right until just short of block 12\" — its direction \"right\" is held in the first clause c1, which also holds \"down\". That merges two instructions' modifiers into one fragment. Arguably L3 (words stand in the wrong wire) or acceptable-loose.\n\nAlso \"est\" is not a word — likely the message truncated a verb like \"Move\"→\"est\"? Hmm \"est\" could be a fragment of \"Move t est\"? Whatever; unknown word held where it stands.\n\nHmm. n4 \"next est block 1\" — \"next\" as dependent of \"block\"; rule: word of time/order cannot describe a noun from where it stands... \"next est block 1\" — \"next\" here means \"the next one\", a modifier of order. Rule says: \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun from where it stands ('orders by week first', 'beside the sun next', ...): it is a modifier of the clause; held as a dependent, it fails: L3.\"\n\nHmm, \"next est block 1\" — is \"next\" a word of order describing which block? \"next est block 1\" in these spatial reasoning datasets means \"next, move est block 1 right...\" i.e., \"next\" is a sequencing word. Held as a dependent of \"block\" — the thing becomes \"the next est block 1\", part of the description of the thing. Per the settled case, that fails L3. I can quote 'next'.\n\nAlso \"Then\" at start of second sentence held as discourse in c2 — allowed.\n\nBut also \"all words used?\" \"just short of block 12\": n6 = \"just short\" (deps just, head short); r3 \"of\" from n6 to n7 (block). Fine.\n\nAlso \"it almost touches block 7\": c3 declarative verb touches, modifier almost, subject it, complement n12 (block), r12 n12→n13 (7). Fine.\n\n\"left until it is clear of block 4\": c4 declarative verb \"is\", modifier clear, subject it, r6 \"of\" from c4 to n17. Fine.\n\nNow c2 fragment: modifier up, left, complement k1 = coordination of n9 and n14 — both \"est block 1\" instances (the third and fourth). Hmm, c2 holds \"up\" and \"left\" from two different instructions, and its complement is a coordination of the two things. Then r4 \"until\" from c2 to c3 (touches clause) and r5 \"until\" from n14 to c4 (is clear). Messy but plausible-ish.\n\nBut \"next\" as dependent is a clear L3 per the settled case. Let me go with L3, quote 'next est block 1' — but wait, must quote words of the message in single quotes; the che