    "text": "n=13: \"from now on, ahve the boy in the picture stay a bit above the horizon line\"\nCircuit: r1 \"from\" from c1 to n1 (now); c1 declarative modifier \"on\", discourse \"ahve\", verb \"stay\", subject n2 (boy), complement n4 (a bit). r2 \"in\" from n2 to n3. r3 \"above\" from n4 to n5.\n\n\"ahve\" = misspelt \"have\". \"ahve the boy ... stay\" is a command. Circuit: c1 declarative with verb \"stay\" and \"ahve\" as discourse. Hmm! Is that wrong? \"have X stay\" — per settled case \"Have the legend switched on.\": a message that opens with \"have\", a nominal and a participle, no question mark, is a command; \"Have\" is verb of imperative clause, \"the legend\" its complement, participle verb of nonfinite clause. Here \"ahve\" is misspelt \"have\". Rule says misspelt word held where it stands when words don't settle what it is. Hmm, but \"ahve\" ≈ \"have\". The circuit treats \"ahve\" as a discourse word (L2? it's a misspelt verb held in discourse → L5: \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier, a dependent, a head or a discourse word, and no clause has it as `verb`\").\n\nIs \"ahve ... stay\" a clause with \"have\"? The sentence \"have the boy in the picture stay a bit above the horizon line\" is a command with \"have\" as verb and \"stay\" as nonfinite complement clause. The circuit makes \"stay\" the declarative verb of c1 and \"ahve\" discourse — losing the command and the \"have\". Hmm, but is \"ahve\" clearly \"have\"? It's a typo, and the settled case explicitly deals with \"Have the legend switched on.\" as imperative. Also \"ahve\" being a misspelt word held where it stands is a pass (\"A misspelt or unknown word, held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\"). But here the words do settle: it's the first word and the structure \"X the boy ... stay\" is exactly the \"have\" pattern.\n\nHmm, this is a judgment call. Also form: c1 written declarative but message is a command → L1 (\"Its form is wrong: a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\"). Hmm, is the message a clear command? \"from now on, ahve the boy in the picture stay a bit above the horizon line\" — yes, it's a command (have ... stay). Written as declarative.\n\nWait, careful: could \"the boy ... stay\" be declarative? \"have the boy stay\" is imperative. Yes, a command. So L1: form wrong (declarative for a command), or L2/L5 for \"ahve\" in discourse.\n\nGiven L1 is the first applicable grade, and form wrong: the clause c1's form is declarative, but the message is a command. Hmm, but if \"ahve\" is a separate verb clause, then then c1's verb \"stay\" is inside a nonfinite clause, and the whole thing is imperative. The circuit's c1 has verb \"stay\" and form declarative — that states the message declares the boy stays. That's wrong.\n\nI'll grade L1 for n=13, quoting 'ahve the boy in the picture stay'.\n\nHmm, alternatively L5: \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as ... a discourse word, and no clause has it as verb\" — \"ahve\" is discourse, no clause has it as verb → exactly L5. But L1 applies first if the form is wrong... The rule says give \"the first of L1 to L6 that applies\". So L1 if applicable. Is L1 applicable? \"Its `form` is wrong: a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\". This is about questions. For a command... L1 also says \"a clause with a verb written as `fragment`\". Hmm, the L1 form clause is specifically about questions and fragments. A command written as declarative — not explicitly covered. So maybe the better grade is L5 (\"ahve\" as discourse, no clause has \"have\" as verb). Or L2 (a word with content held in discourse — \"ahve\" has content, its place lost).\n\nHmm. L2: \"A word with content is held in `discourse`, so its place in the sentence is lost.\" \"ahve\" is a word with content (a verb) held in discourse → L2 applies and comes before L5. So grade L2 for n=13? Hmm, L2's example is a word with content like \"Thanks\" content. For a verb held in discourse... L5 is specifically about a verb held as a discourse word and no clause has it. Hmm, but L2 comes first numerically. The table is ordered L1..L6, \"the first ... that applies\".\n\nBoth apply: L2 (word with content in discourse) and L5 (a clause not written, verb held in discourse). The rule says take the first that applies → L2.\n\nHmm, risky either way. The note needs quote + what's wrong. I'll pick L2 with note: \"'ahve the boy in the picture stay' is a command with 'ahve' for 'have' as its verb, but 'ahve' stands in `discourse` and no clause has 'have' as its verb\". That names both.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe I should reconsider whether \"ahve\" should be treated as an unknown word held where it stands, which passes. If a reasonable formalizer treats \"ahve\" as gibberish... but the words do settle to \"have\" given the pattern. And the settled case about \"Have the legend switched on.\" strongly implies the imperative reading. Note also \"from now on\" — circuit: r1 \"from\" from c1 to n1 (now), \"on\" as modifier of c1. The fixed phrase \"from now on\" may be held whole as a modifier. Splitting it into a relation + modifier — the \"from\" relation from c1 to \"now\" while \"on\" as modifier: is that wrong? \"from now on\" as a fixed phrase of time may be a modifier. Splitting is not required to fail; a relation marked \"from\" from c1 to \"now\" plus \"on\" modifier — hmm, \"on\" as a modifier of the clause is weird but the case says fixed phrase may be held whole as modifier; holding differently isn't explicitly forbidden. But there's a bigger issue: \"from\" is a marker and \"on\" a trapped word. Eh, minor.\n\nI'll reject n=13 for the \"ahve\" verb matter.\n\nn=14: \"I'd like a black bird up above the house there\"\nc1 declarative auxiliary \"'d\" verb \"like\" modifier \"up\" subject n1 complement n2; r1 \"above\" from n2 to n3; n3 = \"the house there\" with \"there\" dependent.\n\n\"there\" as dependent of \"house\" — settled case: \"A word of place after a nominal ('near the lake there', 'the one above') may be a dependent of that nominal or a modifier of the clause\". Good → L8.\n\nn=15: \"I also need you to set Reject left of Archive so that someone reading from left to right reaches Reject last\"\nc1 declarative verb need subject I, modifier also. r1 \"to\" from c1 to c2 (nonfinite set). c2 nonfinite verb \"set\", modifier \"left\", subject n2 (you), complement n3 (Reject). r2 \"of\" from c2 to n4 (Archive). Hmm, \"left of Archive\": per settled case, \"left of\" may be one marker, or \"left\" a nominal/modifier with relation \"of\" starting at the clause or at \"left\". Here \"of\" from c2 to Archive — starting at the clause. Passes per the case (\"A relation marked 'of' that starts at the thing placed ('a gazelle of the hill') fails\"). Here starts at clause c2. Good.\nr3 \"so that\" from c2 to c4. c4 declarative verb \"reaches\", modifier \"last\", subject n5 (someone), complement n8 (Reject). c3 nonfinite \"reading\" tied to n5 via r6. r4 \"from\" from c3 to n6 (left); r5 \"to\" from c3 to n7 (right) ambiguous. \n\"someone reading from left to right reaches Reject last\" — good. \"last\" modifier. Fine. Form declarative for c4 (subordinate clause) — correct per settled case (\"so that ...\" clause states). Accept L8.\n\nWait: is c2's subject \"you\" - \"need you to set\" - yes. And r1 from c1 to c2 with marker \"to\" (infinitive). Good.\n\nAny issue: \"left\" as modifier of c2 — \"set Reject left of Archive\"; ok per case. L8.\n\nn=16: \"the dog on the path in front of the hill: face left\"\nc1 fragment complement n1 (the dog on the path in front of the hill). r1 \"on\" from n1 to n2; r2 \"in\" from n2 to n3 (front); r3 \"of\" from n3 to n4 (hill). n5 = \"face left\" (head face, dep left); r4 from n1 to n5.\n\nHmm: \"face left\" is a command! \"face left\" — \"face\" as a verb, imperative. The circuit holds n5 as a nominal with head \"face\" and dependent \"left\", tied to n1 by relation. That's a command lost: settled case \"A command needs its verb. When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ('book design review', 'schedule the kickoff'), it is the verb; held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1.\"\n\nHere \"the dog on the path in front of the hill: face left\" — two parts: a nominal (set the scene) and a command \"face left\". The command's verb is \"face\", decided by \"no other verb\" in that part. Held as a nominal head → L1. Quote 'face left'.\n\nAlternatively L5. L1 is specific: \"held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1\". Good → L1.\n\nn=17: \"Describe how order value moved over the quarter\"\nc1 imperative verb Describe; c2 declarative verb moved subject n1 (order value), modifier how; r1 \"over\" from c2 to n2. r2 from c1 to c2. Settled case: clause opening with question word inside another clause → declarative, tied. Good. L8.\n\nHmm, is \"over the quarter\" a relation from c2 — fine.\n\nn=18: \"sidee the green block one space left and a quarter space up but never touching the blue block\"\nCircuit: n1 \"sidee\" alone as nominal head; n2 the green block; n3 \"one space\" with dep \"left\" (head space, deps one, left); k1 coordination n3, n4 (a quarter space); c1 nonfinite \"touching\", negation never, modifier up, complement n5 (the blue block); k2 coordination \"but\" conjunct c2, c1; c2 fragment complement n1; r1 n1→n2; r2 n2→k1.\n\nIssues:\n1. \"sidee\" is a misspelt verb (probably \"slide\")? \"sidee the green block one space left\" — command. Unknown word held where it stands... \"sidee\" as a nominal head — hmm, a misspelt word \"held where it stands\" — the rule for misspelt words: \"A misspelt or unknown word, held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is.\" Here, \"sidee the green block one space left\" strongly suggests \"slide\". The settled case: \"A command needs its verb. When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ... it is the verb; held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1.\" But here the first word is misspelt, not a noun-or-verb word. Hmm. The misspelt-word rule says held where it stands passes when the words don't settle what it is.\n\nHmm. \"sidee\" — could be \"side\" + typo? \"side the green block\"? Hmm, in these diagram-instruction datasets, \"slide\" is a common verb; \"sidee\" looks like \"slide\" with letters swapped. Hmm.\n\n2. n3 \"one space left\" with \"left\" as dependent of \"space\" — per the settled case: \"a word after a measure ('two spaces left') is a modifier of the clause... Held as a dependent of the nominal, the word becomes part of the description: L3.\" Here \"left\" is a dependent of \"space\" (n3, head space, deps one, left). That's exactly the L3 case! \"one space left\" → \"left\" should be a modifier of the clause; held as dependent of \"space\" → L3.\n\nHmm wait, but here n3's head is \"space\" with deps \"one\" and \"left\". Yes: \"one space left\" — \"left\" as dependent of \"space\". Per the settled case \"move block 6 two spaces left\" where \"left\" is a modifier of the clause, and \"Held as a dependent of the nominal, the word becomes part of the description by which the thing is found: L3.\"\n\nBut hold on: n3 is part of a coordination of measure-ish nominals; also n3's \"left\" — the clause would be c2 (fragment). Hmm, L3 applies. I'll reject n=18 as L3 quoting 'one space left'.\n\nActually, careful — also \"the green block one space left\" — n2 tied r2 to k1 (the coordination of measures). So the \"left\" is inside the measure nominal. Yes, L3.\n\nn=19: long one. \"half the stand-up was grumbling that the numbers were impossible to read from the corridor screen; to settle it, go for marker spacing wider on the order scatter — while you're at it, have the error bars magenta going forward and then show both grid lines\"\n\nc1 declarative modifier \"half\" auxiliary \"was\" verb \"grumbling\" subject n1 (the stand-up). Hmm: \"half the stand-up\" — \"half\" is a quantity word before the nominal \"the stand-up\"; per the settled case, quantity phrase may be dependent of the noun (\"half the stand-up\" like \"all of the cushions\"). Here \"half\" held as a modifier of c1. Is that a wrong statement? \"half\" as modifier of the clause = \"half grumbling\", degree. Hmm, the case list is for \"a pair of trees\"/\"any of my meetings\" style. Not directly. \"half\" as modifier of the clause... The message \"half the stand-up was grumbling\" = half of the stand-up (people) were grumbling; here subject is \"the stand-up\" with \"was\" (singular - no wait, \"was grumbling\" with subject \"half the stand-up\"). Modifier \"half\" of the clause — a next layer could read \"half\" as a degree. Not clearly a wrong statement... it's \"what a modifier says\" which is not judged. And is \"half\" a word with content in the wrong wire? L3: \"Words stand in the wrong wire\". Hmm. The words \"half the stand-up\" - if \"half\" is a modifier of the clause, then the subject is \"the stand-up\" — which loses \"half\". Hmm. But the settled case for grading words (\"a bit longer\", \"way shorter\") says grading words are modifier of the clause, and quantity phrase before noun may be dependent. So \"half\" as modifier of the clause: plausible-ish. Not clearly wrong. I'd accept on that point.\n\nc2 declarative verb \"were\", modifier \"impossible\", subject n2 (the numbers). r1 \"that\" from c1 to c2. r2 \"to\" from c2 to c3 (nonfinite read). r3 \"from\" from c3 to n3 (corridor screen). Good.\n\nc4 nonfinite \"settle\" complement n4 (it); r4 \"to\" from c5 to c4. c5 imperative \"go\"; r5 \"for\" from c5 to n5 (marker spacing wider — with \"wider\" as dependent of n5!). Again: \"marker spacing wider\" — \"wider\" is a word after a nominal saying how the thing should be → modifier of the clause; held as dependent → L3 per the settled case. Hmm! Same as \"a size larger\" case and \"font bigger\". Yes: \"go for marker spacing wider on the order scatter\" — \"wider\" as dependent of \"spacing\" is exactly the case: \"a word after a nominal that is a complement of its clause and says how the thing should be is a modifier of the clause\". Held as dependent → L3.\n\nHmm, but \"spacing\" is not a complement of the clause; it's the object of the relation \"for\". The settled case examples: \"font bigger\", \"bar gap small\", \"line thinner\", \"pie radius smaller\" — these are fragments with nominals as complements. And \"move block 6 two spaces left\", \"the dog a size bigger\". In \"go for error bar caps a size larger on the sales chart\" (n=10), \"larger\" was dependent of \"caps\" → I judged L3. Similarly here \"wider\" is a dependent of \"spacing\" → L3.\n\nSo n=19 → L3, quote 'marker spacing wider'.\n\nLet me double-check n=10's case: \"a word after a measure ... So is the word after a measure: 'move block 6 two spaces left', 'the dog a size bigger', with 'two spaces' and 'a size' nominals of their own. Held as a `dependent` of the nominal, the word becomes part of the description by which the thing is found: L3.\" In n=10 the nominal is \"error bar caps a size larger\" with \"a\",\"size\",\"larger\" as deps. So \"larger\" dependent → L3. Yes.\n\nFor n=19, \"marker spacing wider\": \"wider\" after nominal \"spacing\" → should be a modifier of the clause. Held as dep → L3. Yes.\n\nn=20: \"the top right corner needs to stay clear, just the way the drawing looks\"\nc1 declarative verb needs subject n1; r1 \"to\" from c1 to c2; c2 nonfinite \"stay\" modifier \"clear\" complement n2 (\"just the way\"); n3 the drawing; c3 declarative \"looks\" subject n3; r2 from n2 to c3.\n\nHmm: \"stay clear\" — \"clear\" as modifier of c2. Good. \"just the way the drawing looks\" — n2 nominal \"just the way\" (head way, deps just, the) with r2 to c3. So \"the way the drawing looks\" treated as a nominal with a clause tied. Plausible: \"the way\" is a noun meaning manner; \"just\" dependent. Acceptable? Alternatively \"just the way\" could be a marker. Hmm. The next layer can continue: \"stay clear just the way [the drawing looks]\". Acceptable → L8. No verb lost, no wrong statement. I'd accept.\n\nn=21: \"for the order system, keep the associations labelled 1..*, then add a multiplicity note to the customer end, with enough space between the parts\"\nc1 imperative keep complement n2 (the associations); c2 nonfinite \"labelled\" complement n3 (\"1\"); n4 \"*\" tied via r6 to n2, r5 n2→c2.\nk1 coordination \"then\" c1, c3. c3 imperative add complement n5; r2 \"to\" c3→n6; r3 \"with\" c3→n7 (enough space); r4 \"between\" n7→n8 (the parts). r1 \"for\" c1→n1.\n\n\"1..*\" — words \"1\" and \"*\" (the program split punctuation: \"..\" is punctuation, so words are \"1\" and \"*\"). n3 = \"1\", n4 = \"*\". Fine — \"1\" as complement of \"labelled\", \"*\" tied to n2 (associations). Plausible reading of \"labelled 1..*\". Acceptable? The label \"1..*\" split into two wires, both tied... n4 tied to n2 via r6. Hmm, \"1..*\" is a multiplicity string; splitting is not ideal but words are all tied. Could be L6? No, \"*\" is tied. Accept L8.\n\nHmm, is \"labelled\" a nonfinite clause tied to n2 — fine per case (participle describing).\n\nL8.\n\nn=22: \"please est the Shell block into the upper right corner, three and a half widths in from the right and two lengths down from the top, next keep every other block as it is\"\nc1 imperative, verb \"keep\", discourse please, modifier \"est\", modifier \"in\", discourse \"and\", modifier \"down\", modifier \"next\", complements n1 (Shell block), n3 (three and a half widths), n5 (two lengths), n7 (every other block). r1 \"into\" from c1 to n2; r2 \"from\" from c1 to n4 (the right); r3 \"from\" from c1 to n6 (the top); r4 \"as\" from c1 to c2 (\"it is\").\n\nBig problem: \"est\" is the verb (misspelling of \"move\"? or truncated) — \"please est the Shell block into the upper right corner\" — the first/main verb. Circuit holds \"est\" as a modifier and makes \"keep\" the verb of the whole thing. Two separate instructions: \"est the Shell block into ... , three and a half widths in from the right and two lengths down from the top, next keep every other block as it is\". So the \"est\" instruction is lost: L1 (\"A command needs its verb. When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ... it is the verb\") — hmm, \"est\" isn't a noun/verb word. But L5: \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier ... and no clause has it as verb.\" That's exactly: \"est\" held as modifier, no clause has it as verb → L5. \n\nAlso \"and\" in \"three and a half widths\" held as discourse — L2? \"and\" is a conjunction, joining numeral words; held as discourse. Hmm, the settled case: \"So is a conjunction between two modifier words of one clause ('flat and dry'): the schema has no wire for that pair.\" That allows discourse for conjunctions between modifiers. Here n3 is a nominal \"three and a half widths\" with \"and\" as dependent — wait: n3 syntax.nominal dependent=\"three\" dependent=\"and\" dependent=\"a\" dependent=\"half\" head=\"widths\". So \"and\" as a dependent of the nominal, AND also c1 has discourse=\"and\". Wait both? Let me re-read:\n\n@n3 syntax.nominal dependent=\"three\" dependent=\"and\" dependent=\"a\" dependent=\"half\" head=\"widths\"\n@c1 syntax.clause ... discourse=\"and\" ...\n\nSo \"and\" appears twice! That means a word stands in two values. The RULES say \"A program has already checked that every word of the message stands in exactly one value.\" Presumably the check counts string occurrences: the message has \"and\" twice (\"widths in from the right and two lengths\"). Yes! Text has \"and\" twice. So one \"and\" as dependent of n3, one as discourse in c1. Hmm, the second \"and\" joining \"three and a half widths in from the right\" with \"two lengths down from the top\" — held as discourse of c1. That's a conjunction between two nominals (measures), not modifiers. The case for \"flat and dry\" covers conjunctions between modifier words; here it's between measure nominals n3 and n5, both complements of c1. Holding it as discourse loses the pairing? Hmm, the two measures are both complements of c1, tied by the relation to the corner... Actually what do the measures measure? \"three and a half widths in from the right and two lengths down from the top\" — two offsets. The circuit: r2 \"from\" c1→\"the right\", r3 \"from\" c1→\"the top\", complements n3, n5 in c1. So both measures are complements of the clause, not tied specifically. Acceptable-ish.\n\nMain problem is \"est\" as modifier → L5. Also form: c1 imperative, but it also holds \"keep\"'s complement \"every other block\" — merging two instructions: \"est the Shell block into the corner ... next keep every other block as it is\". Merging them into one clause loses the second clause: \"keep\" is the verb of a separate instruction introduced by \"next\". So one clause for two instructions → L1? \"Words of one written clause are held in another\". Hmm.\n\nBut \"est\" being a modifier (a verb held as modifier) → L5, and the \"next\" held as modifier of the same clause. I'd say L5 with quote 'est the Shell block into the upper right corner' — the instruction with \"est\" as its verb is not written.\n\nCareful: is \"est\" possibly not a verb? \"please est the Shell block into the upper right corner\" — clearly a verb (typo of \"move\"? Actually in these message sets, maybe \"est\" = \"set\"? Hmm \"est\" could be \"set\" misspelled... \"set the Shell block into the upper right corner\"). Either way it's the verb. The rule about misspelt words: \"held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\" — but here the words do settle it's the verb (imperative opening, followed by nominal and place phrase). Hmm, does the misspelt rule protect the formalizer? The settled case about commands: \"When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ('book design review', 'schedule the kickoff'), it is the verb; held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1.\" \"est\" can't be a noun... hmm. But intent is clear.\n\nAlso compare n=12 \"est block 1 down to the bottom\" — there \"est\" is also a verb (truncated \"Move\"/\"Place\"?). Hmm! n=12's circuit holds \"est\" as a dependent of \"block\"! Wait: n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"est\" head=\"block\". So \"est\" is a dependent of \"block\" and no clause has \"est\" as verb → L5 as well!\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider n=12. Same truncated verb \"est\". In n=12, c1 is a fragment with modifier down/right, complement n1 (\"est block\"). So \"est\" is held as dependent → the command verb lost → L5. That is a stronger, cleaner rejection than the \"next\" L3. Both are possible; which grade? L3 for \"next\" as dependent (wrong statement about a word of order) and L5 for \"est\" not a verb. L3 comes first numerically. The instruction says give \"the first of L1 to L6 that applies\". Hmm — for n=12, both L3 and L5 apply. First = L3.\n\nHmm, but is L5 right for \"est\"? Is \"est\" really a verb there? \"est block 1 down to the bottom\" — with \"est\" as part of the nominal \"est block 1\"? Suppose the message is truncated from \"Move to est block\"? Unclear. Hmm, \"est\" could be an abbreviation. This is a synthetic/truncated message. Given uncertainty, the misspelt/unknown word rule (\"held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\") might make \"est\" acceptable. In n=12, the four parallel instructions \"est block 1 down/right/up/left\" — the word precedes a nominal and direction; that's verb position. Hmm.\n\nFor n=12 I'll reject on \"next\" (L3), which is clean per the settled case. For n=22, \"est\" is a modifier, plus \"keep\" merges two instructions. In n=22, is there another basis? Let's see: \"please est the Shell block into the upper right corner, ... , next keep every other block as it is\" — the circuit merges the two into one clause with verb \"keep\" and treats the first instruction's verb as a modifier. That IS \"Words of one written clause are held in another\"? No, rather a clause's verb held as a modifier → L5. I'll reject n=22 with L5, quote 'est the Shell block'.\n\nn=23: \"I keep squinting at Revenue trend every time I paste it into the weekly newsletter; this week, make the line medium width as well, would you. and then make the markers a bit bigger\"\nCircuit: c1 declarative keep subject I; c2 nonfinite squinting complement n3 (\"every time\"); r1 \"at\" c2→n2 (Revenue trend); n4 I; c3 declarative paste subject n4 complement n5 (it); r2 \"into\" c3→n6; n7 \"this week\"; c4 imperative make modifier as, modifier well, complements n7 (this week), n8 (\"the line medium width\" — head width, deps the, line, medium!); c5 interrogative \"would\" subject you; k1 coordination \"and\" conjunct c1, c4, c6; c6 imperative \"make\" modifier then, modifier bigger, complements n10 (the markers), n11 (a bit); r3 c1→c2; r4 n3→c3; r5 c4→c5.\n\nIssues:\n1. n8: \"the line medium width\" with head \"width\", deps \"the\",\"line\",\"medium\". Should be: \"make the line medium width\" — the line is the complement, \"medium width\" the value. Hmm, splitting \"the line\" and \"medium width\" into one nominal with head \"width\" merges two nominals → L3 (\"two nominals are merged\"). Hmm, but per the case about \"make the box red\"? \"medium width\" is like a modifier/adjective phrase (\"medium width\" = a width value). \"make the line medium width\": n8 = \"the line medium width\", head \"width\". Reading: \"the line medium width\" as a single nominal with \"line\" and \"medium\" as dependents — head \"width\" wrong? The head should be \"line\". This is a merge of the thing (\"the line\") and its value (\"medium width\"). Hmm.\n\nAlso, note the parallel in the message: \"make the markers a bit bigger\" (c6) which the circuit splits as n10 (markers) + n11 (a bit) with \"bigger\" as modifier. So c4 handling is inconsistent.\n\nIs n8 a wrong nominal? \"make the line medium width as well\" — \"the line\" is the object; \"medium width\" says how it should be. Per the settled case: \"a word after a nominal that is a complement of its clause and says how the thing should be is a modifier of the clause\" — that's for a word, but \"medium width\" is two words (\"medium\" grading \"width\", a nominal). Hmm. In the rules example \"pie radius smaller\", \"font bigger\". Here \"make the line medium width\" — \"medium width\" is arguably a nominal of its own (a measure) or a modifier phrase. The circuit merges \"the line\" with \"medium width\" into one nominal, head \"width\". That states the thing acted on is \"the line medium width\" — i.e., the thing is a width. That's a merge of two nominals → L3.\n\nHmm, is that a clear call? \"the line medium width\" as a nominal — the head \"width\" with dependent \"line\" and \"medium\" — that's like \"the line's medium width\". So \"make [the line's medium width]\" — the action applied to \"the width of the line\". Plausibly the same thing? \"make the line medium width\" ≈ \"make the width of the line medium\". Hmm, but then \"medium\" is the modifier of the clause in the correct reading. Honestly, merging \"the line\" (a thing) into the nominal of the value \"width\" is a merger of two nominals: L3.\n\n2. Also \"as well\" as modifiers of c4 — fine (fixed phrase \"as well\" may be modifier).\n3. \"this week\" as a complement of c4 — word of time set off by comma belongs to the clause; a relation from the clause with no marker passes; but here it's a complement. Hmm, \"this week\" as complement of \"make\" — that states the thing acted on includes \"this week\". Eh, complements = \"the nominals the verb takes, whatever they are to it\". Hmm, it's like holding a time phrase as a complement — arguably acceptable (\"whatever they are to it\"). Not judged what a complement is to the verb. OK.\n4. c5 interrogative \"would\" subject you — elliptical tag \"would you\" may be a clause with the auxiliary as verb. Good.\n5. k1 joins c1, c4, c6 with \"and\" — the \"and\" is at the start of the last sentence: \"and then make the markers a bit bigger\". Per settled case, \"and\" at start of a later sentence may be discourse or coordination joining the clauses of the two sentences. Here it joins c1 (I keep squinting), c4 (make the line), c6 (make the markers). Hmm, joining c1 with c4/c6 across the semicolon and \"would you\" — allowed (\"the clauses of the two sentences\"). It's a coordination of 3 clauses; c4/c6 are imperatives and c1 declarative. Settled case says a coordination joining an imperative with a clause inside a relation has a wrong conjunct — not this pattern. But there's a \"would you\" clause c5 between c4 and c6 (tied to c4 by r5). The \"and\" coordination joins c4 and c6 with c5 in between tied to c4 — analogous to the \"clause between two conjuncts that belongs to none of them\" case: here c5 belongs to c4 via r5, so fine.\n\nSo the main flaw: n8 merging \"the line\" + \"medium width\" with head \"width\" → L3.\n\nHmm, is head \"width\" wrong? Rule: \"A nominal whose `head` is the graded word ('a bit longer' with head 'longer') or the lone grading word ('way') fails: L3.\" Here head is \"width\" and \"medium\" grades it — \"medium width\" head being \"width\" is fine (like \"a bit longer\" with head \"longer\" fails because \"longer\" is graded). Hmm, \"medium width\": \"medium\" is the grading word, \"width\" the head. That's proper for a nominal \"medium width\". The issue is \"the line\" is inside that nominal. So L3: two nominals merged / head wrong.\n\nActually maybe better: \"The `head` is wrong\" — the thing made is \"the line\"; the circuit's nominal head is \"width\". Quotes: 'the line medium width' — that's in the message. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait — could the intended nominal be n8 = \"the line\" with... no, it's as written.\n\nI'll reject n=23 as L3.\n\nBut hold on, maybe I should reconsider: is it possible that \"medium width\" here is meant as \"the line\" having property, and the formalizer's fusing is exactly the L3 \"two nominals merged\"? Yes I'm fairly confident.\n\nn=24: \"name the Sales by month chart Q1 revenue\"\nc1 imperative \"name\" complements n1, n3; n1 = \"the Sales chart\" (deps the, Sales, head chart) with r1 \"by\" attachment ambiguous from c1 to n2 (month); n3 = \"Q1 revenue\" (dep Q1, head revenue).\n\nHmm: \"the Sales by month chart\" — \"by month\" modifies \"chart\" (the chart named \"Sales by month\"). The relation r1 \"by\" starts from c1 (the clause) with attachment=ambiguous. Rule: \"The start of a relation with a marker: the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows, with or without attachment=ambiguous.\" But here \"by month\" — attachment ambiguous means the formalizer didn't settle whether it attaches to the verb or a nominal. Fine → passes.\n\nBut is that a wrong statement? \"by\" from c1 to n2 with ambiguous attachment — allowed. Accept L8.\n\nHmm, however: \"Sales by month chart\" — the whole is the chart's name. Hmm, name of several words may be whole in head or last word head. \"the Sales by month chart\" with \"Sales\" as dependent and \"chart\" as head, and \"by month\" a relation. Acceptable.\n\nL8.\n\nLet me continue reading.",
