    "text": "n=25: \"no, not that, the branches growing right\"\nCircuit: n1 \"not that\" (dep not, head that); c2 fragment complement n1; c1 nonfinite verb \"growing\" modifier right subject n2 (the branches); k1 coordination c1, c2 (no conjunction).\n\nWords: no, not, that, the, branches, growing, right — all placed. Form: c1 nonfinite \"growing\" — the message's last part \"the branches growing right\" is a fragment with participle. Hmm, \"growing\" as verb of nonfinite clause with subject \"the branches\". OK.\n\nIs a coordination between \"no\" fragment (\"not that\") and the participle clause fine? Hmm. The \"no\" is discourse of c1. c2 is a fragment \"not that\". A coordination joining them — plausible; the message is corrections. Accept? I think L8, garbled/elliptical message, no wrong statement evident.\n\nn=26: \"point the big bear in the woods toward the pond, and a huge bear in the woods facing left — with everything else left where it is\"\nc1 imperative point complement n1; r1 in → n2; r2 toward → n3 ambiguous; k1 coordination \"and\" c1, c5; n4 a huge bear; r3 in → n5; c2 nonfinite facing left; r4 \"with\" from n4 to c3; n6 everything else; c3 nonfinite verb \"left\" subject n6; r5 \"where\" from c3 to c4; n7 it; c4 declarative is subject it; c5 fragment complement n4 ellipsis=c1; r6 n4→c2.\n\n\"and a huge bear in the woods facing left\" — the second instruction: \"point ... and [point] a huge bear in the woods facing left\"? with ellipsis of \"point\" — c5 fragment with ellipsis=c1. Good.\n\n\"with everything else left where it is\" — r4 \"with\" from n4 (a huge bear) to c3 (\"left where it is\"). Hmm, \"with everything else left where it is\": the with-phrase's object is \"everything else\" (subject of nonfinite \"left\"). Per rules, a \"with\" relation typically from the clause. Here r4 from n4 to c3 means \"a huge bear ... with everything else left where it is\" — attaching the with-phrase to the bear nominal. Hmm, that's a start at a nominal of the same clause (n4 is complement of c5 which is coordinated with c1). Hmm, is that \"a start the words rule out, such as a nominal of another clause\"? n4 is in c5 (the fragment with ellipsis). The with-phrase belongs to the whole instruction. Attachment: \"The start of a relation with a marker: the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\". n4 is a nominal within the message's second part. Hmm. Could a \"with ...\" phrase attach to \"a huge bear in the woods facing left\"? Attaching \"with everything else left as it is\" to the bear nominal would mean the bear has everything else left where it is — semantically weird, but semantics of attachment is grammar. Actually the with-phrase is a scene-wide note. Hmm.\n\nAlso is the \"with\" relation going from n4 to c3 with c3 being the nonfinite \"left\" clause whose subject is \"everything else\" — but \"everything else\" n6 is the subject of c3, and the with marker relates n4 to c3. Hmm, \"with\" should relate the clause to \"everything else\"? Per the rules, prepositions start at a clause/nominal and end at a nominal. The end is c3 (a clause), not a nominal. Rule 6: \"The end of every relation, its marker, and its start when the words settle it\" must be right. A relation ending at a clause: the settled case allows relations ending at clauses (\"so it sits under the table\", \"to\" of infinitive ending at nonfinite clause, \"as thick as it gets\" ending at clause c2, \"as\" to c3). So it's allowed when the marker takes a clause.\n\nHere \"with everything else left where it is\" — with + nominal + participle. Best: relation \"with\" from the clause to n6 (everything else), and n6 subject of c3. Instead: r4 from n4 to c3, with n6 as subject of c3. Since c3 has subject n6, one can read \"with [everything else left where it is]\" as a nonfinite small clause. The relation from n4 to c3 with marker \"with\" — the start being n4 (the bear) instead of the clause c5/c1. Is that \"a start the words rule out\"? Hmm, attaching \"with...\" to \"a huge bear in the woods facing left\" — grammar allows a with-adjunct on a nominal in English (\"a bear with everything else left where it is\" — as a reduced relative? possible but odd). Hmm.\n\nAlso: is there an issue that \"everything else\" being the subject of c3 means c3 is a nonfinite clause \"left\" with subject. That's acceptable per rules (\"a participle tied to its clause\").\n\nHmm, r4 start from n4 — that's a nominal of the same written clause (c5 which shares the verb of c1). I'd say this is within \"any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\" tolerance. Accept L8.\n\nHmm, but the with-phrase in the message comes after a dash, applying to the whole instruction (\"with everything else left where it is\" = leave everything else). Attaching to n4 is a stretch but not clearly ruled out. Judge fairly → L8.\n\nHmm, one more: c2 nonfinite \"facing\" with n4 tied via r6 (n4→c2) — good, the bear facing left.\n\nL8.\n\nn=27: \"show me a bar chart of the wines by grape, biggest first\"\nc1 imperative show complements n1 (me), n2 (a bar chart); r1 \"of\" n2→n3 (the wines); r2 \"by\" n3→n4 (grape); n5 = \"biggest first\"! head \"first\", dep \"biggest\"; r3 from n3 to n5.\n\nHmm, \"biggest first\" — word of order \"first\" held as head with \"biggest\" as dependent. Rule: \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun from where it stands ('orders by week first' ...): it is a modifier of the clause; held as a dependent, it fails: L3.\" Here \"first\" is a HEAD, not dependent — worse. And \"biggest first\" — hmm, also the rule \"A nominal whose `head` is the graded word or the lone grading word fails: L3\". \"first\" as head with \"biggest\" as dependent: \"biggest\" is a grading word / superlative describing the ordering. The correct handling: \"biggest\" and \"first\" are modifiers of the clause (the chart ordered biggest first). Held as a nominal \"biggest first\" tied to \"the wines\" — the order word describes the noun → L3.\n\nQuote: 'biggest first' — the note: \"'first' is a word of order and cannot describe 'the wines' from where it stands; held as a nominal attached to them, the ordering instruction is wrong: L3\". Yes, L3.\n\nn=28: \"add a pair of clouds on top of the cloud\"\nc1 imperative add complement n1; n1 \"a pair\" (a dep, pair head); r1 \"of\" n1→n2 (clouds); r2 \"on\" from c1 to n3 (top) ambiguous; r3 \"of\" n3→n4 (the cloud).\n\nPer settled case: quantity phrase before a noun (\"a pair of trees\") may be held as dependent words of that noun, or as a nominal of its own with relation \"of\" starting at it — both pass. Here n1 \"a pair\" as own nominal with r1 \"of\" to \"clouds\" — passes. r2 \"on\" from c1 to n3 (top) — \"on top of the cloud\": marker \"on\" ends at \"top\"; then \"of\" from n3 to n4. Fine. Accept L8.\n\nn=29: \"a little black cat curled up near the bench, then dress the scene up as first light all the way through, the entire scene, mind\"\nCircuit: n1 \"a little black cat\"; c1 nonfinite \"curled\" modifier up; r1 \"near\" from c1 to n2 (bench); k1 coordination \"then\" conjunct c3, c2, c4, c5; c2 imperative \"dress\" modifier up complement n3 (the scene); r2 \"as\" from c2 to n4; n4 = \"first light all the way through\" — deps first, head light, deps all, the, way, through! n5 = \"the entire scene\"; n6 = \"mind\"; c3 fragment complement n1; r3 n1→c1; c4 fragment complement n5; c5 fragment complement n6.\n\nHmm — \"all the way through\" as dependent words of \"light\"?? Per settled case: \"A fixed phrase of time or manner ('from now on', 'after that', 'for now', 'side by side', 'at once') may be held whole as a `modifier`.\" \"all the way through\" is such a phrase of manner; held as a modifier. Here it's held as dependent words of the nominal \"light\" (\"all\",\"the\",\"way\",\"through\" as dependents of \"light\"). That makes the light \"all the way through\" part of the description of the light — hmm. Rule for modifiers of order: \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun from where it stands ... held as a dependent, it fails: L3.\" \"all the way through\" is manner/time, describing the dressing (\"dress the scene up as first light all the way through\" = through the whole scene). Held as dependents of \"light\" — L3? Hmm.\n\nAlso n6 = \"mind\" — \"the entire scene, mind\" — \"mind\" is a word said to the listener (discourse: \"mind\" = a caution)! Held as nominal complement of a fragment c5 in a coordination. Hmm, \"mind\" here: \"..., the entire scene, mind\" — \"mind\" is like \"mind you\", a discourse word. Rule L2: \"A word with content is held in `discourse`, so its place in the sentence is lost.\" Here \"mind\" is in a nominal, which is the opposite direction: a discourse word held as a nominal → not L2. Is there a grade for that? L3 \"Words stand in the wrong wire\". Hmm, but is \"mind\" clearly discourse? \"the entire scene, mind\" = \"mind [you], the entire scene\". Yeah, \"mind\" as an attention word. Holding it as a nominal complement of a separate fragment clause with the coordination — that states there's a thing \"mind\"? Hmm, that's the wrong wire for a discourse word → L3? Hmm, rules don't explicitly cover discourse-word-in-nominal except L2 in reverse and L3 broadly.\n\nHmm, \"mind\" could also be a noun (\"mind\")? In this context \"..., the entire scene, mind\" — it's clearly the verbal \"mind\" = pay attention.\n\nBut careful with fairness: the biggest issue for me is the L3 dismissal of \"all the way through\" as dependents of \"light\". Let me evaluate: is that a clear L3? The settled case: \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun from where it stands ('orders by week first', 'beside the sun next', 'the shed walls too', 'now a second wooden boat'): it is a `modifier` of the clause; held as a `dependent`, it fails: L3.\"\n\n\"all the way through\" — words of extent. As dependents of \"light\", the nominal becomes \"first light all the way through\". The message means \"dress the scene up as first light, all the way through\" — through the whole scene. Hmm, actually \"all the way through\" modifies the light (first light all the way through?). Ambiguous. Hmm — \"dress the scene up as first light all the way through, the entire scene, mind\" — \"all the way through\" = throughout the scene, then clarified by \"the entire scene\". So it modifies the dressing event → should be a modifier of the clause.\n\nIts being held as a dependent of \"light\" makes it part of the description of the light. Per the case pattern, that fails: L3. I'd reject n=29 as L3 quoting 'all the way through'.\n\nHmm, but wait: could it be considered a \"fixed phrase of manner\" held whole as a modifier — the circuit didn't do that; it split it into deps. Alternatively, \"all the way through\" as deps is not obviously wrong... I'm somewhat unsure. The case-list is about a word of time or order after a nominal. Here the words come after the nominal \"first light\", i.e., after a nominal — same pattern. Rule says such words cannot describe a noun from where they stand; they are modifiers of the clause. So L3.\n\nAlternatively for n=29 \"mind\" in a nominal is a cleaner rejection? \"mind\" is a word said to the listener held as a nominal — a word with content... hmm, L2 is about the opposite direction. L3 (wrong wire) might fit but the rules' examples for discourse mismatches are L2 only.\n\nI'll use L3 with 'all the way through'.\n\nn=30: \"add a series with chart Sales by region and values 3, 5 and 8 and label Review\"\nc1 imperative add complement n1 (a series); r1 \"with\" from n1 to k1; n2 \"chart Sales\" (dep chart, head Sales!); r2 \"by\" n2→n3 (region); k1 coordination conjunction=\"and\" twice, conjunct n2, n4, n8; n4 \"values\", n5 \"3\", n6 \"5\", n7 \"8\"; k2 coordination \"and\" conjunct n5,n6,n7; n8 \"label Review\" (dep label, head Review); r3 n4→k2.\n\nWords: add, a, series, with, chart, Sales, by, region, and, values, 3, 5, and, 8, and, label, Review = \"and\" 3 times. k1 has conjunction=\"and\" twice (joining 3 conjuncts) and k2 has one. Total 3. OK.\n\nn2 head \"Sales\" with \"chart\" as dependent — \"chart Sales\": hmm, \"chart\" is a noun here (\"chart Sales\" — hmm, \"with chart Sales by region\" = the series has chart named \"Sales\"). Hmm, could be \"chart\" as head and \"Sales\" dependent (name). Per the case \"Two bare nouns side by side after a marker ('with id neck', 'type radar'): either may be the head.\" Good, either passes.\n\nn8 \"label Review\" — \"and label Review\": hmm, \"and label Review\" — is \"label\" here a verb?! \"add a series with chart Sales by region and values 3, 5 and 8 and label Review\" — \"and label Review\" = \"and label [it] Review\" — a second command with verb \"label\"! Hmm. Or \"label Review\" as a nominal (the label named \"Review\"). Which reading? \"with chart Sales by region and values 3,5,8 and label Review\" — the \"with\" list: chart..., values..., label... So \"label Review\" = the label is \"Review\"? Hmm. Or an additional command \"label [the series] Review\". Both readings plausible; grammar allows \"label\" as a noun or verb → passes per \"A word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb\". The circuit holds it as a nominal in the coordination with \"with\". Plausible. L8.\n\nWait, is \"chart Sales by region\" also possibly a verb \"chart\"? Similar. Fine, L8.\n\nn=31: \"Could you create a document titled Grandad 90 letter containing \"Everyone can read the new summary.\". I appreciate it.\"\nc1 interrogative auxiliary Could verb create subject you complement n2 (a document); c2 nonfinite \"titled\" complement n3 (\"Grandad 90 letter\"); c3 nonfinite \"containing\" complement n4 (\"Everyone can read the new summary\" as one nominal head, quoted content); c4 declarative appreciate subject I complement it; r1 n2→c2; r2 n2→c3.\n\nHmm: \"titled Grandad 90 letter containing \"...\" — is the title \"Grandad 90 letter\" and the document contains the sentence? The circuit: c2 \"titled\" complement n3 = \"Grandad 90 letter\" (head letter, deps Grandad, 90); c3 \"containing\" complement n4 = the quoted sentence. r1 and r2 both from n2. Good.\n\nHmm, n3 head \"letter\" with deps Grandad, 90 — \"Grandad 90 letter\" — a name of several words: \"Grandad\" then \"90\" then \"letter\". Head \"letter\" is the noun; \"Grandad 90\" a name. OK.\n\nThe quoted sentence held whole as a nominal head \"Everyone can read the new summary\" — the quoted content as one nominal. Is that acceptable? The RULES say a program checked that every word stands in one value; a whole sentence as a nominal head... Hmm, the settled case mentions content mentioned as content is a nominal or a clause tied to the word of saying. Here it's a nominal. Passes (\"Words said to the listener that are mentioned as content ... are that content: a nominal\").\n\n\"I appreciate it.\" c4 declarative with verb appreciate — per settled case, that's a clause, not discourse. Good. Is c4 tied to anything? c1 and c4 are separate sentences — \"Separate sentences need no tie.\" But there's no coordination — fine.\n\nL8.\n\nn=32: \"instead, orange blocks in place of the purple blocks, same shape as the old row\"\nc1 fragment modifier \"instead\" complement n1 (orange blocks); r1 \"in\" n1→n2 (place); r2 \"of\" n2→n3 (the purple blocks); n4 \"same shape\"; r3 \"as\" n4→n5 (the old row); r4 n1→n4.\n\nHmm \"same shape as the old row\" — n4 = \"same shape\" (dep same, head shape) tied to n1 via r4, and r3 \"as\" to the old row. Plausible. L8.\n\nIs \"instead\" a modifier — fine (fixed phrase? just a word of discourse-ish; \"instead\" held as modifier — fine, or discourse; either).\n\nL8.\n\nn=33: \"grow the Sort text a touch — it's for the back row\"\nc1 imperative grow complements n1 (the Sort text), n2 (a touch). n2 \"a touch\" as a nominal of its own = measure. Good per case (\"a bit\" may stand as a nominal). c2 declarative verb \"'s\" subject it; r2 \"for\" from c2 to n4; k1 coordination c1, c2.\n\nHmm, \"it's for the back row\" — \"it's\" = it is. c2 with verb \"'s\" declarative. Fine. r1? there's no relation for \"'s\"... it's a verb. OK.\n\nk1 coordination joining c1 and c2 with no conjunction word — \"grow the Sort text a touch — it's for the back row\". The dash joins. A coordination with no conjunction. Acceptable.\n\nn2 \"a touch\" as complement of grow (a measure) — fine.\n\nL8.\n\nn=34: \"I cannot find it, where is the Idea box?\"\nc1 declarative negation \"cannot\" verb find subject I complement it. Hmm: \"cannot\" contains \"can\" + \"not\"; the circuit writes negation=\"cannot\" and no auxiliary. Is that right? \"cannot\" as a single word - the negation word. Hmm: \"Its `auxiliary` and `negation` words.\" \"cannot\" is an auxiliary+negation fused. Writing it as negation with verb \"find\" — the auxiliary \"can\" is not written as auxiliary. Is that L1 (\"An `auxiliary` or `negation` word is held in another place\")? Hmm, \"cannot\" is held as negation. Does the message show an auxiliary \"can\"? The word is \"cannot\", one word. The rule doesn't require splitting a fused word... Actually \"can't\"/\"cannot\" — the schema might need auxiliary=\"can\" negation. Hmm. Since the message's word is \"cannot\", holding it as negation is a defensible single-word handling. But is the auxiliary lost? \"Secondary: a clause's auxiliary\". The message's \"cannot\" carries both. Hmm.\n\nRule L1: \"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here, or an auxiliary while the main verb stands elsewhere.\" Not that. \"An `auxiliary` or `negation` word is held in another place, or negates the wrong clause.\" \"cannot\" is held as negation of c1 — right clause, right place-ish.\n\nI think this is acceptable: \"cannot\" as negation word. L8? Hmm, but one could argue the auxiliary \"can\" should be an auxiliary. Given the message's word is fused \"cannot\", I'd accept. Actually hmm, let me think about how the grader (the human who wrote these rules) would judge. The rules' settled cases don't cover \"cannot\". I'll accept as L8 (judge fairly).\n\nc2 interrogative \"where is the Idea box?\" — modifier where, verb is, subject the Idea box. Good per case. k1 coordinates c1, c2 — joining two clauses of one message without conjunction (comma). Fine.\n\nL8.\n\nn=35: \"please layer the four blocks in the order blue, blue, green, red, and after that, stack two green blocks stacked on the green block, thanks\"\nc1 imperative layer complement n1 (the four blocks); r1 \"in\" c1→n2 (the order); n3 = \"blue blue green red\" (deps blue, blue, green; head red); r4 n2→n3; k1 coordination \"and\" c1, c2; r2 \"after\" c2→n4 (that); c2 imperative \"stack\" discourse \"thanks\" complement n5 (two green blocks); c3 nonfinite \"stacked\"; r3 \"on\" c3→n6 (the green block); r5 n5→c3.\n\nWords: please, layer, the, four, blocks, in, the, order, blue, blue, green, red, and, after, that, stack, two, green, blocks, stacked, on, the, green, block, thanks. Check the circuit: please(discourse c1), layer, the four blocks n1, in r1, the order n2, blue blue green red n3, and (k1), after r2, that n4, stack c2 verb, two green blocks n5, stacked c3, on r3, the green block n6, thanks (discourse of c2). All words placed.\n\nHmm: \"thanks\" as discourse of c2 — fine (the settled case allows \"Thanks\" as discourse). Though it's at the end of the message; which clause holds a discourse word is not judged. Good.\n\nn3: \"blue, blue, green, red\" as one nominal with head \"red\" and others as deps — merging four separate items into one nominal with head \"red\"? The order is a list of four colours: \"layer the four blocks in the order blue, blue, green, red\". Hmm. Merging them into one nominal where head is \"red\" — the items should be separate wires in a coordination, or deps? This is like \"the order blue, blue, green, red\". Is this a wrong head? Rule: \"The words of one nominal are split between wires, or two nominals are merged. The head is wrong.\"\n\nFour colour items (\"blue\",\"blue\",\"green\",\"red\") — each names a colour for a block; they're a list, not one thing described. Merging them into one nominal with head \"red\" says the order is \"red\" with modifiers. Hmm. Is that a wrong statement / lost information? The list order is preserved by word order... but a nominal \"blue blue green red\" with head \"red\" is a merger of four nominals. I think this is L3 (\"two nominals are merged\" / head wrong).\n\nHmm, but hold on — is that too strict? In these diagram datasets, \"layer the four blocks in the order blue, blue, green, red\" — hmm. The next layer needs to know the four colours in order. In a nominal with deps blue, blue, green and head red, the information is arguably present in order but structurally the head is \"red\" — and \"blue, blue, green\" as dependents of \"red\" makes them descriptions of the red (e.g., \"the light blue-green red\"?). I'd call that merged nominals → L3.\n\nHmm, but is there also a cleaner fault? c3 \"stacked\" is tied to n5 via r5. \"stack two green blocks stacked on the green block\" — a doubled \"stacked\" in the message; fine.\n\nI'm moderately inclined to reject n=35 with L3 for 'blue, blue, green, red'. Hmm. But maybe the grader would accept this as a list held loosely. Let me weigh: RULES explicitly says \"two nominals are merged\" is L3. Four separate colour words = four nominals (each a thing named). Merging into one with a wrong head. I'll reject.\n\nHmm, careful though: could \"blue, blue, green, red\" be one nominal legitimately? No — they're alternative colours, not one description.\n\nn=36: \"set aside the left edge — just like that in the scene — keeping the rest of the scene as it is\"\nc1 imperative set modifier aside modifier just complement n1 (the left edge); r1 \"like\" c1→n2 (that); r2 \"in\" n2→n3 (the scene); c2 nonfinite keeping complement n4 (the rest); r3 \"of\" n4→n5; r4 \"as\" c2→c3 (is); n6 it; c3 declarative is subject it; r5 c1→c2.\n\n\"just like that in the scene\" — \"just\" as modifier of c1, \"like\" relation from c1 to \"that\". Hmm, \"just\" grades \"like\" → per case, may be a modifier of the clause. Good. r2 \"in\" from n2 (that) to n3 (the scene) — hmm, \"that in the scene\" — \"in the scene\" modifies \"that\". Fine.\n\nc2 nonfinite \"keeping\" tied to c1 via r5. Good.\n\nL8.\n\nContinue reading.",
