    "text": "n=49: \"a big bench down at the bottom, then make a big sun in the top right corner, plus a tall tree on the left, then a small dog by the tree facing left\"\nc1 fragment modifier down, then, complement k2 (coordination of n1 big bench, n5 tall tree \"plus\"); r1 \"at\" n1→n2 (the bottom); k1 coordination \"then\" c1, c2; c2 imperative make complement n3 (a big sun); r2 \"in\" c2→n4 ambiguous; n7 small dog; r4 \"by\" n7→n8; c3 nonfinite facing left; r5 n1→n7; r6 n7→c3.\n\nHmm: \"then a small dog by the tree facing left\" — the dog is tied to n1 (bench) by r5 and to c3. Hmm, is the dog a complement of c1? c1's complement is k2 only. The dog n7 is tied from n1 (the bench) via r5 — that says the bench relates to the dog. Hmm, the \"then a small dog\" is a fourth list item (like c1's complements). It's not in k2 nor a complement of c1; it's linked to n1 by a relation. Is that acceptable? Hmm — it's tied, so not L6. But is it a wrong statement (L3: a relation has the wrong end)? The relation n1→n7 with no marker asserts a tie between the bench and the dog that the message doesn't state... but actually \"then a small dog\" is a fourth item in the list of things to make: \"make a big sun ..., plus a tall tree ..., then a small dog ...\". So the dog belongs to the same list (k2 or c1's complements). Being related to the bench nominal — that's arguably an acceptable \"list ties\" loose relation? Hmm.\n\nNote n5 (tall tree) is in k2 joined by \"plus\". The \"then\" before the dog — is \"then\" used? c1 has modifier \"then\" — one of the two \"then\"s. Text has \"then\" twice: \"then make a big sun\", \"then a small dog\". k1 conjunction \"then\" is one; c1 modifier \"then\" is the other. OK.\n\nIs r5 (n1→n7) acceptable as tie of the dog to the list? The next layer would read \"bench ... dog\" tie. Hmm, it's like a loose list relation. I'd say acceptable-ish (the dog is tied into the instruction). But is the \"then\" of c1's modifier the one before the dog while the dog is tied to n1? Slight mess. I'd accept L8? Hmm.\n\nOther check: \"a big bench down at the bottom\" — modifier down with relation \"at\" from n1 to \"the bottom\". Fine.\n\nHmm, \"plus a tall tree on the left\" is joined via k2 with n1. Fine.\n\nAccept L8 (loose but continuable). Hmm, but the dog being tied to the bench by a relation without marker — \"two nominals are merged\"? No, they're separate nominals related. Rules allow a relation without a marker between nominals (like \"block 2\", \"x 120\"). Acceptable.\n\nL8.\n\nn=50: \"make part 0 of House A purple\"\nc1 imperative make modifier purple complement n1 (\"part\"); n2 \"0\" tied r2 n1→n2; r1 \"of\" n1→n3 (\"House A\": dep House, head A).\n\n\"make part 0 of House A purple\" — \"purple\" as modifier of the clause: good per case (\"make the box red\"). n1 \"part\" with \"0\" tied — fine (number after noun as separate nominal tied). n3 \"House A\" as a name with head \"A\" and \"House\" dependent — hmm, \"House A\" is a name; case allows last word as head. Fine. L8.\n\nn=51: same as n=3 except \"nope\" — same structure. L8.\n\nn=52: \"Can you point at the region with the highest revenue, and how large a share is it?\"\nc1 interrogative auxiliary Can verb point subject you; r1 \"at\" c1→n2 (the region); r2 \"with\" n2→n3 (the highest revenue); k1 coordination \"and\" c1, c2; n4 \"how large a share\" (deps how, large, a, head share); c2 interrogative verb is subject n5 (it) complement n4.\n\nFine? \"how large a share is it\" — n4 as complement. head \"share\" with \"how large a\" deps. Acceptable (per \"what colour\" as dependent). Interrogative forms correct. L8.\n\nn=53: \"replace Bus driver note with \"Bring the revised notes to the meeting.\"\"\nc1 imperative replace complement n1 (\"Bus driver note\": deps Bus, driver, head note); r1 \"with\" ambiguous c1→n2 (\"Bring the revised notes to the meeting\" as one nominal head). Fine per content rule. L8.\n\nn=54: \"a little cat near the bench, then redo the house roof as one of those barn roofs, nothing else of the house, everything else untouched\"\nc1 fragment modifier then, complement n1 (a little cat); r1 near n1→n2; c2 imperative redo complement n3 (the house roof); r2 \"as\" c2→n4 (\"one\"); r3 \"of\" n4→n5 (those barn roofs); n6 = \"nothing else everything else untouched\" — a nominal with head \"nothing\" and deps else, everything, else, untouched!; r4 \"of\" n6→n7 (the house); k1 coordination c1, c2; r5 n4→n6.\n\nProblem: n6 merges \"nothing else of the house\" and \"everything else untouched\" into ONE nominal with head \"nothing\" and deps else, everything, else, untouched. That's two separate parts merged: \"nothing else of the house\" (a fragment) and \"everything else untouched\" (another fragment). They are separate clauses of the message. Merging them into one nominal → L3 (\"two nominals are merged\"). Quote 'nothing else of the house'.\n\nYes definitely L3.\n\nn=55: \"first the annotation sweep, then the underline on sections Intro, 2, 3 and Second, enabled marker Main, attrs in tow\"\nc1 fragment modifier first, then, complement n1 (the annotation sweep); n2 the underline; r1 \"on\" n2→k1 (coordination sections Intro, 2, 3, Second); n3 \"sections Intro\" (dep sections, head Intro); n4 \"2\"; n5 \"3\"; n6 \"Second\"; n7 = \"enabled marker Main\" (deps enabled, marker, head Main!); n8 \"attrs\"; r2 \"in\" n8→n9 (tow); r3 n1→n7; r4 n1→n8; r5 n1→n2.\n\nHmm. Lots of ties from n1 to n7, n8, n2. The list: \"then the underline on sections ..., enabled marker Main, attrs in tow\" — these are chart config items? The circuit ties them all to n1 (the annotation sweep) via relations. Hmm, whatever, they are tied.\n\nCheck words: first, the, annotation, sweep, then, the, underline, on, sections, Intro, 2, 3, and, Second, enabled, marker, Main, attrs, in, tow. Circuit: c1 modifiers first, then; n1; n2; r1 marker on; n3 sections Intro; n4 2; n5 3; k1 conjunction \"and\" conjuncts n3,n4,n5,n6; n6 Second; n7 enabled marker Main; n8 attrs; r2 in; n9 tow. All words accounted, \"and\" once.\n\nIs \"enabled\" as dependent of \"Main\"? n7 head \"Main\" with deps enabled, marker. Hmm, \"enabled marker Main\" — like \"enabled: true, marker: Main\". Garbled config. Head \"Main\" — plausible.\n\nAccept L8? Hmm. The n1 ties: r3, r4, r5 all from n1 (the annotation sweep). The list items are tied to the first item. Is that a wrong statement? \"a relation ... between wires that the message does not relate\" (L4)? The message doesn't relate \"the annotation sweep\" with \"attrs in tow\" — hmm, they are all items of a list of things, related only by juxtaposition. Tying each to n1 asserts a relation... but the rules require items be tied to something (L6 otherwise). Hmm, this is tension: a bare list item tied to nothing = L6? \"A clause or a nominal is tied to nothing inside its sentence\" = L6. So items need ties. The formalizer tied them to n1. Which end is \"right\"? Unclear; the message gives no relation. Since ties are needed and the message gives none, tying them to the list head is a plausible reading. I'd accept L8.\n\nn=56: \"draw a bird above the pond so the whole scene still reads — keeping the rest of the scene as it is\"\nc1 imperative draw complement n1 (a bird); r1 \"above\" c1→n2 (the pond); r2 \"so\" c1→c2; n3 the whole scene; c2 declarative still reads subject n3; c3 nonfinite keeping complement n4 (the rest); r3 \"of\" n4→n5; r4 \"as\" c3→c4 (is); n6 it; c4 declarative is subject it; r5 c1→c3.\n\nGood. L8.\n\nn=57: \"scatter plot of for each region scatter: sales versus profit, with gridlines behind it, legend at the bottom, as the closing chart for the review, and show the values on it\"\nc1 fragment modifier \"of\", complements n1 (scatter plot), n3 (scatter); r1 \"for\" n1→n2 (each region); n4 sales; r2 \"versus\" n4→n5 (profit); r3 \"with\" n4→n6 (gridlines); r4 \"behind\" n6→n7 (it); n8 legend; r5 \"at\" n8→n9 (the bottom); r6 \"as\" n8→n10 (the closing chart); r7 \"for\" n10→n11 (the review); k1 coordination \"and\" c1, c2; c2 imperative show complement n12 (the values); r8 \"on\" n12→n13 (it); r9 n3→n4; r10 n4→n8.\n\nHmm, \"of\" as a modifier of c1 — \"The marker is the linking word\"; \"of\" as a modifier rather than a marker → L3 (\"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\")? Hmm, \"scatter plot of for each region scatter\" — garbled. \"of\" held as modifier of the fragment. Is \"of\" a linking word here? \"scatter plot of [for each region scatter]\" — incoherent, so \"of\" is dangling. Holding it as a modifier of the clause... Per the rules: L3 \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\". Since \"of\" is a preposition = linking word, holding it as a modifier seems to be L3. But the message is garbled (\"of for each region scatter\") — the words don't settle what \"of\" links. Hmm. The misspelt/unknown rule covers misspelt words, not this.\n\nHmm, \"of\" ties nothing. Since the text is garbled and \"of\" can't be tied to anything sensible... A formalizer could hold \"of\" as a modifier. Would a judge reject? The rule is explicit: \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\" = L3. But is that rule about cases where the linking word's role is clear? \"of\" is a preposition; per the schema it should be a marker of a relation. Hmm.\n\nBut L3 requires \"A relation has ... a `marker` that is no linking word\" and \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\". \"of\" here held as a modifier → L3 candidate.\n\nHmm, let me think about the likely intent of this message: \"scatter plot of [for each region scatter: sales versus profit...]\" — the user is describing a chart. \"scatter plot of\" is a dangling \"of\" — actually there's a bigger issue: r9 n3→n4 ties \"scatter\" to \"sales\" and r10 n4→n8 ties \"sales\" to \"legend\". Hmm.\n\nIs \"of\" clearly a linking word here? In \"scatter plot of\", yes \"of\" links \"plot\" to something. Held as a modifier of the clause c1 — this is the L3 case. But the thing it links to is missing (for each region scatter). Hmm, but a formalizer could have made a relation from n1 (plot) to n3 (scatter) marked \"of\". \n\nRisky. Let me look at other flaws in n=57. Words: scatter, plot, of, for, each, region, scatter, sales, versus, profit, with, gridlines, behind, it, legend, at, the, bottom, as, the, closing, chart, for, the, review, and, show, the, values, on, it. Circuit: n1 scatter plot; c1 modifier of; n3 scatter; r1 for n1→n2; n4 sales; r2 versus; n5 profit; r3 with n4→n6; r4 behind n6→n7; n8 legend; r5 at n8→n9; r6 as n8→n10; r7 for n10→n11; c2 show; n12 values; r8 on n12→n13; r9 n3→n4; r10 n4→n8.\n\nAll words used once. Ties: n1 tied to n2 (for) and... n1 is a complement of c1 and tied? r1 n1→n2. n1 is in c1. OK.\n\n\"with gridlines behind it\" — r3 from n4 (sales) to n6 (gridlines)? That's odd — the gridlines belong to the scatter/chart, not to \"sales\". \"a scatter ... sales versus profit, with gridlines behind it\" — the \"with\" phrase attaches to the chart/scatter. r3 from n4 \"sales\" to \"gridlines\" — the start is \"sales\", which is the left side of the axis pair! That's a wrong start: the gridlines are behind the scatter, not behind \"sales\". Hmm, \"a start the words rule out, such as a nominal of another clause\" — n4 is a nominal in the same message. Is it ruled out? \"with gridlines behind it\" attaching to \"sales versus profit\" — hmm, \"sales versus profit with gridlines behind it\" could read as the pair. It's a possible attach: the with-phrase could attach to the nearest preceding nominal (\"profit\" n5, or \"sales\" n4). Since \"sales versus profit\" is a pair, attaching to \"sales\" is sloppy but not ruled out by grammar? Grammatically the with-phrase attaches to the nearest NP (\"profit\") or the clause.\n\nHmm. So candidates for n=57 rejection: \"of\" as modifier (L3); \"with\" from \"sales\" (L3, wrong start).\n\nWhich is cleaner? Hmm. Honestly \"of\" held as a modifier of the fragment clause is explicitly an L3 pattern (\"A linking word is held as a modifier\"). Since the message has \"scatter plot of for each region scatter\" — the formalizer couldn't tie \"of\" anywhere sensible; but \"of\" could tie n1→n3. Hmm, r9 ties n3→n4 and there's no \"of\" relation.\n\nI'll reject n=57 as L3, quoting 'of for each region scatter' and noting 'of' is a linking word held as a modifier, tying nothing.\n\nHmm, wait. Would a lenient judge accept? The rule list is explicit about linking words as modifiers being L3. I'll go with it.\n\nn=58: \"to the right of the main road, there should be a round well, on the side whre the sun sets.\"\nc1 declarative auxiliary should verb be subject n3 (\"there\") complement n4 (a round well); r1 \"to\" c1→n1 (the right); r2 \"of\" n1→n2 (the main road); r3 \"on\" c1→n5 (the side); r4 \"whre\" n5→c2; n6 the sun; c2 declarative sets subject n6.\n\n\"there\" as subject of \"should be\" — hmm, \"there should be a round well\": \"there\" is an expletive; held as subject. Is that a wrong statement? The rules don't say. \"The `subject`, when the words show one\" — \"there\" as subject is arguably fine (or a modifier). Not clearly wrong. Acceptable.\n\n\"whre\" misspelt \"where\" as marker of relation to c2 — held where it stands. Good (\"whre the sun sets\" — misspelt linking word as marker: acceptable? The rule for misspelt words allows holding it where it stands; as a marker linking to the clause, fine).\n\nL8.\n\nn=59: \"a little black cat curled up near the bench, then set the fence solidly bold mustard, right over the whole of it\"\nc1 declarative verb curled modifier up subject n1 (a little black cat)!! Hmm: \"a little black cat curled up near the bench\" — participle. Written as a declarative clause with verb \"curled\" and subject the cat. Per settled case: \"a bird facing left\" is a nonfinite clause; a participle... Form: \"curled up near the bench\" is a nonfinite (past participle) clause describing the cat, not a declarative. Hmm! Rule L1: \"a clause with a verb written as `fragment`\" — here it's the reverse direction: a nonfinite participle written as declarative. Is that covered? L1: \"Its `form` is wrong: a clear question written as `imperative` or `declarative`, or the reverse; a clause with a verb written as `fragment`.\"\n\nHmm, \"curled\" as a declarative verb with subject \"the cat\" would state \"the cat curled up near the bench\" — as a past-tense statement. In the message \"a little black cat curled up near the bench, then set the fence ...\" — could \"curled\" be a finite past verb? \"a little black cat curled up near the bench\" — as a fragment describing the scene (like \"a little cat curled up near the bench\"), it's a participle. But alone it could read as a past statement \"a little black cat curled up near the bench\" — hmm, that reads as a noun phrase with a reduced relative.\n\nCompare n=29's circuit: c1 nonfinite \"curled\" with n1 tied from... yes n=29 had form=nonfinite for \"curled\". Here form=declarative.\n\nIs a declarative form wrong? The message starts a list of instructions: \"a little black cat curled up near the bench, then set the fence ...\" So it's drawing instructions: put a cat curled up. So \"curled\" is a participle → nonfinite. Written declarative states a fact about the cat's past curling. Hmm — L1 says form wrong for \"a clause with a verb written as fragment\" (the participle-as-fragment direction is ok?). Hmm, actually the reverse: a participle clause written as declarative — is that listed? Not explicitly. Hmm.\n\nThe rules settled case: \"A participle that names or describes ('a group called g2', 'a bird facing left') is the `verb` of a nonfinite clause tied to the thing.\" Here the circuit does tie n1 (the cat) as subject. The form declarative vs nonfinite — hmm.\n\nUgh, ambiguity. Let's look at the rest: \"set the fence solidly bold mustard, right over the whole of it\": c2 imperative set modifier \"right\" complement n3 (the fence) complement n4 (\"solidly bold mustard\": deps solidly, bold, head mustard). Hmm: \"set the fence solidly bold mustard\" — \"solidly bold mustard\" as a nominal with head \"mustard\"? Colors: \"bold mustard\" = a color name (like \"mustard\"), with \"solidly\" a modifier. Hmm. Per the color patterns in these datasets, \"set the fence solidly bold mustard\" — \"solidly\" is a modifier (like line style \"solid\"?), \"bold\" hmm. Holding \"solidly bold mustard\" as one nominal with head \"mustard\" — could be plausible as a color name \"bold mustard\".\n\nBut per the settled case: \"a word after a nominal that is a `complement` of its clause and says how the thing should be is a `modifier` of the clause, also in a part with no verb: 'font bigger', 'bar gap small'\". Here \"solidly bold mustard\" says how the fence should be — a modifier phrase, not a nominal. Hmm, but it's a color value; in these datasets colors are often nominals (\"set the fence mustard\"). \"solidly bold\" would be modifiers.\n\nHmm, this is like \"the dog a size bigger\". Hmm.\n\nThe \"right over the whole of it\" — r2 \"over\" from c2 to n5 (the whole); r3 \"of\" n5→n6. \"right\" as modifier of c2 (grading \"over\") — good per case.\n\nSo for n=59, the clearest flaw? The form of c1: declarative for a participle describing a scene item. Compare n=29 (same first part) where the formalizer wrote nonfinite and I accepted. The rules: L1 \"Its form is wrong: ... a clause with a verb written as `fragment`.\" The declarative vs nonfinite mismatch for a participle: since \"curled\" is a participle, writing the clause as declarative claims a finite statement. Hmm, does L1 cover it? Only partially.\n\nHmm, let me consider: is \"a little black cat curled up near the bench\" ambiguous between the participle and a past-tense statement? \"a little black cat curled up near the bench, then set the fence...\" — the \"then set the fence\" command follows. Grammatically, \"a little black cat curled up near the bench\" cannot be a sentence (a determiner-initial NP + verb = \"a cat curled up\" is a grammatical sentence? \"A little black cat curled up near the bench\" IS a grammatical sentence (subject \"a little black cat\", verb \"curled up\"). Yes! \"A little black cat curled up near the bench.\" is a complete declarative sentence. So the reading is grammatically available. But then the message would be asserting a fact, and the following \"then set the fence ...\" is a command. Plausible?\n\nIn the message context (drawing instructions), the first part is a scene description. The formalizer wrote declarative with subject. Given both readings are grammatically available, and the circuit keeps all words with a coherent structure, judge fairly → accept? Hmm.\n\nBut then the participle information \"curled up near the bench\" as a description of the cat is retained (subject n1 with verb curled, near the bench). The next layer can continue. I'd accept L8.\n\nHmm, hmm. But L1: \"Its form is wrong: a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse; a clause with a verb written as fragment.\" A participle clause written as declarative isn't listed. So accept.\n\nWait — but is \"solidly bold mustard\" a merge of the fence and the color? c2 complements: n3 (the fence), n4 (\"solidly bold mustard\"). Two complements: the thing and the value. That's fine (like \"make the box red\" but with nominal value). Acceptable.\n\nAlso is \"set ... right over the whole of it\" — \"right\" modifier. Fine.\n\nI'll accept L8 for n=59. Hmm, but compare with n=29 where the same \"curled\" was nonfinite — both acceptable readings.\n\nn=60: \"mouth — add a box, please\"\nc2 fragment complement n1 (mouth); c1 imperative add discourse please complement n2 (a box); k1 coordination c2, c1.\n\n\"mouth\" is a lone word before a dash — a fragment with a nominal. Fine, coordinated with the add clause. L8? Hmm, is \"mouth\" a discourse word? No, it's a noun. Fine. L8.\n\nContinue.",
