    "text": "n=61: \"Main is who the Intro meeting should carry as participants\"\nc1 declarative is subject n1 (Main); n2 \"who\"; n3 the Intro meeting; c2 declarative auxiliary should verb carry subject n3 complement n2 (who); r1 \"as\" c2→n4 (participants); r2 c1→c2.\n\nHmm, \"who the Intro meeting should carry as participants\" — the clause c2 opens with \"who\" held as complement n2. Per settled case: a clause opening with a question word inside another clause states: declarative and tied. Good.\n\nIs \"who\" as complement fine? \"The Intro meeting should carry who as participants\" — \"who\" as complement. Plausible.\n\nL8.\n\nn=62: \"turn bold on for characters 10 to 15 of Body\"\nc1 imperative turn modifier bold modifier on; r1 \"for\" c1→n1 (characters); n2 \"10\"; n3 \"to 15\" (dep \"to\", head \"15\")!; r2 \"of\" n1→n4 (Body); r3 n1→n2; r4 n2→n3.\n\nHmm: n3 = \"to 15\" with \"to\" as a DEPENDENT of \"15\". \"to\" is a linking word held as a dependent → L3 (\"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\").\n\nHmm, or is \"10 to 15\" handled as a range nominal? The case \"Two bare nouns side by side after a marker: either may be head\" — not. Here \"to\" is a preposition/linking word joining two numbers. Held as a dependent of \"15\" → it's a linking word held as a dependent. Per L3 that fails.\n\nHmm, but \"to\" in \"10 to 15\" — is it a linking word or part of a range phrase? It is a preposition-like word. The rule: \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\" = L3. So reject n=62, quote 'to 15'? Note: \"'to' is a linking word held as a dependent of '15', so the range is not tied\".\n\nHmm, but could \"to\" be a fixed-phrase part? \"10 to 15\" is a range; the schema has no range wire. Hmm, similar to \"and\" between modifiers case (\"the schema has no wire for that pair\") which allows discourse. But the rules' settled case only allows discourse for conjunctions. For \"to\"...\n\nHmm, the case list says \"So is a conjunction between two modifier words of one clause ('flat and dry'): the schema has no wire for that pair.\" — \"so is a conjunction ... discourse\". A relation with marker \"to\" from n2 (10) to n3 (15) would be the natural handling. Holding \"to\" as a dependent of \"15\" — per L3 fails.\n\nAlternatively accept as a number-pair phrase? Hmm.\n\nLet me consider fairness: is this \"a wrong statement or lost explicit information\"? The range 10 to 15 remains readable (\"10\" and \"to 15\"). Hmm. The rule about linking words held as dependents is explicit in L3, and \"to\" is definitely a linking word. I'll reject L3.\n\nHmm, but I want to be careful not to over-reject. Let me tally how many rejections I have so far: n=7, 10, 12, 13, 16, 18, 19, 22, 23, 27, 29, 35, 37, 41, 43, 44, 45, 47, 48, 54, 57, 62... That's a lot (22 of 150 by line 72). Rejection rate ~30% by mid-packet. Hmm, that's plausible for formalizer output? Unknown. The instructions warn to judge fairly and accept plausible readings. Let me be careful with borderline ones. I'll revisit the borderline list at the end.\n\nn=63: \"a pointed for me, ear olive\"\nn1 head \"a\"; c1 fragment modifier pointed complement n1; r1 \"for\" c1→n2 (me); n3 \"ear olive\" (dep ear, head olive); r2 n1→n3.\n\nGarbled (like n=4, n=38). \"a\" as a nominal head — hmm, in n=38 \"a\" was a modifier. Here n1 head \"a\" with \"pointed\" as modifier of the clause and a relation \"for\" to \"me\". Then \"ear olive\" nominal tied to n1.\n\nIs \"a\" as nominal head acceptable? \"a\" is an article — a word with no content alone. Garbled message. Hmm, \"a pointed for me, ear olive\" — same family as n=4 \"a sad for me, eyebrow yellow default\" and n=38 \"a looking out for me, eye auburn big\". In n=4, \"a\" was discourse (L2 risk); n=38 \"a\" was a modifier; n=63 \"a\" is a nominal head.\n\nFor n=63: \"a\" as a head nominal — nothing says \"a\" is a thing. Hmm, but garbled. The head being a function word: \"The `head` is wrong\" (L3)? Ehh. For a garbled message, holding \"a\" where it stands... Hmm, \"a\" is not misspelt or unknown; it's an article. An article as a nominal head is a wrong statement (the article belongs with \"pointed\"/\"ear\"). Hmm, but which? \"a pointed ear, olive\"? These garbled messages come from a template like \"a pointed ear for me, ... olive\".\n\nI'd accept these garbled ones as plausible: no clear wrong statement to quote... Actually for n=63, one could quote 'a pointed' and say the article is held as a head nominal, losing that it belongs with the next word. Hmm.\n\nGiven \"if you cannot quote the words, the circuit is acceptable\" and the fairness instruction, garbled messages with all words placed should mostly pass. I'll accept n=63 as L8. But then consistency: n=4, n=38 also L8. OK.\n\nn=64: \"and Claims by month on the revenue trend needs the same yellow fading to red, please\"\nc1 declarative discourse and, verb needs, discourse please, subject n1 (Claims); r1 \"by\" n1→n2 (month); r2 \"on\" ambiguous c1→n3 (the revenue trend); n4 \"the same yellow\" (deps the, same, head yellow); c2 nonfinite fading subject n4; r3 \"to\" c2→n5 (red); r4 c1→c2.\n\n\"needs the same yellow fading to red\" — c1 has no complements! n4 \"the same yellow\" is only tied to c2 (as its subject), and c2 tied to c1 via r4. So \"needs\" has no complement written; the thing needed is inside the nonfinite clause. Hmm: \"and Claims by month on the revenue trend needs the same yellow fading to red\" — the object of \"needs\" is \"the same yellow fading to red\". The circuit makes \"the same yellow\" the subject of \"fading\" and ties c2 to c1 — that's the same structure as \"a participle describing/naming\"... The relation r4 c1→c2 with no marker represents the object. Acceptable? It's like \"the dog a size bigger\"? Hmm.\n\nThe reading: \"needs [the same yellow [fading to red]]\" — the circuit has n4 as subject of nonfinite \"fading\". The next layer reads \"Claims ... needs the same yellow which fades to red\". Reasonable. Accept L8.\n\nHmm, \"discourse and\" at start — allowed by the case. \"please\" in discourse — fine.\n\nL8.\n\nn=65: \"reschedule Review, 2026-11-18 at 10:00 UTC\"\nc1 imperative reschedule complement n1 (Review); n2 \"2026-11-18\"; r1 \"at\" ambiguous c1→n3 (\"10:00 UTC\" — dep 10:00, head UTC); r2 n1→n2.\n\nHmm: \"at 10:00 UTC\" — n3 with head \"UTC\" and \"10:00\" dependent. Hmm, \"10:00\" is the time and \"UTC\" the zone: head should be \"10:00\"? Per \"two bare nouns side by side after a marker: either may be the head\" — \"at 10:00 UTC\": the two bare \"nouns\" are \"10:00\" and \"UTC\". So either may be head. Passes.\n\n\"2026-11-18\" as a nominal tied to Review via r2 — the date tied to the thing. Fine. L8.\n\nn=66: \"get the dog on the lawn to chase the cat, and leave everything else in the picture untouched — the way the drawing looks so far\"\nc1 imperative get complement n1 (the dog); r1 \"on\" ambiguous c1→n2; r2 \"to\" c1→c2 (nonfinite chase) complement n3 (the cat); k1 coordination and: c1, c3; c3 imperative leave modifier untouched complement n4 (everything else), complement n6 (the way); r3 \"in\" n4→n5 (the picture); n7 the drawing; c4 declarative looks modifier so, modifier far, subject n7; r4 n6→c4.\n\nHmm: \"leave everything else in the picture untouched — the way the drawing looks so far\": \"the way the drawing looks so far\" — n6 \"the way\" as complement of c3 with r4 to c4. Hmm, per n=20 pattern, accepted. \"so far\" as modifiers of c4 — fine (fixed phrase).\n\nL8.\n\nn=67: long. \"for the monthly ops review on Friday I need the revenue trend legible from the back of the conference room, so how about line thinner?; also let's have the marker borders thicker on the revenue trend before we circulate it wider; plus show both grid lines\"\nc1 interrogative verb \"need\" modifier legible subject I complement n4 (the revenue trend)!! \n\n\"for the monthly ops review on Friday I need the revenue trend legible...\" — \"I need the revenue trend legible from the back\" — a declarative statement (not a question). c1 form=interrogative → L1 (a clause with a clear declarative form written as interrogative? L1 says \"a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\"). \"or the reverse\" = a declarative written as interrogative. So L1. Quote: 'I need the revenue trend legible'.\n\nYes I think that's a clear rejection: c1 is written interrogative but the message's clause is declarative (\"I need ...\").\n\nAlso k1: coordination with \"so\" and \"plus\" joining c1, c2, c3, c6 — the \"so\" joins c1 with c2 (\"so how about line thinner?\") and \"plus\" joins c6. Hmm, c2 is a fragment with ellipsis=$c1 — \"how about line thinner?\" with ellipsis of \"need\"?? That's odd: \"how about line thinner?\" doesn't share \"need\". Hmm, L4? ellipsis on a fragment that shares no verb. Hmm, \"how about line thinner\" — hmm, per n=47 pattern \"how about thinner grid lines\" was a fragment; here the formalizer added ellipsis=$c1. Does \"how about line thinner\" share the verb \"need\"? \"so how about line thinner?\" = \"so how about [having] line thinner?\" — no shared verb with \"need\". Hmm, but per settled case (n=47 style), ellipsis is optional. Adding a wrong ellipsis → L4? Hmm, L4: \"ellipsis on a fragment that shares no verb\".\n\nBut L1 first. I'll reject L1 for the form.\n\nn=68: \"same as last step exept with objects 3 and 4\"\nc1 fragment modifier same; r1 \"as\" c1→n1 (last step); n2 \"exept\"; r2 \"with\" n2→k1 (objects 3 and 4); n3 objects; n4 3; n5 4; r3 n1→n2; r4 n3→n4.\n\n\"exept\" is a misspelt \"except\", held as a nominal head \"exept\" — hmm, \"exept\" is a preposition/linking word (except). Held as a nominal → is it \"A linking word is held as ... a dependent\"? It's a head, not a dependent. Held as a nominal head — \"exept\" as head of a nominal tied from n1 and to k1 via \"with\". Hmm.\n\nOptions: it's a misspelt word (except) — held where it stands, when words don't settle. Hmm, the words do settle it's \"except\"? \"except with objects 3 and 4\" — the segment \"exept with objects 3 and 4\" — has the \"with\" as marker. Hmm.\n\nIs holding \"exept\" as a nominal head a wrong statement? It makes \"exept\" a thing (\"same as last step [exept] with objects 3 and 4\"). Hmm. The information \"except\" (exclusion) is arguably preserved by the word itself standing there. A next layer sees \"exept\" and can interpret.\n\nBut the rule \"A misspelt or unknown word, held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\" — \"exept\" is misspelt and it stands where it stands. Passes. L8? Hmm, holding it as a nominal head — \"held where it stands\" — it stands between \"last step\" and \"with objects 3 and 4\", and being a nominal tied there is roughly \"where it stands\". I'd accept L8.\n\nn=69: \"perhaps you could seat a green block in the middle of the top row and in the second column of red blocks\"\nc1 declarative modifier perhaps, auxiliary could, verb seat, subject you, complement n2; r1 \"in\" c1→n3 (the middle); r2 \"of\" n3→n4 (top row); k1 coordination \"and\" conjunct r1, r3 (relations!); r3 \"in\" c1→n5 (the second column); r4 \"of\" n5→n6 (red blocks).\n\nHmm — k1 coordinates relations r1 and r3. Is coordination of relations allowed? \"syntax.coordination: wires put side by side, conjunct`. Rule: \"Which earlier wire a coordination joins, when grammar allows several\" — the conjuncts are relations; that's allowed? Hmm, \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\" (L3). Coordinating the two \"in\" relations — hmm, the message \"a green block in the middle of the top row and in the second column of red blocks\" coordinates the two place phrases. Representing each place as a relation and coordinating them: plausible. Hmm, but the second relation r3's \"of red blocks\"... \"of red blocks\" – hmm.\n\nHmm, is \"form=declarative\" right for \"perhaps you could seat a green block...\"? It's a request phrased as a statement — declarative form is correct (not interrogative). Good.\n\nAccept L8? The coordination of two relations is unusual but grammar-wise the message coordinates the two prepositional phrases. Accept.\n\nn=70: \"draw a small sun partly hidden behind the tree, not in front of it.\"\nc1 imperative draw complement n1 (a small sun); c2 nonfinite modifier partly verb hidden; r1 \"behind\" c2→n2 (the tree); r2 negation=not marker \"in\" c2→n3 (front); r3 \"of\" n3→n4 (it); r4 n1→c2.\n\n\"not in front of it\" — negation held on the relation r2 — good, negation attribute of a relation exists in the schema. Good. Accept L8.\n\nHmm, \"partly\" as modifier of c2, \"hidden\" verb nonfinite tied to n1 via r4. Good.\n\nL8.\n\nn=71: \"put a box between Draft ant Review\"\nc1 imperative put complement n1 (a box); r1 \"between\" ambiguous c1→n2 (\"Draft ant Review\": deps Draft, ant, head Review).\n\n\"ant\" is a typo for \"and\" — \"Draft and Review\" are two things. The circuit holds \"Draft\", \"ant\", \"Review\" as one nominal with head Review. Hmm, that merges two names into one nominal. \"between Draft and Review\" — two objects. Merged into one nominal → L3 (two nominals merged)? Or is \"ant\" a misspelt word held where it stands?\n\nHmm. \"between Draft ant Review\" — the two ends of \"between\" should be \"Draft\" and \"Review\". Merging them into a single nominal \"Draft ant Review\" with head \"Review\" — the \"between\" relation then has only one end. The information that there are two distinct boxes is lost → L3.\n\nHmm, but is \"ant\" an unknown word (misspelt \"and\")? If the formalizer treats \"ant\" as an unknown word, it holds it inside the nominal — the case allows misspelt words held where they stand when the words don't settle what it is. But \"Draft\" and \"Review\" are two clearly separate names, and merging them into one nominal (head Review, with Draft as dependent) makes \"Draft\" a description of \"Review\" — \"the Draft Review\" — hmm, that's like a two-word name (\"Sales 3\"), which is allowed! \"A name of several words may stand whole in head, or with its last word as head and the others as dependent.\"\n\nSo \"Draft ant Review\" as a nominal with head \"Review\" and deps \"Draft\",\"ant\" is a name-with-typo reading. Hmm. Both \"Draft\" and \"Review\" are known names of boxes though. In this dataset (mind maps), \"Draft\" and \"Review\" are separate nodes.\n\nJudging: merging two nominals into one is L3. But the words don't settle (\"ant\" garbles the conjunction) so the name reading is plausible...? Hmm. I think a fair judge would accept this one, since \"ant\" is unknown and the words are held where they stand — the next layer can read \"Draft ant Review\" and recover.\n\nHmm. Ehh. But there's a real loss: \"between X and Y\" — the two ends. With one nominal, the relation \"between\" ends at one nominal. Then the next layer gets \"between Draft ant Review\" as a whole, and can interpret. I'd accept L8 (misspelt word rule).\n\nn=72: \"let's have the font smaler on the profit chart and then set the error bar cap size smaller, that's the tweak\"\nc1 imperative verb let complement n1 (\"'s\"); c2 nonfinite verb have; c3 nonfinite modifier \"the\" verb \"smaler\" complement n2 (font); r1 \"on\" c3→n3 (profit chart); k1 coordination \"and\" c2, c4; c4 nonfinite modifier then, verb set, modifier smaller, complement n4 (error bar cap size); n5 that; c5 declarative \"'s\" subject that complement n6 (the tweak); k2 coordination c1, c5; r2 c1→k1; r3 c2→c3.\n\nProblems: c3 \"smaler\" — the word \"the\" held as a modifier of c3, and \"smaler\" as verb. Hmm: \"have the font smaler\" — the font is a nominal, \"smaler\" a modifier. The circuit: c3 nonfinite verb \"smaler\" with complement n2 \"font\", modifier \"the\". That's wrong: \"the\" is the article of \"font\"; holding it as a clause modifier splits the nominal (L3: \"The words of one nominal are split between wires\"). And \"smaler\" as a verb — a misspelt adjective (\"smaller\") held as verb → hmm, \"A word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb\" doesn't apply. Holding an adjective as the verb — is that L1 (\"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here\")? Or L3 (head/modifier wrong).\n\nThe cleaner statement: \"the\" is a modifier of the clause while \"font\" is a nominal → the nominal \"the font\" is split → L3. Also \"smaler\" as verb.\n\nHmm, which grade? L1: \"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here\". \"smaler\" — misspelt \"smaller\", an adjective. Could \"smaler\" be a verb? No. Hmm, but the misspelt-word rule: unknown word held where it stands... held as a verb though; the rule says held where it stands, not necessarily as a verb.\n\nHmm. L3 for splitting \"the font\": the rule \"The words of one nominal are split between wires\" is explicit. Since L1 comes first and might apply (\"verb is a word that is no verb\"), hmm.\n\nLet me think: c3's verb \"smaler\" — is \"smaler\" a word that is no verb here? An adjective in \"have the font smaller\". So yes, L1 candidate. And the article \"the\" held as a clause modifier is L3.\n\nI'll go L3 quoting 'the font smaler' and note the nominal \"the font\" is split ('the' as a clause modifier) and 'smaler' is not a verb... Hmm, but naming the wrong statement matters more than the grade maybe. I'll pick L3 with a note about the split nominal. Hmm.\n\nHmm, actually — wait. Is c3's structure \"have [the font smaler]\" where c3 is a nonfinite clause whose verb is \"smaler\"? Grammatically reading \"smaler\" as a verb with \"font\" as object and \"the\" as modifier — that's a wrong statement about the words: \"the\" belongs to \"font\" (article can't be separated from its noun). That's a clear L3 split. Good: L3.\n\nLet me be careful about L1 vs L3 ordering. The instruction: give L8 or \"the first of L1 to L6 that applies\". If L1 applies, give L1. Does L1 apply? \"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here\" — is \"smaler\" a word that is no verb here? Yes, \"smaler\" is an adjective (misspelt \"smaller\"). But the misspelt rule (\"an unknown word held where it stands when the words do not settle what it is\") could excuse it... \"smaler\" next to \"font\" with \"the\"... the words settle it's the adjective \"smaller\". Hmm.\n\nI'll go with L1 then? Hmm. Let me weigh which reads as a fairer note. The note can mention both: \"'the font smaler' holds 'the' as a clause modifier and 'smaler' as the `verb`, so the nominal 'the font' is split\" — with grade L3. That's clear and defensible. But if the reference grade is L1, my grade mismatches.\n\nGiven ambiguity and the explicit L3 rule about splitting a nominal, I'll take L3.\n\nContinue reading.",
