    "text": "n=73: \"instead, a small dog down at the bottom, next clear the top two of the five green blocks, leaving the bottom three exactly as they are — keep the rest as it is\"\nc1 fragment modifier instead, down, next, **clear**, complement n1 (a small dog), n3 (the top two); r1 \"at\" c1→n2 (the bottom); r2 \"of\" n3→n4 (the five green blocks); c2 nonfinite leaving modifier exactly complement n5 (the bottom three); r3 \"as\" c2→c3 (they are); c4 imperative keep complement n7; r4 \"as\" c4→c5; k1 coordination c1, c4; r5 n1→c2.\n\nProblem: \"next clear the top two ...\" — \"clear\" is the VERB of a command (\"clear the top two of the five green blocks\"), but it's held as a modifier of the fragment c1. So the command \"clear the top two...\" has no clause → L5 (\"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier ... and no clause has it as verb\"). Yes! Clear L5.\n\nQuote: 'next clear the top two' — note: \"'clear' is held as a modifier of the fragment, so the command 'clear the top two of the five green blocks' has no clause\" → L5.\n\nn=74: \"add the Cool, Grind, Sort chain after Sift, arrows and all\"\nc1 imperative add complement n1 (the Cool Grind Sort chain); r1 \"after\" ambiguous c1→n2 (Sift); n3 arrows; k1 coordination \"and\" c1, c2; n4 all; c2 fragment complement k2 ellipsis=c1; k2 coordination n3, n4.\n\n\"arrows and all\" — c2 fragment with ellipsis=c1 (\"add\") and complement = coordination of \"arrows\" and \"all\". Hmm, \"arrows and all\" is an idiom meaning \"including the arrows\". Fine-ish; ellipsis on the fragment saying it shares \"add\". Acceptable? The fragment \"arrows and all\" shares \"add\"? Ambiguous idiom. Hmm, the ellipsis claims it shares the verb \"add\" — plausible (\"add ... arrows and all\"). Accept L8.\n\nn=75: \"mid-move: object Intro relocates to 260, 240\"\nc1 declarative modifier mid, modifier move, verb relocates, subject n1 (object Intro); r1 \"to\" c1→n2 (\"260 240\": dep 260, head 240).\n\nAcceptable? \"mid-move\" as two modifiers of the clause — \"mid\" and \"move\" as modifiers. Hmm, \"mid-move:\" is a label (\"mid-move\"). Held as modifiers of the clause; plausible.\n\n\"260, 240\" — a coordinate pair. n2 head \"240\" with dep \"260\" — hmm, two numbers merged into one nominal. Per the case: \"a number or a name right after a noun ('block 2', 'x 120', ...) may be a dependent in that noun's nominal, or a nominal of its own tied to the noun by a relation without a marker.\" Here 260 and 240 both follow nothing (they're the target). Merging into one nominal with head 240: reads \"240 with 260\"? The next layer gets both numbers in order. Acceptable-ish → L8.\n\nHmm, a coordinate pair as one nominal — the rules' examples of number pairs are \"x 120\". Accept.\n\nn=76: \"instead, a small dog down at the bottom, next shift the BMW block down and a little left past the Esso block, lining it up above the Shell block, then shift the BMW block left until it is in line with the Target block below — keep the rest as it is\"\nc1 imperative modifier instead verb keep complement n11 (the rest)!! That's wrong: \"keep the rest as it is\" is the last part, but c1 has verb \"keep\" and modifier \"instead\" — c1 begins the message? c1 is coordinated via k1 with \"then\" as conjunction joining c1, c2, c3, c5. So c1 = \"instead keep the rest as it is\" — the \"instead\" from the start, \"keep\" from the end. Hmm, that merges the first word with the last clause. Hmm.\n\nc2 fragment \"down\" complement n1 (a small dog) with r1 \"at\" → the bottom. OK.\nc3 imperative \"next\" verb shift modifier down, discourse \"and\", modifier \"a\", modifier \"little\", modifier \"left\", complement n3 (the BMW block); r2 \"past\" c3→n4 (the Esso block).\nc4 nonfinite lining up complement n5 (it); r3 \"above\" c4→n6 (Shell block); r8 c3→c4.\nc5 imperative shift modifier left complement n7; r4 \"until\" c5→c6; c6 declarative is subject it; r5 \"in\" c6→n9 (line); r6 \"with\" n9→n10 (the Target block below); r7 \"as\" c1→c7; c7 declarative is subject it (n12).\n\nWords: instead(c1), a small dog n1, down c2, at r1, the bottom n2, next c3, shift, the BMW block n3, down mod, and discourse c3, a little left mods, past r2, the Esso block n4, lining c4, it n5, up mod, above r3, the Shell block n6, then (k1 conjunction), shift c5, the BMW block n7, left mod, until r4, it n8, is c6, in r5, line n9, with r6, the Target block below n10, keep c1 verb, the rest n11, as r7, it n12, is c7.\n\nBig issue: \"and a little left\" — \"and\" held as discourse of c3 while it joins \"down\" and \"a little left\" (two modifiers). Per the settled case, a conjunction between two modifier words of one clause may be discourse (schema has no wire). OK.\n\n\"a\" and \"little\" as modifiers of c3 — \"a little left\" like \"a bit left\" — per the case, grading words as modifiers; \"a little\" may also be a nominal of its own. Fine.\n\nThen \"keep the rest as it is\": c1 = instead keep the rest as it is, coordinated at the START of the list k1 (c1 first conjunct), where \"instead\" is the first word. Hmm, that means the \"instead\" (first word) is in the same clause as \"keep the rest\" (last part). And \"instead\" logically belongs to the whole instruction. Is that a wrong statement? Hmm, \"Words of one written clause are held in another\" — the first part \"instead, a small dog down at the bottom\" — hmm.\n\nAlso, c1 (keep) is placed as the FIRST conjunct of k1 with the conjunction \"then\" joining c1, c2, c3, c5 — the coordination's conjunctions: \"then\" appears twice in the message (\"then shift the BMW block left\") and... \"next\" is in c3's modifier. k1 has conjunction=\"then\" only once for 4 conjuncts. Hmm, \"instead\" as modifier of c1, and c1 comes first. The next layer reading the coordination: [keep the rest as it is], [a small dog down at the bottom], [shift the BMW block ...], [shift the BMW block left ...]. Order changed (keep moved to front) — \"The order and the names of the wires\" are not judged. Hmm, the coordination order isn't judged?\n\nBut: \"instead\" (the first word of the message) is held in the clause of \"keep\" (the last part) — is that \"Words of one written clause are held in another\"? The first part \"instead, a small dog down at the bottom\" has c2 = fragment \"down\" complement \"a small dog\". So \"instead\" belongs to the first part but is in c1. Hmm.\n\nThat's a real problem: \"instead\" (word 1) placed in a clause made of words from the end (\"keep the rest as it is\"). The clause c1 doesn't exist in the message — \"instead ... keep the rest\" merges two separated parts. This is a wrong statement: the words of the first part are held in the clause of the last part.\n\nActually, is it? c1 holds \"instead\" + \"keep the rest as it is\". The message: \"instead, a small dog down at the bottom, next shift ... — keep the rest as it is\". The \"instead\" is separated by the whole message from \"keep\". So c1's clause conflates them. L1: \"Words of one written clause are held in another.\" Hmm, c1 isn't a written clause; it's assembled.\n\nGrade? Hmm, maybe L3 (\"Words stand in the wrong wire\") for \"instead\". Hmm.\n\nAlternatively focus on a clearer flaw: \"then shift the BMW block left until it is in line with the Target block below\" — c5 shift modifier left; r4 \"until\" c5→c6; c6 \"it is\" ... \"in line\" — n9 head \"line\" with r5 \"in\" from c6. Fine.\n\n\"the Target block below\" — \"below\" as dependent of \"block\": \"A word of place after a nominal ('near the lake there', 'the one above') may be a dependent of that nominal or a modifier of the clause\" — passes.\n\nHmm, what about \"next shift the BMW block down and a little left past the Esso block\" — \"next\" is held as a modifier of c3 (a word of order as a modifier — allowed: \"Now at the start ... may be discourse or a modifier\"). \"next\" as modifier — fine.\n\nSo the flaw is the \"instead\"+\"keep\" clause. How clear is it? c1 = \"instead keep the rest as it is\" — the message's last part is \"keep the rest as it is\" which is a clause of its own; adding \"instead\" to it (a word from the message's first part) is holding a word of one written clause in another → L1.\n\nHmm, but \"instead\" is a discourse-ish word; \"which clause holds a discourse word\" is NOT judged. If \"instead\" were a discourse word, its clause wouldn't matter. Here it's a modifier of c1. Hmm, but the rule \"Which clause holds a `discourse` word\" is not judged — that's for discourse words. \"instead\" as a modifier of the clause — the rule \"A word that grades...\" no.\n\nHmm. Risky. Let me look for other flaws in n=76.\n\nc3's modifiers: down, a, little, left — \"shift the BMW block down and a little left\" — \"down\" and \"left\"; fine.\n\nk1 coordination: conjuncts c1, c2, c3, c5 with \"then\". Message order: [instead, a small dog down at the bottom], [next shift ...], [then shift ... left ...], [keep the rest as it is]. The coordination joins them; order not judged.\n\nBut wait — is c1 an imperative \"keep\"? yes. So k1 joins imperative clauses; fine.\n\nHmm. So the \"instead\" placement is the only flaw. Given \"Which clause holds a discourse word\" is not judged, and \"instead\" as a modifier... hmm, but the substantive issue is that c1's clause = \"instead keep the rest as it is\" is a clause the message doesn't contain — because \"instead\" belongs to the beginning.\n\nI think a fair judge might accept this: all words placed, structure plausible, next layer can follow. Actually no — the next layer would read \"instead keep the rest as it is\" as an instruction \"instead, keep the rest as it is\", and separately \"a small dog down at the bottom\". The meaning is essentially preserved! \"instead\" placement doesn't change much. Hmm.\n\nI'll accept n=76 as L8? Hmm. Let me hold it as borderline and decide later.\n\nHmm, one more check on n=76: is any word missing? \"instead, a, small, dog, down, at, the, bottom, next, shift, the, BMW, block, down, and, a, little, left, past, the, Esso, block, lining, it, up, above, the, Shell, block, then, shift, the, BMW, block, left, until, it, is, in, line, with, the, Target, block, below, keep, the, rest, as, it, is\". Circuit has: instead(c1 mod), a small dog(n1), down(c2 mod), at(r1), the bottom(n2), next(c3 mod), shift, the BMW block(n3), down(c3 mod), and(discourse c3), a(c3 mod), little(c3 mod), left(c3 mod), past(r2), the Esso block(n4), lining(c4), it(n5), up(c4 mod), above(r3), the Shell block(n6), then(k1), shift(c5), the BMW block(n7), left(c5 mod), until(r4), it(n8), is(c6), in(r5), line(n9), with(r6), the Target block below(n10), keep(c1), the rest(n11), as(r7), it(n12), is(c7). All good.\n\nOK, later decision. Lean accept.\n\nn=77: \"put a boy about the same size as the bear\"\nc1 imperative put complement n1 (a boy); n2 = \"about the same size\" (deps about, the, same, head size); r1 \"as\" n2→n3 (the bear); r2 n1→n2.\n\n\"about\" as a dependent of \"size\" — \"about the same size\" — \"about\" is a grading word. As a dependent of the nominal it becomes part of the description; hmm, \"about\" grades \"the same size\" → per the case, grading words are modifiers of the clause; held as a dependent fails L3? Hmm, the case: \"Words that grade a graded word ('a bit longer', 'way shorter', 'a little smaller') are `modifier` words of the clause... A nominal whose head is the graded word ... fails: L3.\"\n\nHere \"about\" grades \"the same size\", and it's a dependent of \"size\" whose head is \"size\" (the graded word). Hmm, the case says a nominal whose head is the graded word fails: L3 (e.g., \"a bit longer\" with head \"longer\"). Here the nominal \"about the same size\" has head \"size\" with \"about\" (grading) as dependent → by analogy, L3.\n\nHmm. But \"about the same size\" is a fairly natural nominal phrase: \"a boy about the same size as the bear\" — \"about\" modifies \"the same size\". Hmm, and the clause has no word for \"about\" otherwise. The rule would have \"about\" as a modifier of the clause c1.\n\nHmm, is this the same pattern as \"a bit longer\"? \"a bit\" = grading; \"about\" = approximation grading. Yes similar.\n\nBut hold on, is L3 right, or should we accept \"about\" as a dependent since \"the same size\" is a noun phrase and \"about\" approximates it? The settled case says: \"A nominal whose `head` is the graded word ('a bit longer' with head 'longer') or the lone grading word ('way') fails: L3.\" The nominal \"about the same size\" has head \"size\" — the graded word is \"same\"/\"size\"? \"the same size\" — \"same\" grades... hmm, \"about\" grades \"the same size\" as a whole. The head is \"size\", which is the graded phrase's head. So by analogy fails L3.\n\nHmm, this feels like it could go either way. The natural reading \"a boy about the same size as the bear\" — holding \"about\" as a dependent of \"size\" is what a formalizer would do. Would the rules' author reject? The case exists precisely for this pattern... The example is \"a bit longer\" where head is \"longer\" (the graded adjective). Here head is \"size\" (a noun with \"same\" as its modifier). \"about\" is not grading \"size\" alone but \"the same size\" — hmm.\n\nEhh. I'd accept L8 here (the nominal \"about the same size\" is a measurement phrase, not a graded word). Judging fairly → accept.\n\nn=78: \"same thing as step one except with the second figure\"\nn1 \"same thing\"; r1 \"as\" n1→n2 (step); n3 \"one\" tied r3 n2→n3; r2 marker \"except with\" n1→n4 (the second figure); c1 fragment complement n1.\n\nMarker \"except with\" as one marker — hmm, \"except with\" is a compound linking phrase. Acceptable? The rules allow fixed phrases... \"as\" and \"except with\" both markers. Fine. L8.\n\nn=79: \"halve teh Shelve label size\"\nn1 = \"halve teh Shelve label size\" — ALL words as dependents of head \"size\"; c1 fragment complement n1.\n\nProblem: \"halve\" is the verb (command) held as a dependent of \"size\" → the command is lost → L5 (\"its verb is held as a ... dependent ... and no clause has it as `verb`\") or L1 (\"A command needs its verb. When the first word ... can be a noun or a verb ... held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1\").\n\n\"halve\" — can it be a noun or a verb? \"halve\" is only a verb. So the L1 entry (about nouns-or-verbs) doesn't directly apply; L5 fits: a clause of the message is not written, its verb held as a dependent. Grade L5. Quote 'halve'.\n\nHmm, but \"halve\" could be seen as... no, it's a verb. L5.\n\nHmm, and \"teh\" misspelt \"the\" held as dependent — fine.\n\nn=80: \"Please, make a bar chart called Survey ratings with values 15, 15 for 1 star and Sample. Thanks!\"\nc1 imperative make complement n1; n1 a bar chart; c2 nonfinite called complement n2 (Survey ratings); r1 \"with\" n1→n3 (values); n4 = \"15 15 for 1 star\" (deps 15,15,for,1, head star!); k1 coordination \"and\" n4, n5 (Sample); c3 fragment discourse Thanks; r2 n1→c2; r3 n3→k1.\n\nHmm: n4 \"15, 15 for 1 star\" — head \"star\" with deps 15, 15, for, 1. \"for\" is a linking word held as a dependent → L3 (\"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\"). Yes! \"for\" should be a marker of a relation.\n\nAlso the numbers \"15, 15 for 1 star\" merged with \"1 star\" into one nominal, head \"star\". Clear L3: \"for\" is a preposition held as a dependent inside a nominal, and the values are merged with the star label.\n\nQuote: '15, 15 for 1 star' → note names \"for\" held as a dependent. L3.\n\nn=81: \"Thu two 120\"\nn1 \"Thu\"; n2 \"two\"; n3 \"120\"; c1 fragment complement n1; r1 n1→n2; r2 n1→n3.\n\nGarbled. \"Thu two 120\" — hmm, maybe \"Thu\" a name, \"two 120\"? All words placed and tied. Accept? Per the case \"A number or a name right after a noun ('block 2', 'x 120', 'the node Intro') may be a dependent in that noun's nominal, or a nominal of its own tied to the noun by a relation without a marker. Both pass.\" Here \"two\" and \"120\" are separate nominals tied to \"Thu\". Passes. L8.\n\nn=82: \"In the Budget table, change the cells to Item, Cost / Venue, 1500 / Catering, 900.\"\nc1 imperative change complement n2 (the cells); r1 \"In\" c1→n1 (the Budget table); r2 \"to\" c1→n3 (Item); n4 \"Cost Venue\" (dep Cost, head Venue); n5 \"1500 Catering\" (dep 1500, head Catering); n6 \"900\"; r3 n3→n4; r4 n4→n5; r5 n4→n6.\n\nThe table content: rows \"Item | Cost/Venue\" and \"1500 | Catering 900\"? Hmm, the message: 'change the cells to Item, Cost / Venue, 1500 / Catering, 900'. Content: \"Item, Cost\" / \"Venue, 1500\" / \"Catering, 900\" — the slashes probably separate rows.\n\nThe circuit: n3 Item, n4 = \"Cost Venue\", n5 = \"1500 Catering\", n6 = \"900\". So the slashes' grouping is lost: instead of pairs Item|Cost, Venue|1500, Catering|900, we get Item, then \"Cost Venue\", then \"1500 Catering\", then 900. Hmm. Is that wrong? Words are held: Item; Cost Venue (Cost dep of Venue); 1500 Catering (1500 dep of Catering); 900.\n\nChains: r3 n3→n4, r4 n4→n5, r5 n4→n6. So \"Cost Venue\" tied to \"1500 Catering\" and to \"900\".\n\nHmm, the layout info (the slashes) is punctuation — punctuation stands in no wire, and the rules say punctuation stands in no wire. So grouping by slash isn't required. But \"Cost Venue\" as one nominal with head Venue vs the cell values \"Cost\" then \"Venue\" — hmm.\n\nIs this acceptable? Words in a table row: Item, Cost, Venue, 1500, Catering, 900. The circuit merges Cost+Venue into one nominal (head Venue, dep Cost), and 1500+Catering into one nominal (head Catering, dep 1500). Merging two cells into one nominal → \"two nominals are merged\" (L3)? Hmm. Also, the case \"Two bare nouns side by side after a marker: either may be the head\" — \"Venue\" and \"1500\" are after the marker \"to\"? Not exactly.\n\nHmm, \"Item, Cost / Venue, 1500\" — the formalizer read \"Cost / Venue\" as adjacent bare nouns after Item. Under the case rule, two bare nouns side by side may form a nominal (\"with id neck\", \"type radar\"). Here they're separated only by a slash — plausible.\n\nI'd accept L8. Hmm. The information: the cells get values; the layout (rows/columns) is punctuation. Accept.\n\nn=83: \"Given that the lock was supposed to flatten the overlay, is there a rectangle still stuck in it regardless?\"\nc1 interrogative modifier \"Given\" verb \"is\" subject n3 (there) complement n4 (a rectangle); r1 \"that\" c1→c2 (declarative was supposed subject the lock); r2 \"to\" c2→c3 (nonfinite flatten complement the overlay); c4 nonfinite modifier still verb stuck modifier regardless; r3 \"in\" c4→n5 (it); r4 n4→c4.\n\n\"Given that ...\" — \"Given\" held as a modifier of c1 and the \"that\" clause tied to c1 via r1. Hmm, \"Given\" is a participle here (\"given that X\"). Held as a modifier of the main clause. Acceptable? Per the case, a participle describing/naming is the verb of a nonfinite clause; \"Given\" as a modifier of the clause — hmm, is \"Given\" a word with content in a modifier? Modifiers aren't judged for what they say (\"what a modifier says\"). So holding \"given\" as a modifier: it's a participle held as a modifier → L5? \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier\". Is \"Given that the lock was supposed...\" a clause of the message whose verb is \"given\"? Hmm — \"given that\" is a subordinator; the clause's verb is \"was supposed\". I'd say \"Given\" functions as a preposition/conjunction here. Holding it as a modifier of c1 is plausible.\n\nc1 subject n3 \"there\" — expletive as subject, as in n=58. Fine.\n\nAccept L8.\n\nn=84: \"tie Start, n 1, and support\"\nc1 imperative \"tie\" complement k1; n1 Start; n2 \"n\"; n3 \"1\"; k1 coordination \"and\" conjunct n1, n2, n4 (support); n4 support; r1 n2→n3.\n\nHmm: \"tie Start, n 1, and support\" — three things: \"Start\", \"n 1\", \"support\". The circuit: n2 \"n\" and n3 \"1\" as separate nominals related (r1), n1 Start, n4 support; k1 joins n1, n2, n4 as complements. Fine — \"n 1\" is a name; holding \"n\" as its own nominal tied to \"1\" is acceptable per the number/name rule.\n\nWait, but should \"tie\" take k1 as complement and all three. Yes. Accept L8.\n\nContinue reading the rest (85-150).",
