    "text": "n=97: \"a little bird up in the top right, then place the green block one space left and a quarter space up but never touching the blue block, no other change, so the spacing comes out even\"\nc1 fragment modifier up complement n1 (a little bird); r1 \"in\" c1→n2 (\"the top right\": deps the, top, head right); k1 coordination with conjunctions then, and, but, so and conjuncts c1, c2, c5, c4; c2 imperative place modifier left, up, complements n3 (green block), n4 (one space), n5 (a quarter space); c3 nonfinite \"never touching\" complement n6; n7 \"no other change\"; n8 the spacing; c4 declarative \"comes\" modifier out, modifier even, subject n8; r2 c2→c3; c5 fragment complement n7.\n\nWords: a little bird, up, in, the top right, then, place, the green block, one space, left, and, a quarter space, up, but, never, touching, the blue block, no other change, so, the spacing, comes, out, even.\n\nCircuit count \"up\": c1 modifier \"up\", c2 modifier \"up\" — two ups; text has \"a little bird up in\" (1) and \"a quarter space up\" (2). Good. \"and\": k1 conjunction \"and\" (1) — text \"one space left and a quarter space up\" (1). Good. \"but\": k1 (1). \"so\": k1 (1). \"then\": k1 (1) — text \"then place\" (1).\n\nStructure: c2's complements: n3 (green block), n4 (one space), n5 (a quarter space); modifiers left, up. Hmm — \"one space left\" and \"a quarter space up\": two measures; \"left\" and \"up\" as modifiers of c2. Per the \"n=5\" pattern (\"three spaces left\") acceptable: measures as nominals, directions as modifiers. Here two measures and two directions in one clause — the pairing again lost (like n=44). Hmm.\n\nThat's the same pattern as n=44, which I was leaning to reject. Consistency matters. In n=44: \"the red block one place left and the blue block one place right\" — two things, two measures, two directions, all in one fragment.\n\nHere n=97: \"place the green block one space left and a quarter space up\" — one thing, two measures (\"one space left\", \"a quarter space up\"), two directions merged as modifiers of c2. A next layer recovers \"place the green block, one space, a quarter space, left, up\" — the pairing of left↔one space is lost-ish but the order is preserved (measure1, left, measure2, up). Hmm, since modifiers and complements are unordered lists, the pairing is lost. Is that a \"wrong statement\"? Hmm. It says the block goes both left and up by both distances — a compound move which is actually what's meant (\"one space left and a quarter space up\" = diagonal move: left 1 and up 0.25). So actually merging is semantically OK! The move is both left and up. In n=44, the two things are different blocks (red left, blue right) — there merging applies both directions to both blocks — that IS wrong (red shouldn't go right).\n\nSo n=97 accept L8 (a single diagonal move), while n=44 reject (two different objects).\n\nLet me double check n=44 again: \"the red block one place left and the blue block one place right\" — the circuit: c1 fragment with modifiers left, right; complements k2 (red block, blue block), n2, n4 (one place each). So both directions apply to the same pair → the red block goes right? No: modifiers are on the clause whose complements are both blocks. That's a wrong statement for the red block (it goes left, not right). So L3 or L6. I'll keep rejecting n=44. Grade: I said L3 initially, then L6. Let me think again: \"A pairing the words give is not written\" — L6 is about a word tied to nothing. Here the information \"which block goes left\" is lost. Hmm, L3: \"Words stand in the wrong wire\" — the words 'right' and 'one place' of the blue block's instruction stand in the clause of the red block's instruction. That fits L3: words of one written clause in another... which is L1's phrasing.\n\nHmm! L1: \"Words of one written clause are held in another.\" The first part is one written part \"the red block one place left\" and the second \"the blue block one place right\". Two parts; the circuit writes one clause c1 holding words from both. So the second part's words ('right', 'one place') are held in the first party clause → L1.\n\nHmm, but are these \"written clauses\"? They have no verbs → fragments. c1 is a fragment clause; the second part's fragment clause is not written at all.\n\nUgh. I'll go with L3 (words in the wrong wire): the note can be: \"'right' and the second 'one place' stand in the same fragment as 'left', so the red block is said to move right as well\" — names the wrong statement.\n\nHmm, actually a cleaner L3 framing: \"a tie is wrong\" — r? The directions are modifiers, not relations. OK, L3.\n\nn=98: \"a bench goes in the middle of the scene, and a tree set on every side of it\"\nc1 declarative goes subject n1 (a bench); r1 \"in\" c1→n2; r2 \"of\" n2→n3; k1 coordination \"and\" c1, c2; n4 a tree; c2 nonfinite \"set\" subject n4; r3 \"on\" c2→n5 (every side); r4 \"of\" n5→n6 (it).\n\nAcceptable? c2 nonfinite \"set\" with subject n4 (\"a tree set on every side of it\"). Fine. Accept L8.\n\nn=99: \"How about a column chart of claims by region for 2024 and then a parallel chart for 2023 on a shared y axis and then give the first chart the title 'Claims by region, two years'\"\nc1 fragment modifier \"How\"; r1 \"about\" c1→k2 (coordination n1 a column chart, n5 \"then a parallel chart\"); r2 \"of\" n1→n2 (claims); r3 \"by\" n2→n3 (region); r4 \"for\" n1→n4 (2024); k1 coordination with conjunctions and, and; conjuncts c1, c2; n5 \"then a parallel chart\" — \"then\" as a DEPENDENT of \"chart\"! → L3 per the settled case (word of order held as dependent of a nominal).\n\nHmm: n5 syntax.nominal dependent=\"then\" dependent=\"a\" dependent=\"parallel\" head=\"chart\". \"then\" as a dependent of \"chart\" → the thing becomes \"the then parallel chart\". Per the settled case: \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun from where it stands ... held as a dependent, it fails: L3.\"\n\nAlso c2 has modifier \"then\" too (c2 modifiers: then). Text has \"then\" twice (\"and then a parallel chart\", \"and then give the first chart\") — so one \"then\" in n5 as dependent, one as c2's modifier. The one in n5 is misplaced.\n\nSo n=99 → L3, quoting 'then a parallel chart'.\n\nn=100: \"make the avatar a guy, human\"\nc1 imperative make complements n1 (the avatar), n2 (a guy); n3 \"human\"; r1 n2→n3.\n\nFine. \"human\" tied to \"a guy\". Accept L8.\n\nn=101: \"just undo the latest, whatever it was\"\nc1 imperative modifier just, verb undo, complement n1 (the latest); r1 \"whatever\" n1→c2 (declarative was subject it). Fine. \"whatever it was\" — a clause tied to \"the latest\". Marker \"whatever\" — hmm, is \"whatever\" a linking word? It's a relative word; using it as a marker is plausible. Accept L8.\n\nn=102: \"could you place a tree twice as tall as the boy — the way the drawing looks so far — exactly where the picture needs it\"\nc1 interrogative could place modifiers twice, as, tall, exactly, subject you, complement n2 (a tree); r1 \"as\" c1→n3 (the boy); n4 \"the way\"; n5 the drawing; c2 declarative looks, modifier so, modifier far, subject n5; r2 \"where\" c1→c3 (needs); r3 n2→n4; r4 n4→c2.\n\nHmm: \"the way the drawing looks so far\" — n4 \"the way\" tied from n2 (tree) and to c2. Fine (as n=20).\n\nAccept L8.\n\nn=103: \"when the regions dial in tomorrow the first chart they see is Sales by month, so for their sake give the bars a gradient fill before the meeting starts — while you're at it, show both grid lines\"\nc1 declarative dial in tomorrow subject the regions; r1 \"when\" from c3 to c1; n2 \"the first chart\"; n3 they; c2 declarative see subject they; c3 declarative is subject n2 complement n4 (Sales); r2 \"by\" n4→n5 (month); k1 coordination \"so\" conjuncts c3, c4, c7; r3 \"for\" c4→n6 (their sake); c4 imperative give complements n7 (the bars), n8 (a gradient fill); r4 \"before\" c4→c5 (starts); r5 \"while\" c7→c6; c6 declarative 're; r6 \"at\" c6→n11; c7 imperative show complement n12; r7 n2→c2.\n\nHmm: k1 \"so\" joins c3 (the declarative \"the first chart ... is Sales by month\"), c4 (give), c7 (show). Settled case: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an `imperative` or `interrogative` clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3.\"\n\nBut that case is about a clause that ENDS a relation with a marker (\"so it sits under the table\") standing inside the clause where the relation starts. Here c3 ends relation r1 marked \"when\" (from c3 to c1) — so c3 is inside c1's clause? Hmm, r1 \"when\" starts at c3 and ends at c1 (c3 is the relation's start, ending at the clause c1). Hmm, the case says \"A clause that ends a relation with a marker ('so it sits under the table') stands inside the clause where the relation starts.\" Here r1 has marker \"when\", from c3 to c1: the relation starts at c3 and ends at c1. So c1 (the \"dial in\" clause) ends the relation, and thus stands inside c3. So the coordination k1 that joins c3 with the imperative c4/c7 — does it join an imperative with \"such a clause\"? The case's failing pattern: joining an imperative with a clause that ends a relation with a marker (because that clause is inside another clause). Here c3 is the relation's START (not the end). And c1 is the end (inside c3). k1 joins c3, c4, c7 — c1 isn't a conjunct. So this seems OK.\n\nHmm, but also the \"when\" relation: r1 from c3 to c1. Is that right? \"when the regions dial in tomorrow the first chart they see is Sales by month\" — the \"when\" clause is c1 (dial in) and the main clause c3 (is Sales by month). The relation marked \"when\" should start at c3 (the main clause) and end at c1 (the when-clause)? The rule for \"the to of an infinitive\" etc.: relations go from the clause to the marker's object. For \"so it sits under the table\", marker \"so\" from the main clause to the sub clause. For \"when X, Y\" — the marker \"when\" is at the start of the subordinate clause; the relation should start at the main clause Y (c3) and end at the subordinate c1. The circuit has it reversed: from c3 to c1 — wait, that IS from c3 (main) to c1 (subordinate). Yes: \"from=$c3 to=$c1\" — correct!\n\nHmm, but hold on: \"when the regions dial in tomorrow\" — the words order: the subordinate clause comes first. r1 from c3 to c1 = from the main clause to the subordinate. That matches \"so it sits under the table\" (from main to sub). Good.\n\nSo all fine? Let me check \"for their sake\" — r3 \"for\" from c4 to n6. Fine. \"while you're at it\" — r5 from c7 to c6 (settled case pattern). Good.\n\nAccept n=103 L8.\n\nn=104: \"add a chart with source holdings and type bar and title Quarterly plan and labels North, South and East and values 3, 5 and 8\"\nc1 add complement n1 (a chart); r1 \"with\" n1→k1 (coordination with 2 \"and\"s: conjuncts n2 source holdings, n3 type bar, n4 title); n5 Quarterly plan; k2 coordination with 2 \"and\"s: conjuncts n5, n6 labels, n10 values; k3 (North, South, East); n10 values; k4 (3,5,8); r2 n3→k2; r3 n6→k3; r4 n10→k4.\n\nHmm, r2 from n3 (type bar) to k2 (Quarterly plan, labels, values) — so \"type bar\" is tied to the title/labels/values coordination. Hmm, that's the chain approach: the last property name ties to subsequent values. r2 should be from n4 (title) to k2. Using n3 instead... Hmm, chain: k1 = [source holdings, type bar, title]; then title's value chain from... The formalizer tied k2 from n3 (\"type bar\") rather than n4 (\"title\"). That's a bit off but similar to n=86 (where r2 went from n3 title to k2).\n\nIs that a wrong statement? \"type bar\" tied to [Quarterly plan, labels, values] — hmm, meaning the type's values include the title etc. Everything is tied, order preserved. Plausible-ish chain. Accept? Hmm.\n\nGiven this is one of a family (n=30, 86, 104) and the family is a property list, I'd accept these as plausible. Hmm, but is the \"and\" placement in k1/k2 consistent? \"source holdings and type bar and title Quarterly plan and labels ...\": the properties are source, type, title, labels, values. k1 has [source holdings, type bar, title] — correct! k2 has [Quarterly plan, labels, values] — joining the title's value with the following property names. Hmm, so \"labels\" and \"values\" are conjuncts with \"Quarterly plan\" rather than properties. Then r3 n6 (labels)→k3 (names), r4 n10 (values)→k4 (numbers). So each property name IS present and tied to its values; the only oddity is they're conjuncts of one coordination with \"Quarterly plan\".\n\nCompare n=30: k1 = [chart Sales, values, label Review] — clean; k2 = [3,5,8]. Accepted.\n\nFor n=104 the \"labels\" and \"values\" are inside k2 with Quarterly plan — a wrong coordination? \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\" (L3). The \"and\" before \"labels\" joins the property \"title Quarterly plan\" with \"labels ...\"; the circuit's k2 joins \"Quarterly plan\" with \"labels\". Hmm, that's a coordination with wrong conjuncts arguably.\n\nHmm, and n=86 had the same pattern (k2 = [Quarterly plan, labels]). I was accepting n=86. Ugh, consistency.\n\nDecision: Both n=86 and n=104 have this chain pattern. Options: accept both or reject both. Which is right? Hmm.\n\nThink about what the list means: \"add a chart with source holdings and type bar and title Quarterly plan and labels North, South and East and values 3, 5 and 8\". Under the \"with\" relation: five properties. The circuit's k1 correctly groups three of them. k2 groups \"Quarterly plan\" + \"labels\" + \"values\" — this says these three are one coordinated thing. Then r2 ties \"type bar\" to that coordination; r3 ties labels to names; r4 ties values to numbers.\n\nThe next layer can read: chart with source holdings, type bar, title, Quarterly plan, labels, North, South, East, values, 3, 5, 8 — the structure is loose but recoverable? The tie from \"type bar\" to k2 is wrong-ish (title should own Quarterly plan).\n\nHmm, honestly this is \"plausible reading, continuable\". Since the rules say reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information, and here no word is lost, and the odd grouping is a wiring choice... Hmm, but \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\" is a specific L3 item, and the wrong conjunct here is \"labels\" (a property name joined as a value of the title).\n\nI need to make a decision. I'll accept both n=86 and n=104 as loose but recoverable? Hmm.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: the more natural parse, if the formalizer had done it well: r1 with n1→coordination of five properties [source holdings, type bar, title Quarterly plan, labels ..., values ...]. Here it split. But the \"and\" chain in the message without commas is genuinely ambiguous; the formalizer's chaining is a plausible greedy reading. Yes — since the message has no commas to delimit properties, a chain reading is defensible: \"and\" joins successive items; where pairs appear (title Quarterly plan, labels North...), the grouping could go either way.\n\nHmm, but \"labels\" as a conjunct of \"Quarterly plan\" breaks the pairing title↔Quarterly plan... The case list says: \"Which earlier wire a coordination joins, when grammar allows several\" — passes. So the coordination's placement is tolerated when grammar allows several. I'll accept both. L8.\n\nn=105: \"add Planning on 2026-11-18 at 10:00 UTC, 30 minutes, ID planning\"\nc1 imperative add complement n1 (Planning); r1 \"on\" n1→n2 (2026-11-18); r2 \"at\" ambiguous c1→n3 (10:00 UTC); n4 \"30 minutes\"; n5 \"ID planning\"; k1 coordination conjunct c1, c2; r3 n1→n4; c2 fragment complement n5.\n\nFine. Per the settled case about a day/time/length set off by commas after a phrase with a marker: belongs to the clause: a relation from the clause with no marker passes, and so does a coordination with the time before it. Here \"30 minutes\" is tied from n1 (the thing added) via r3 — hmm, the case says the time belongs to the clause. Being tied from n1 — the case's allowance: \"a relation from the clause with no marker passes, and so does a coordination with the time before it\" — tied from n1 instead. Hmm, is that \"a tie that is wrong\" (L3, a nominal tied to the wrong thing)? Hmm. The \"30 minutes\" is the duration of the meeting, tied to the meeting nominally — recoverable.\n\nThen \"ID planning\" as a fragment coordinated with c1 — fine.\n\nAccept L8.\n\nn=106: \"hey — I always want the arrows dashed, so set the existing ones now\"\nc1 declarative discourse hey, modifier always, verb want, subject n1 (I); n2 the arrows; c2 nonfinite \"dashed\" subject n2; k1 coordination \"so\" c1, c3; c3 imperative set modifier now complement n3 (the existing ones); r1 c1→c2.\n\nFine: \"I want the arrows dashed\" — c2 nonfinite tied to c1. \"so set...\" k1 joins c1 and c3 with \"so\". Accept L8.\n\nn=107: \"Let's do a bar chart of monthly average claims across 2024, by region, restricted to policies that stayed active all year, the top six regions, ties kept, sorted from high to low, with the y axis starting at zero, make the bars blue\"\nc1 imperative Let complement n1 (\"'s\"); c2 nonfinite do complement n2 (a bar chart); r1 \"of\" n2→n3 (monthly average claims); r2 \"across\" n3→n4 (2024); r3 \"by\" n3→n5 (region); c3 nonfinite restricted; r4 \"to\" c3→n6 (policies); r5 \"that\" n6→c4 (declarative stayed, modifier active, complement n7 all year); n8 \"the top six regions\"; n9 ties; c5 nonfinite kept subject n9; c6 nonfinite sorted; r6 \"from\" c6→n10 (high); r7 \"to\" c6→n11 (low); r8 \"with\" n8→c7 (nonfinite starting subject the y axis); r9 \"at\" c7→n13 (zero); c8 declarative verb make, modifier blue, subject n8 (the top six regions), complement n14 (the bars); r10 c1→c2; r11 c1→c8; r12 n2→c3; r13 n8→c5; r14 n8→c6.\n\nBig issue: c8 = \"make the bars blue\" — a command, written as `declarative` with subject n8 (\"the top six regions\")! That's a wrong subject + wrong form. That's L1 (\"Its form is wrong: ... the reverse\" — a command written as declarative; and a subject the words do not show → L4). Also the subject \"the top six regions\" is wrong (\"make the bars blue\" has no subject).\n\nWhich grade? L1 first: form wrong — a clear command written declarative. Hmm, L1's form list: \"a clear question written as `imperative` or `declarative`, or the reverse\". A command written as declarative = \"the reverse\"? Hmm, ambiguous. But L4: \"a `subject` the words do not show\" — c8's subject \"the top six regions\" is a subject the words do not show! That's clearly L4.\n\nGrade choice: L1 or L4? For \"make the bars blue\", the words show no subject. The circuit's declarative form + subject n8. Hmm, is form wrong? A command written as a declarative — L1 says form wrong: \"a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\". Reading \"the reverse\" = \"a clear imperative or declarative written as interrogative\"? The phrase is symmetric: question↔(imperative|declarative). So a command written declarative = L1 too (\"its form is wrong ... a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\" — the reverse of \"question written as declarative\" is \"declarative written as question\").\n\nHmm, so a command written as declarative isn't clearly L1. The settled case: \"'Have the legend switched on.' ... Written as an `interrogative` clause with 'Have' as `auxiliary`, it fails: L1.\" So a command written as interrogative = L1. By analogy, a command written as declarative = L1 too? Hmm, \"interrogative\" ↔ \"imperative\" reversal is what the case shows.\n\nI'd say the cleanest for n=107: L4, \"a `subject` the words do not show\" — 'make the bars blue' has no subject, but the circuit makes 'the top six regions' its subject. That's a wrong statement (states the top six regions make the bars blue). Hmm, but \"Its form is wrong\" comes first if it applies... I'd rather use L3? \"The `subject` is wrong\" — L3 says: \"The `subject` is wrong, or subject and complement are swapped.\" Hmm! For a clause that has a subject in the message, a wrong subject = L3. For a clause with no subject at all, L4 (\"a subject the words do not show\").\n\nHmm, L3 vs L4 for a made-up subject... L4 explicitly: \"a `subject` the words do not show\". Here the words don't show any subject for \"make the bars blue\" → L4.\n\nBut is the form issue L1? I'd say the primary defect: \"make the bars blue\" is a command, and the circuit writes a declarative clause with an invented subject. I'll grade L1 (a command written as declarative) — hmm.\n\nLet me think about which is *less* likely to be wrong. Ugh.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: is c8's form actually the problem? Actually the bigger deal: \"make the bars blue\" is a second instruction to the addressee; writing it as declarative with subject \"the top six regions\" is clearly wrong. A judge writing a note would say: \"'make the bars blue' is a command with no subject, but it is written as a declarative clause with 'the top six regions' as its subject\" and grade... L1 (form) or L4 (subject). \n\nThe rules order: L1 first. Does L1 apply? \"A clause that the circuit writes is wrong. Its `form` is wrong: a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse; a clause with a verb written as `fragment`.\" The list is specific: questions and fragments. A command→declarative isn't in it (though \"or the reverse\" of \"question written as declarative\" = \"declarative written as question\"). So L1 doesn't clearly apply. Then L4 (invented subject) applies. Hmm, but L3 \"The `subject` is wrong\" also.\n\nI'll go L4: \"a subject the words do not show\" — exactly the case. Note quotes 'make the bars blue'.\n\nHmm, but hmm — also consider whether \"the top six regions, ties kept, sorted from high to low, with the y axis starting at zero, make the bars blue\" — those are all tied to n8. Fine.\n\nL4 for n=107.\n\nn=108: \"I want the Draft box split into two boxes side by side, with the incoming arrow on the left one and the outgoing arrow on the right\"\nc1 declarative want subject I; n2 the Draft box; c2 nonfinite split subject n2; r1 \"into\" c2→n3 (two boxes); n4 side; r2 \"by\" n4→n5 (side); r3 \"with\" n3→k1 ([the incoming arrow, the outgoing arrow]); r4 \"on\" n6→n7 (the left one); k1 (n6, n8); r5 \"on\" n8→n9 (the right); r6 c1→c2; r7 n3→n4.\n\nHmm: \"side by side\" — n4/n5 as nominals with relation \"by\" between them; also r7 ties n3 (two boxes) to n4 (side). Per the case: \"A fixed phrase of time or manner ('from now on', 'after that', 'for now', 'side by side', 'at once') may be held whole as a modifier.\" Held as two nominals with a \"by\" relation instead — hmm, is that a wrong statement? \"side by side\" as two nominals \"side\" and \"side\" with a relation marked \"by\" — reads \"side by side\". Reasonable. Acceptable? I think so. Also c1 \"want\" has no complement; c2 tied via r6 — fine.\n\nAccept L8.\n\nContinue 109-120.",
