    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "n=109: JSON dataset in the message. c1 imperative create complement n1 (a doughnut chart); c2 nonfinite called complement n2 (Sales); n3 \"7\" tied r4 n2→n3; r1 \"for\" n2→k1 (Jan, Feb); r2 \"with\" n1→n6 (datasets); n7 = \"label North data\" (deps label, North, head data); n8 7; n9 9; n10 \"label\"; n11 South; n12 data; n13 8; n14 10; r3 n1→c2; r5 n6→n7; r6 n7→n8; r7 n7→n10; r8 n8→n9; r9 n10→n11; r10 n10→n12; r11 n12→n13; r12 n13→n14.\n\nJSON structure: [{\"label\": \"North\", \"data\": [7,9]}, {\"label\": \"South\", \"data\": [8,10]}]. The circuit: n7 \"label North data\" merges label and data into one nominal with head \"data\"?? Hmm, \"label\", \"North\", \"data\" as deps with head \"data\". Merging \"label North\" and \"data\" into one nominal — the pairing label↔North works (r7 n7→n10 = label→South, hmm).\n\nChain: n7 (label North data) → n8 (7) → n9 (9); n7 → n10 (label) → n11 (South), n10→n12 (data) → n13 (8) → n14 (10).\n\nMessy but all tokens present, chained in order. The JSON keys/values are held as nominals and tied in order. Plausible-garbled handling. Honestly, a next layer can read the sequence. I'd accept L8? Hmm.\n\nThe one nominal n7 = \"label North data\" with head \"data\" merges three words from different JSON slots. Would this be \"two nominals merged\" L3? \"label\" and \"North\" are separate JSON tokens; \"data\" is a key. Hmm.\n\nI'd accept — the content is a data blob; the circuit keeps order and ties. Accept L8.\n\nn=110: \"while you're there, I want the marker interval 3 on the revenue trend and I'd like the series name Trajectory on the revenue trend\"\nc1 declarative \"'re\" modifier there subject you; c2 declarative want subject I complement n3 (the marker interval); n4 \"3\" tied r4 n3→n4; r2 \"on\" n3→n5 (revenue trend); k1 coordination \"and\" c2, c3; c3 declarative 'd like subject I complement n7 (the series name); n8 Trajectory tied r5 n7→n8; r3 \"on\" c3→n9.\n\nGood. Accept L8.\n\nn=111: \"change the backdrop to a garden full of roses\"\nc1 imperative change complement n1; r1 \"to\" c1→n2 (\"a garden full\": deps a, full, head garden); r2 \"of\" n2→n3 (roses).\n\n\"full\" as dependent of \"garden\" — fine. Accept L8.\n\nn=112: \"half the stand-up was grumbling that the numbers were impossible to read from the corridor screen; to settle it, I'd like the numbers back on the right on the sales chart — while you're at it, how about top numbers in tens? going forward, also make the font a bit bigger\"\nc5 = \"I'd like the numbers back on the right on the sales chart\" written as **interrogative** with auxiliary 'd, modifier back, subject I, complement the numbers! That's a declarative statement written as interrogative → L1 (the reverse: a declarative written as interrogative).\n\nYes: \"I'd like the numbers back on the right on the sales chart\" is not a question. The circuit form=interrogative → L1.\n\nAlso c7 fragment modifier \"how\" with ellipsis=$c5 — ellipsis claiming it shares \"like\"? \"how about top numbers in tens?\" — hmm.\n\nGrade L1, quote \"I'd like the numbers back on the right\".\n\nn=113: \"No, make the avatar shaven after all, because the beard still shows.\"\nc1 imperative discourse No, verb make, modifier shaven, modifier after, modifier all, complement n1; r1 \"because\" c1→c2; c2 declarative still shows subject n2.\n\n\"after all\" as two modifiers — fine (fixed phrase; as separate modifier words also fine). Accept L8.\n\nn=114: \"mirror the boat top to bottom but not left to right, so the bow still points at the harbour\"\nc1 imperative mirror complements n1 (the boat), n2 (top); r1 \"to\" n2→n3 (bottom); k1 coordination \"but\" c1, c2; c2 fragment modifier \"not\", modifier \"left\", ellipsis=c1; r2 \"to\" c2→n4 (right); r3 \"so\" c1→c3; c3 declarative points subject the bow; r4 \"at\" c3→n6.\n\nHmm: \"not left to right\" — c2 fragment with modifiers \"not\" and \"left\", ellipsis c1. Reasonable.\n\nAccept L8.\n\nn=115: \"for the monthly ops review on Friday I need the revenue trend legible from the back of the conference room, so make the reference line dashed, grey and horizontal before we circulate it wider and then make the font a bit bigger\"\nc1 declarative need modifier legible subject I complement the revenue trend; r1 \"for\" c1→n1; r2 \"on\" n1→n2; r3 \"from\" c1→n5; r4 \"of\" n5→n6; c2 imperative make modifiers dashed, grey, discourse \"and\", modifier horizontal, complement the reference line; k1 coordination with conjunctions so, and: conjuncts c1, c2, c4; r5 \"before\" c2→c3 (circulate); c3 declarative circulate modifier wider subject we complement it; c4 imperative then make modifier bigger complements the font, a bit.\n\nHere c1 is declarative — correct (the n=67 counterpart had interrogative). Good.\n\n\"discourse and\" — the \"and\" between \"grey\" and \"horizontal\" (two modifiers) → discourse allowed per case. Good.\n\nAccept L8.\n\nn=116: \"one pie chart, the wedges rides 30%, repairs 25%, spares 45%\"\nc1 declarative verb \"rides\" subject n2 (the wedges) complement n3 (\"30%\"); c2 declarative verb \"repairs\" complement n4 (\"25%\"); c3 declarative verb \"spares\" complement n5 (\"45%\"); c4 fragment complement n1 (one pie chart); k1 coordination c4, c1, c2, c3.\n\n\"repairs\" and \"spares\" held as verbs of declarative clauses — they're labels (nouns) in a list: wedges 30%, repairs 25%, spares 45%. The circuit makes \"repairs\" and \"spares\" verbs of declarative clauses → \"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here\" → L1? Hmm. \"repairs\" can be a verb (\"he repairs\") and a noun. Grammar allows both in isolation. But the message: \"one pie chart, the wedges rides 30%, repairs 25%, spares 45%\" — a list of label/value pairs. Reading \"repairs 25%\" as \"repairs [conj] 25%\" — the verb reading yields \"repairs 25%\" with no subject. Hmm, \"25%\" can't be an object of \"repairs\" sensibly... but grammar-wise \"repairs 25%\" is a verb + complement.\n\nC1: \"the wedges rides 30%\" — subject \"the wedges\", verb \"rides\". Hmm, that's how \"the wedges rides 30%\" reads literally... Actually the intended: the wedges ride 30% (a typo of \"rides\"). So c1 is fine.\n\nc2 \"repairs 25%\" as a declarative with verb repairs and complement 25% — no subject. Grammatically possible as an elliptical clause (\"repairs 25%\" = \"[it] repairs 25%\"), but the natural reading is a label \"repairs\" with value 25%.\n\nIs this a wrong statement? \"repairs\" as a verb states an action; the message lists categories. Per the settled case pattern for label/value lists (like n=116 and n=120: \"add a cat 260 Take 2\" where \"Take\" was made a verb), hmm.\n\nC2 and c3 have no subject and their \"verbs\" are nouns (repairs, spares). I'd say L1: \"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here\" — \"repairs\" here is the name of a slice (like \"spares\"). Hmm, but \"grammar allows a word as noun or verb\" is a pass criterion (\"A word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb: 'make y axis scale linear'\"). Hmm! That criterion exists to protect exactly this kind of ambiguity.\n\nHmm. Then, is a coordination joining c4 (fragment \"one pie chart\") with c1, c2, c3 fine? And the nouns-as-verbs pass.\n\nUgh. The pass rule \"A word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb\" — \"repairs\" in \"repairs 25%\" — as a verb phrase \"repairs 25%\" needs a subject; in a coordination with \"the wedges rides 30%\", the subject could be shared: \"the wedges rides 30%, repairs 25%, spares 45%\" — subject sharing? Not written for c2/c3 though (they have no subject). Hmm.\n\nOK: for n=116, the reading \"the wedges rides 30%, repairs 25%, spares 45%\" with c2/c3 as declaratives lacking subjects → the labels are verbs. I think this is a rejection: the three labels \"wedges\", \"repairs\", \"spares\" are slice names each with a percentage; making \"repairs\" and \"spares\" verbs loses the labels as items and states actions. But the pass rule about noun/verb ambiguity...\n\nLet me compare with n=120 \"add a cat 260 Take 2\": \"Take\" as a verb of an imperative clause with complements \"260\" and \"2\" — \"Take\" is capitalised (a name?). Hmm.\n\nI'll decide: for n=116, accept? Hmm. Think about what's lost: the pairing \"repairs 25%\" is preserved (verb repairs, complement 25%). The word \"repairs\" is there. The next layer sees \"repairs\" as a verb and can reinterpret. Nothing is lost except the label reading. Hmm, it's a plausible reading.\n\nBut the \"form\" declarative for \"repairs 25%\" — a fragment-ish part written as a declarative clause states something the message doesn't say (that something repairs 25%). Hmm.\n\nGiven the explicit pass rule for noun/verb words, I'll accept n=116 as L8. Hmm... but \"the wedges rides 30%\" — c1 fine.\n\nHmm, honestly, I think the reference judge likely rejects this one, since \"repairs 25%\" and \"spares 45%\" as declarative clauses is a stretch with no subject. But the rules' pass list protects noun-or-verb words. I'll accept — being lenient per the instructions (\"reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information\").\n\nHmm, wait. L4? \"a `subject` the words do not show\" — c1 has subject \"the wedges\"; c2/c3 have no subject written (they don't claim a subject either, so no L4). OK accept.\n\nn=117: \"Could you what values does Lunch orders show?. Thanks!\"\nc1 interrogative verb \"Could\" subject you (no verb after Could!); n2 \"what values\"; c2 interrogative auxiliary does verb show subject n3 (Lunch orders); c3 fragment discourse Thanks; k1 coordination c1, c2.\n\nHmm: c1 \"Could\" as the VERB of an interrogative clause with subject \"you\" — \"Could\" is an auxiliary; L1: \"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here, or an auxiliary while the main verb stands elsewhere.\" Here \"Could\" is an auxiliary and the main verb \"show\" stands elsewhere (in c2). So L1!\n\nYes: \"Could\" held as the verb while \"show\" is the main verb → L1. Quote 'Could you what values'.\n\nHmm, but the message is garbled (\"Could you what values does Lunch orders show?\" — two templates). Still, \"Could\" as verb = auxiliary as verb with the main verb elsewhere → L1. Good.\n\nNote the settled case: \"Could you move it?\" is one interrogative clause: \"Could\" is an auxiliary. Here \"Could\" is written as verb → L1.\n\nn=118: \"please to insert a picture of the old culvert and move it to the right corner\"\nc1 fragment discourse please; r1 \"to\" c1→k1; c2 nonfinite insert complement n1; n1 a picture; r2 \"of\" n1→n2; k1 coordination \"and\" c2, c3; c3 nonfinite move complement n3 (it); r3 \"to\" c3→n4.\n\nHmm. \"please to insert ...\" — \"to insert\" infinitive; \"and move it\" — the second verb is finite-imperative \"move\" (a command). The circuit makes both nonfinite clauses coordinated under the \"to\" relation from a fragment clause \"please\". Per the settled case (\"the 'to' of an infinitive is the marker of the relation that ends at the nonfinite clause; when nothing stands before it, the relation starts at the main clause\").\n\nIs \"move\" as nonfinite wrong? \"insert ... and move it to the right corner\" — \"move\" is coordinated with \"insert\", sharing \"to\" — so nonfinite is fine (\"to insert ... and [to] move it\"). OK.\n\nSo the structure: c1 fragment \"please\" with the relation to the coordination of two nonfinite clauses. Plausible. Accept L8? Hmm, \"please to insert\" is odd but the formalizer handled it.\n\nAccept L8.\n\nn=119: \"set the bend of arrow from Start to Review to 26 and the x to 120 and the y to 80\"\nc1 imperative set complement n1 (the bend); r1 \"of\" n1→n2 (arrow); r2 \"from\" n1→n3 (Start); r3 \"to\" n3→n4 (Review); r4 \"to\" ambiguous c1→n5 (26); k1 coordination with 2 ands: conjuncts c1, c2; n6 the x; r5 \"to\" n6→n7 (120); n8 the y; r6 \"to\" n8→n9 (80); c2 fragment complement k2 (n6, n8) ellipsis=c1.\n\nHmm: \"from Start to Review\" — r2 \"from\" from n1 (the bend) to Start; r3 \"to\" from Start to Review. Reads \"the bend of arrow from Start to Review\". OK-ish.\n\nAccept L8.\n\nn=120: \"add a cat 260 Take 2\"\nc1 imperative add complement n1 (a cat); n2 \"260\"; c2 imperative verb \"Take\" complement n2 (260), complement n3 (2); k1 coordination c1, c2.\n\nHmm: \"add a cat 260 Take 2\" — garbled. \"Take\" as a verb in an imperative clause with complements \"260\" and \"2\". n2 \"260\" is held as a complement of c2 (Take) rather than tied to \"cat\". Hmm.\n\nIs this wrong? \"add a cat 260 Take 2\" — likely \"add a cat 260\" + \"Take 2\"?? Honestly garbled (\"Take 2\" = a retake marker). The circuit holds \"260\" with \"Take\" — and k1 coordinates the two imperative clauses.\n\nPer the number-after-noun rule, \"260\" could be a dependent of \"cat\" or a nominal tied to it. Here it's a complement of c2 \"Take\". Hmm, that's a merge: \"260\" is separated from \"cat\".\n\nIs there a wrong statement? \"Take 260 2\" — reading: c2 \"Take\" takes complements 260 and 2, and the coordination with \"add a cat 260\"? Hmm, c2's complement includes n2 \"260\" which is the cat's number. Hmm.\n\nHmm, \"Take\" as a verb of an imperative clause: plausible (\"Take 2\"). \"260\" tied to it: hmm.\n\nIs a rejection warranted? The number \"260\" right after \"cat\" is now in the next clause, which means \"add a cat\" loses its number and \"Take\" gets it. Per the case: \"A number or a name right after a noun ('block 2', 'x 120', 'the node Intro') may be a dependent in that noun's nominal, or a nominal of its own tied to the noun by a relation without a marker. Both pass. Left tied to nothing, it is L6.\" Here the number is tied to \"Take\" — a wrong end? The relation r? there's no relation; n2 is a complement of c2. Hmm — \"A nominal is the complement of the wrong clause\" (L3)? \"260\" is the complement of \"Take\" instead of belonging to \"cat\".\n\nHmm, but is \"260\" the cat's number? \"add a cat 260 Take 2\" — could be \"add a cat 260\" (a cat at 260) then \"Take 2\" as a name. Yeah, plausible.\n\nGrade: L3 (\"A nominal is the complement of the wrong clause\"). I'd reject. Hmm. Or maybe accept as garbled.\n\nLet me think about how wrong it is: the message's words: add a cat 260 Take 2. The circuit reads: imperative \"add a cat\" + imperative \"Take 260 2\". The \"Take 2\" is a separate item and \"260\" belongs with the cat. So c2's complement \"260\" is wrong. But is \"260\" the cat's? Ambiguous — \"260\" after \"cat\" per the case belongs to the cat.\n\nGiven the case explicitly says a number right after a noun belongs to that noun's group, taking it into the next clause is a wrong tie. L3. Hmm, but the pass rule \"either may be a dependent in that noun's nominal or a nominal of its own tied to the noun\" — both require tying to THE NOUN. Here it's tied to another clause's verb → fails.\n\nI'll reject n=120 as L3: \"'260' stands right after 'cat' but is held as a complement of the clause of 'Take'\".\n\nNow 121-150.",
