    "text": "n=121: \"draw the diagram, then check the profit area is above zero\"\nc1 imperative draw complement the diagram; k1 coordination \"then\" c1, c2 (imperative check); c2 imperative check (no complement); c3 declarative is subject the profit area; r1 \"above\" c3→zero; r2 c2→c3.\n\nGood. \"check the profit area is above zero\" — c3 tied to c2. Accept L8.\n\nn=122: \"would you make that one look nicer\"\nc1 interrogative would make subject you (no complement); n2 \"that one\"; c2 nonfinite look modifier nicer subject n2; r1 c1→c2.\n\nGood: \"make that one look nicer\" — c2 nonfinite \"look\" with subject \"that one\", tied to c1. Accept L8.\n\nn=123: \"slide block 15 under block 2, then slide block 15 left to block 14\"\nc1 imperative slide complement n1 (block); n2 \"15\" tied; r1 \"under\" n1→n3 (block); n4 \"2\" tied; k1 coordination \"then\" c1, c3; n5 = \"slide block\" (dep slide(!), head block); n6 15; c2 nonfinite verb \"left\"(!); r2 \"to\" n5→n7 (block); n8 \"14\"; c3 fragment complement n5 ellipsis=c1; r5 n5→c2; r6 n5→n6; r7 n7→n8.\n\nProblem: the second \"slide\" is held as a DEPENDENT of \"block\" (n5 deps slide, head block), so the second imperative \"slide block 15 left to block 14\" is written as a fragment with ellipsis=c1. Hmm — is that OK? The second sentence's verb \"slide\" is repeated in the message: \"then slide block 15 left to block 14\". The circuit holds \"slide\" as a dependent of \"block\" and makes c3 a fragment with ellipsis=c1 (sharing the first \"slide\")! That's a wrong statement: the word \"slide\" is not a dependent of \"block\"; it's the verb of the second clause. Since the fragment's ellipsis covers it, the second \"slide\" word is redundant/misplaced → L3 (words in the wrong wire) — the word \"slide\" (second instance) is held as a dependent of \"block\" rather than as the clause's verb.\n\nAlso c2 nonfinite \"left\" as verb — \"left\" is a direction word, held as the verb of a nonfinite clause?! \"slide block 15 left to block 14\" — \"left\" should be a modifier. Making \"left\" the verb of a nonfinite clause → L1 (\"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here\").\n\nHmm, two flaws. Grade L1 comes first? \"left\" as verb is L1. Hmm, but which to name? Both are wrong statements. I'll write one note naming the clearer: \"'slide' is held as a dependent of 'block' and 'left' as the verb of a nonfinite clause, so the second command has no verb of its own\".\n\nGrade: L1 (verb \"left\" is no verb here; and the second \"slide\" is not a verb anywhere). Good.\n\nn=124: like n=109. Accept L8.\n\nn=125: \"the olive treatment for the shape, and olive lettered inside\"\nn1 \"the olive treatment\"; r1 \"for\" n1→n2 (the shape); k1 coordination \"and\" n1, n3 (\"olive lettered\": dep olive, head lettered); c1 fragment modifier \"inside\" complement k1.\n\nHmm: \"olive lettered inside\" — \"lettered\" as a nominal head with \"olive\" as a dependent; \"inside\" as a modifier of the clause. Calling \"lettered\" a head nominal — \"lettered\" is a participle; per the case, a participle that describes is the verb of a nonfinite clause tied to the thing. Held as a nominal head... hmm. Is this a wrong statement? \"olive lettered inside\" — the label lettered in olive, inside. As a nominal \"olive lettered\" with head \"lettered\" — hmm, \"lettered\" as a noun? Weird.\n\nHmm, is it L3 (head wrong)? Or acceptable garbled? The message is a fragment: \"the olive treatment for the shape, and olive lettered inside\". Both parts are fragments describing styling. Holding \"lettered\" as a head with \"olive\" dependent — the next layer reads \"olive lettered\" as a thing. Hmm, \"olive lettered\" is not a thing.\n\nI'm not confident. Let me consider accepting: all words placed, tied; plausible for a fragment. Accept L8? The participle-as-head is a bit off but the rules' pass list has \"a word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb\" — \"lettered\" as a noun? no.\n\nHmm. Risk either way. Considering leniency instructions, accept L8.\n\nHmm, but hmm: \"inside\" as a modifier of the clause — fine.\n\nAccept.\n\nn=126: \"I walked the team through Revenue trend this morning; nobody argued with the data, only with how it reads at a glance, so as a first pass how about line thinner? and then let's have the marker borders thicker on the revenue trend, then we revisit next week, also put the y axis on the right\"\nCircuit: c1 declarative walked subject I complements n2 (the team), n4 (this morning); r1 \"through\" c1→n3; c2 = declarative **interrogative** verb \"argued\" modifier only, modifier how, subject nobody; r2 \"with\" c2→n6; r3 \"with\" c2→c3 (\"how it reads\"); c3 declarative reads subject it modifier how — wait, c3 has modifier \"how\"; c2 also has modifier \"how\"?! Text has \"how\" twice (\"with how it reads\" and \"how about line thinner\"). Yes two \"how\"s. OK.\nr4 \"at\" c3→n8; k1 coordination \"so\",\"and\" conjuncts c1, c2, c4, c7, c8; r5 \"as\" c2→n9 (a first pass); r6 \"about\" c2→n10 (\"line\" with dep \"thinner\"!); c4 imperative let complement n11 (\"'s\"); c5 nonfinite have; n12 \"the marker\"; c6 declarative verb \"borders\" modifier thicker subject n12!!; r7 \"on\" c6→n13; c7 declarative revisit subject we complement next week; c8 imperative put complement the y axis; r8 \"on\" c8→the right; r9 c4→c5; r10 c5→c6.\n\nProblems:\n1. c2 \"nobody argued with the data, only with how it reads at a glance\" — written **interrogative** → wrong form (a declarative written as interrogative) → L1.\n2. n10 \"line thinner\" — \"thinner\" as a dependent of \"line\" → L3 per the settled case.\n3. c6: \"the marker borders thicker\" — \"borders\" as the VERB of a declarative clause with subject \"the marker\" → L1/L3 wrong.\n4. r6 \"about\" from c2 to n10 — the \"how about\" belongs to c2 (nobody argued)? Since c2 is `interrogative`... hmm.\n\nMultiple flaws. Grade: L1 (c2's form interrogative for a declarative; and \"borders\" as a verb). Note will quote 'nobody argued with the data'.\n\nQuote: 'nobody argued with the data' — in the message: \"nobody argued with the data\" — yes.\n\nSo n=126 → L1.\n\nn=127: \"and also Sales by month: grid line style dash-dot\"\nc1 fragment discourse \"and\" complement n1 (\"also Sales\": dep also, head Sales); r1 \"by\" n1→n2 (month); n3 \"grid line style dash-dot\" (deps grid, line, style, head dash-dot); r2 n1→n3.\n\nHmm, \"also\" as a dependent of \"Sales\" — \"a word of order\"? \"also\" is an additive adverb. Held as a dependent of the nominal → part of the description? Hmm, L3? \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun\" — \"also\" isn't time/order exactly, but it's like \"too\" (\"the shed walls too\" case is in the list!). The case: \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun from where it stands ('orders by week first', 'beside the sun next', 'the shed walls too', 'now a second wooden boat')\". \"also\" is similar to \"too\" — an additive adverb that cannot describe a noun. Held as a dependent → L3.\n\nHmm, \"and also Sales by month\" — the \"also\" scope is the whole item. Holding it inside the nominal \"also Sales\" makes it describe Sales. Hmm, \"also\" before a noun... \"also Sales\" — hmm, in some contexts \"also\" can modify a noun phrase (\"also John came\" — it's a focusing adverb that can adjoin NPs). Hmm, \"also\" as a dependent of the nominal: \"the also Sales\"? Ehh.\n\nI'd lean L3 per the \"too\" case analogy. Hmm, but \"also\" is not exactly order/time. The case list mentions \"the shed walls too\" — \"too\" as a dependent of \"walls\" fails. \"also\" is the same family (additive adverb). So L3.\n\nHmm, risky. Alternatively accept. Let me note \"n3: 'grid line style dash-dot' as one nominal with head 'dash-dot'\" — that's a merged nominal of property+value, similar to other chains; acceptable-ish.\n\nI'll reject n=127 as L3 quoting 'also Sales' with the note that 'also' is held as a dependent of 'Sales' though it modifies the whole item like 'too'.\n\nHmm, is that solid? \"also\" is the second word of the message (\"and also Sales by month\"), so it's a clause-level word. Held as a dependent of \"Sales\" — yes, this changes the item into \"also Sales\". I'm fairly comfortable: L3.\n\nn=128: \"This year and last side by side: order value by delivery time\"\nn1 \"This year\"; k1 coordination \"and\" n1, n2 (\"last\"); n3 \"side\"; r1 \"by\" n3→n4 (side); n5 \"order value\"; r2 \"by\" n5→n6 (delivery time); c1 fragment complements k1, n3, n5.\n\n\"side by side\" as two nominals tied by \"by\" — fine (as n=108). Accept L8.\n\nn=129: same family as n=103 with \"and then make the font a bit bigger\" as c6 imperative discourse \"and\" — but c6 is NOT in any coordination! k1 coordinates c3 and c4 only. c6 \"and then make the font a bit bigger\" is a clause with no tie to anything. Hmm — is it L6? \"A clause ... is tied to nothing inside its sentence.\" Hmm, but c6 is a separate sentence (\"... before the meeting starts. and then make the font a bit bigger\"). Rule L6: \"Separate sentences need no tie.\" So c6 as a separate sentence needs no tie. Fine.\n\nThe \"and\" at the start of the later sentence held as discourse in c6 — allowed by the case.\n\nAccept L8.\n\nn=130: \"Revenue by month of the order date, top five regions only\"\nn1 Revenue; r1 \"by\" n1→n2 (month); r2 \"of\" n2→n3 (the order date); c1 imperative verb \"top\" modifier only complement n4 (\"five regions\"); c2 fragment complement n1; r3 n1→c1.\n\nProblem: \"top five regions only\" — \"top\" is held as the VERB of an imperative clause! \"top\" is an adjective here (\"top five regions\"). L1: \"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here.\" Yes → L1.\n\nQuote 'top five regions only' — note: \"'top' is held as the verb of an imperative clause though it is the adjective of 'five regions'\".\n\nL1.\n\nn=131: \"add a star called dog; then put dog below owner.\"\nc1 imperative add complement n1 (a star); n2 = \"called dog\" (dep called, head dog!); k1 coordination \"then\" c1, c2; c2 imperative put complement n3 (dog); r1 \"below\" c2→n4 (owner); r2 n1→n2.\n\nHmm: \"a star called dog\" — n2 has \"called\" as a DEPENDENT of \"dog\", so \"called\" is a modifier word inside the nominal. Per the settled case: \"A participle that names or describes ('a group called g2', 'a bird facing left') is the `verb` of a nonfinite clause tied to the thing. Written as the `marker` of a relation between the thing and its name, it also passes.\"\n\nHere \"called\" is neither: it's a dependent of the name \"dog\". Is that a wrong statement? Hmm. The case allows verb of a nonfinite clause, or marker. \"Held as a dependent of the name\" — not among the passing options. Hmm, but the rule is about participle handling; holding \"called\" as a dependent of \"dog\" makes \"called dog\" a name phrase — \"a star called dog\". Hmm, the next layer reads: star, with n2 \"called dog\" tied. The info is preserved. Is there a wrong statement? Not clearly.\n\nThe case says those two ways \"pass\"; it doesn't say other ways fail. Rejecting needs a quote + wrong statement. \"called\" as a dependent — hmm, the nominal \"called dog\" with head \"dog\" means the name is \"called dog\" — it's like a multi-word name, allowed (\"A name of several words may stand whole in head, or with its last word as head and the others as dependent\"). \"called dog\" could be read as a name of several words! OK accept L8.\n\nn=132: \"I showed Budget split round the team; they didn't argue with the numbers but not with how it reads at a glance, so as a first pass make the legend smaller, then we revisit next week; plus show the percentages\"\nc1 declarative showed subject I complement n2 (\"Budget split\": dep Budget, head split); r1 \"round\" n2→n3 (the team); c2 declarative did n't argue subject they; r2 \"with\" c2→n5; k1 coordination \"but\" conjunct r2, r3 (relations); r3 negation=not marker \"with\" from c2 to c3 (how it reads); c3 declarative reads subject it modifier how; r4 \"at\" c3→n7; r5 \"so\" c5→c4; r6 \"as\" c4→n8; c4 imperative make modifier smaller complement n9 (the legend); c5 declarative revisit subject we complement next week; k2 coordination \"plus\" conjuncts c1, c2, c5, c6; c6 imperative show complement n12.\n\nHmm: k1 coordinates r2 and r3 (the two \"with\" relations of c2). The message: \"they didn't argue with the numbers but not with how it reads at a glance\" — coordinating the two with-phrases. Plausible.\n\nr5 \"so\" from c5 (revisit) to c4 (make): hmm, \"so as a first pass make the legend smaller, then we revisit next week\" — the \"so\" relation starts at c4 (make) typically (the clause after the marker). Here r5 from c5 to c4 — from \"revisit\" to \"make\". Hmm. The relation marked \"so\" — which way? Per the settled style, \"so it sits under the table\": relation from the main clause to the sub-clause. Here \"so\" starts the part \"so as a first pass make the legend smaller, then we revisit\" — the \"so\" connects the previous sentence to the following instruction. Starting at c5 (we revisit) means \"revisit so make the legend smaller\"? Hmm, that's backwards. But is it ruled out? The rules pass: \"The start of a relation with a marker: the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\".\n\nHmm, with c4 (make) and c5 (revisit) in the same sentence, the start of the \"so\" relation should be c4 or... The message: \"... , so as a first pass make the legend smaller, then we revisit next week\". \"so\" belongs to the contents following it: c4 and c5. Choosing c5 as the start... The rule about markers between clauses: \"A clause with a marker that stands between two clauses, after a dash/semicolon/full stop and before a comma, opens the clause after the comma\" — the case there. \"so\" here follows a comma and precedes \"as a first pass make...\" → it opens c4. Started at c5 (after c4)? Hmm, the case says: started at the clause BEFORE the dash fails (L3). Here it started at c5 which is AFTER c4. Hmm, similar spirit: should start at c4; starting at c5 is wrong.\n\nHmm, but \"revisit\" comes after \"make\" — does the \"so\" relation start at the entire following coordination (c4 then c5)? Starting at c5 skips c4.\n\nAlso, \"so\" might better be a coordination conjunction joining c2 with c4. The formalizer used k2's conjunctions: \"plus\" only for conjuncts c1,c2,c5,c6.\n\nHmm. This is getting deep. Let me check other things in n=132: \"Budget split\" — n2 head \"split\" with \"Budget\" dep. Fine (name-ish). c2 negation n't with auxiliary did — good.\n\nThe k2 coordination joins c1, c2, c5, c6 with \"plus\": c1 (I showed), c2 (they didn't argue), c5 (we revisit), c6 (show) — the message has \";\" separators and \"so\", \"plus\". Joining all four clauses; hmm, \"so\" joins c2→c4; not a conjunct issue.\n\nI'd accept n=132 as L8? The r5 direction is debatable. Hmm.\n\nLet me consider rejecting on r5: \"so\" relation from c5 to c4 = \"so\" starting at \"we revisit next week\". The words put \"so\" right before \"as a first pass make the legend smaller\" → it should start there. Starting at c5 means the relation's start is a clause that comes after the marker and after another clause. Per the settled case about markers between clauses (that case is about after a dash/semicolon/full stop), started elsewhere fails L3. I think a strict judge rejects; a lenient judge accepts.\n\nHmm. I'll accept (leniency) — the relation is within the same sentence and both clauses are tied by it; the next layer can read \"so\" tying the instruction. Hmm.\n\nHmm, but wait: is r5's direction maybe fine? \"so\" from c5 to c4: in the schema, a relation from A to B with marker M reads \"A M B\"? For \"so it sits under the table\": from main clause to sub → \"the main clause, so [it sits...]\". So \"from c5 to c4\" reads \"we revisit next week, so [make the legend smaller]\". That IS a valid reading of the sentence with reordering? The message order is \"so ... make the legend smaller, then we revisit\". Hmm, \"so\" reads as \"so, as a first pass, make the legend smaller\" — the consequence. The relation start should be the preceding clause (c2: they didn't argue / or c1). Hmm! Actually \"so\" here is a coordinator connecting the previous sentence with the instruction. The formalizer's choice c5→c4 is odd.\n\nGiven multiple lenient acceptances, keep L8. Hmm. Ehh.\n\nLet me look at the remaining lines and then do the final calibration pass.",
