    "text": "n=133: \"sorry to nag, but how about z axis upright again? and let's have the y axis inverted on the surface chart\"\nc1 fragment discourse sorry; r1 \"to\" c1→c2 (nonfinite nag); k1 coordination but, and: conjuncts c1, c3, c4; c3 fragment modifier how; r2 \"about\" c3→n1 (\"z axis upright again\" — deps z, upright, again, head axis); c4 imperative let complement n2 (\"'s\"); c5 nonfinite have; n3 the y axis; c6 nonfinite inverted subject n3; r3 \"on\" c6→n4; r4 c4→c5; r5 c5→c6.\n\nProblem: n1 has \"upright\" and \"again\" as dependents of \"axis\" — per the settled case: \"upright\" (a word after a nominal saying how the thing should be) should be a modifier of the clause; held as a dependent → L3. And \"again\" is a word of time held as a dependent → L3.\n\nYes: \"how about z axis upright again?\" — \"upright\" describes how the z axis should be; \"again\" is time. Both held as dependents of the nominal → L3.\n\nQuote 'z axis upright again'.\n\nn=134: \"let there be an ocena in the background\"\nc1 imperative let; n1 there; c2 nonfinite be subject there complement n2 (an ocena); r1 \"in\" ambiguous c2→n3; r2 c1→c2.\n\nHmm, \"let there be ...\" — \"there\" as subject of \"be\": fine (per n=58). Accept L8.\n\nn=135: \"set o1 to 150 wide and 120 high\"\nc1 imperative set complement n1 (o1); r1 \"to\" c1→n2 (\"150 wide and 120 high\": deps 150, wide, and, 120, high; head 150!).\n\nHmm: n2 head \"150\" with deps wide, and, 120, high. Merging \"150 wide\" and \"120 high\" into one nominal. Hmm — like n=10/n=19 (a word after a measure should be a modifier of the clause). Per the settled case: \"So is the word after a measure: 'move block 6 two spaces left', 'the dog a size bigger', with 'two spaces' and 'a size' nominals of their own. Held as a dependent of the nominal, the word becomes part of the description by which the thing is found: L3.\"\n\nSo \"150 wide\" — \"wide\" after the measure \"150\" → \"wide\" should be a modifier of the clause; held as a dependent of n2 → L3.\n\nHmm, but here n2's head is \"150\" (the measure itself) with \"wide\" a dependent. Per the case, \"wide\" held as a dependent of the nominal fails L3. Yes.\n\nAlso everything merged into one nominal (\"150 wide and 120 high\") — two measures with \"and\" — merged.\n\nQuote '150 wide and 120 high'. L3.\n\nn=136: \"let there be a medium cloud on the very top edge, and a big bench down at the bottom — exactly where the picture needs it\"\nc1 imperative let; n1 there; c2 nonfinite be modifier down, modifier exactly, subject there, complement k1 ([the cloud, the bench]); r1 \"on\" n2→n3; r2 \"at\" c2→n5; r3 \"where\" c2→c3 (needs); r4 c1→c2.\n\nHmm, \"down\" as a modifier of \"be\" — the \"down at the bottom\" belongs to the bench. Modifiers of the same clause merge — acceptable-ish (like n=97). Accept L8.\n\nn=137: \"shift the same woman in the red dress all the way over to the very left\"\nc1 imperative shift modifier over complements n1 (the same woman) and n3 (\"all the way\"); r1 \"in\" n1→n2; r2 \"to\" c1→n4 (the very left).\n\n\"all the way\" as a nominal complement of c1 — per the case, fixed phrases may be a modifier; here a nominal. Acceptable? It's a measure-ish nominal. Fine. Accept L8.\n\nn=138: \"the junior pulled the figures together overnight and left the sensor contour looking unfinished round the edges, so make the colour map blue to lighter blue and I'll take it from there and then put the y axis on the right\"\nc1 declarative pulled together overnight subject the junior complement the figures; k1 coordination and/so/and/and with conjuncts c1, c2, c4, c5, c6; c2 declarative \"left\" (no complement, no subject); c3 nonfinite looking modifier unfinished subject n3; r1 \"round\" c3→n4; c4 imperative make modifier blue complement n5 (the colour map); r2 \"to\" c4→n6 (lighter blue); c5 declarative 'll take subject I complement it; r3 \"from\" c5→n9 (there); c6 declarative then put complement the y axis (no subject!); r4 \"on\" c6→n11; r5 c2→c3.\n\nHmm, c6 \"put the y axis on the right\" written as **declarative** with no subject — it's a command (\"and then put the y axis on the right\"). Per the settled case for verbs after \"and\": \"a verb after 'and' or 'then' that follows a clause with a subject and an auxiliary, in the form that auxiliary takes, may share them: a clause with no subject of its own, declarative or nonfinite. Written as a command, imperative, it passes too.\"\n\nHere c6 follows c5 \"I'll take it from there\" which HAS a subject \"I\" and an auxiliary \"'ll\". The verb after \"and then\" is \"put\" — in the form the auxiliary takes (\"'ll put\") → declarative with no subject passes! Good.\n\nAnd c2 \"left\" after c1 \"the junior pulled\" — declarative sharing the subject \"the junior\" → passes per the case.\n\nSo n=138 is fine? c2 has no complement written: \"left the sensor contour looking unfinished\" — the complement is via r5 c2→c3 (c3 \"looking\" with subject the sensor contour). OK.\n\nAccept L8.\n\nn=139: \"guide block 10 left of block 9, and after that, guide block 10 further left until it is even with block 9\"\nc1 imperative guide modifier left, further, left, complement k1 ([block(10), \"guide block\"(10)]); r1 \"of\" ambiguous c1→n3 (block); r2 \"after\" n6→n5 (that); r3 \"until\" c1→c2; c2 declarative is modifier even subject it; r4 \"with\" c2→n9; n7 10; n10 9; ties.\n\nProblem: the second \"guide\" (word) is held as a dependent of \"block\" (n6 deps guide, head block) again! Like n=123. The second instruction \"guide block 10 further left until it is even with block 9\" — its verb \"guide\" is held as a dependent of \"block\", so the second imperative has no verb written. Hmm, and c1 holds modifiers left, further, left for both instructions.\n\nSo: words \"left\" (×2) and \"further\" all in c1; the second \"guide\" as a nominal dependent. That's L3/L5-ish. The second command's verb is not a verb anywhere → L5 (\"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a ... dependent ... and no clause has it as `verb`\").\n\nHmm, but \"guide\" IS the verb of c1 (the first instance). The second instance held as a dependent. Hmm, so L5's wording: \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier, a dependent, a head or a discourse word, and no clause has it as `verb`.\" The second \"guide\" is held as a dependent and no clause has it as verb — but a clause (c1) does have \"guide\" as verb from the other instance. Technically it's L5 by the letter? The clause isn't written: the second instruction has no clause. Yes L5-ish. Or L3 (words of one written clause held in another — the second instruction's words left/further held in c1).\n\nI'd grade L3: the second instruction's words stand in the first clause. Hmm. Which one? Let me pick L3 for consistency with n=123? For n=123 I chose L1 because \"left\" was made the verb of a nonfinite clause. For n=139 there's no such thing except c1.\n\nHmm, in n=139: is the \"left\"/\"further\" merge the flaw? c1: modifiers left, further, left; complement k1 = coordination of \"block\" (n1) and \"guide block\" (n6). Hmm, so c1 = \"guide block [and guide block] left further left\" — a mess where both objects are complements and all directions modifiers of one clause.\n\nI'll reject as L3 with note quoting 'guide block 10 further left' and saying that the second 'guide' is held as a dependent of 'block' and both 'left' and 'further' stand in the first clause, so the second instruction is not written.\n\nGrade... L3 vs L5. Hmm. The second command's verb is \"guide\" held as a dependent → L5 says exactly this: \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a ... dependent ..., and no clause has it as `verb`.\" Hmm, technically the clause \"guide block 10 further left\" is not written — its verb held as a dependent. But L5 requires \"no clause has it as `verb`\" — c1 has \"guide\" as verb (the first instance). Since the second instance is a separate word occurrence held as a dependent, and no clause has THAT word as verb. Hmm, ambiguous.\n\nFor n=123 I said L1 because \"left\" was a verb. For n=139, no verb word is missing invention... I'll go L5. Hmm. Or L3 (words in the wrong wire).\n\nUgh. These grade choices are guesses. I'll go with L3 for n=139 (words of the second command held in the first clause's wires: 'guide' as a dependent of 'block', 'left'/'further' as modifiers of c1).\n\nn=140: \"draw a house, at x 120, at y 90, 160 wide, 100 high, size 80, coloured red, labelled Home\"\nc1 imperative draw modifier \"wide\" complement n1 (a house) complement n6 (\"160\"); r1 \"at\" n1→n2 (x); n3 120 tied r5; r2 \"at\" n1→n4 (y); n5 90 tied; n6 160; n7 \"100 high\" (head 100, dep high); n8 \"size\"; n9 80; c2 nonfinite coloured modifier red; c3 nonfinite labelled complement Home; r3 n1→n7; r4 n1→n8; r7 n8→n9; r8 n8→c2; r9 n8→c3.\n\nHmm: \"wide\" as a modifier of c1 (correct per case: word after a measure). \"160\" as a complement of c1 (with \"wide\" as the clause modifier) — hmm, \"160 wide\": the measure \"160\" is separated from \"wide\" — acceptable-ish? The case says \"with 'two spaces' and 'a size' nominals of their own\" and the direction is a clause modifier. So \"160 wide\" → \"160\" nominal, \"wide\" modifier. OK.\n\n\"100 high\" — n7 with head \"100\" and dep \"high\" — hmm, inconsistent with \"160 wide\": here \"high\" is a dependent of the nominal (the case's L3 pattern!). Hmm. \"100 high\" — \"high\" after a measure held as a dependent of the nominal → L3 per the case.\n\nAlso \"size 80\" — n8 \"size\" and n9 \"80\" tied. OK.\n\n\"coloured red\" and \"labelled Home\" tied from n8 (size)! r8 and r9 from n8 → the colouring and labelling attach to \"size\" rather than the house. Hmm, that's a wrong start? Attaching \"coloured red\"/\"labelled Home\" to the \"size\" nominal — they modify the house. Hmm, \"a relation ... a start the words rule out\"? A participle describing the house attached to \"size\" — hmm.\n\nThe cleanest flaw: \"high\" as a dependent of the measure → L3 per the settled case. I'll use that: quote '100 high'.\n\nn=141: \"after that centre Bin just below e2 so the label is unmistakably attached to the link\"\nr1 \"after\" from n2 (Bin) to n1 (\"that centre\": dep that, head centre!); c1 fragment modifier just complement n2 (Bin); r2 \"below\" n2→n3 (e2); k1 coordination \"so\" c1, c2; c2 declarative auxiliary is, modifier unmistakably, verb attached, subject the label; r3 \"to\" c2→the link.\n\nProblems:\n1. \"centre\" is the VERB (\"centre Bin just below e2\")! Held as a dependent... n1 = \"that centre\" with head \"centre\", tied from n2 (Bin) via r1 \"after\". So the command \"centre Bin just below e2\" has no verb — c1 is a fragment with modifier \"just\" complement \"Bin\". The verb \"centre\" is held as a nominal head → L5 (\"its verb is held as ... a head ... and no clause has it as verb\").\n2. \"after that\" — \"that\" and \"centre\" merged into one nominal \"that centre\" — wrong merge (L3).\n\nAlso is \"after that\" a fixed phrase that may be held whole as a modifier? Yes — but the formalizer split it and merged with \"centre\".\n\nGrade: L3 (the words \"that\" and \"centre\" merged; \"centre\" is the verb). Hmm, L5 also. L3 first among these after L1/L2? Both L3 and L5 apply; L3 first.\n\nQuote 'after that centre' — note that 'centre' is the verb and 'that' belongs to 'after that'.\n\nHmm, careful: L1? \"Its `verb` is a word that is no verb here\" — no. L2? no discourse misuse (no discourse words here). Fine: L3.\n\nHmm, wait — L5 might be the intended for a verb held as a head. The rules: L5 \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier, a dependent, a head or a discourse word, and no clause has it as `verb`.\" That's exactly this: \"centre\" held as a head, no clause has it as verb. L3 also applies (merge of \"that\"+\"centre\"). Since the instruction says the first applicable of L1..L6, L3 wins if it applies. Hmm, does L3 apply? \"Words stand in the wrong wire, or a tie is wrong\" — \"that\" and \"centre\" merged into one nominal → \"two nominals are merged\" is L3. Yes.\n\nI'll use L5 for n=141? Hmm. The note can focus on the verb; but the grade must match. Let me think about which the reference would use... For a verb held as a head with no clause having it, L5's text matches word for word (\"a head\"). I'll go L5, and mention the \"that\"+\"centre\" merge in the same sentence. Hmm, but if L3 applies first...\n\nUgh. Decision rule: when both apply, use the first (lower) grade. I'll be consistent: use the lowest applicable grade. For n=141: L3 applies (merged nominals) → L3.\n\nHmm, but wait: is the merge really L3? \"after that centre Bin\" — \"that\" is a demonstrative belonging to \"after that\"; \"centre\" is the verb. Merging them into \"that centre\" as a nominal — yes, \"two nominals are merged\"/words in wrong wire. L3.\n\nOK: n=141 → L3.\n\nLet me now read the last lines 142-150.",
