    "text": "n=142: like n=138 but with c4 imperative \"make the lines on the claims chart distinct colours\" and c6 declarative \"make the font a bit bigger\" (no subject, after \"and\" — following c5 \"I'll take it from there\" with a subject and auxiliary → passes per the case). Accept L8.\n\nn=143: \"when the regions dial in tomorrow the first chart they see is Claims by month, so for their sake make the markers black before the meeting starts, and give it the title Claims history\"\nr3 \"so\" from c3 to k1 (coordination of c4, c6). Fine. Accept L8.\n\nn=144: \"and also let's restore the arrow size to one step smaller\"\nc1 imperative discourse and, modifier also, verb let, complement n1 (\"'s\"); c2 nonfinite restore complement n2 (arrow size) complement n3 (\"one step\"); r1 \"to\" c2→n4 (\"smaller\"); r2 c1→c2.\n\nHmm: n3 \"one step\" as a complement of c2 (restore) — and \"to smaller\" with n4 \"smaller\" as a nominal head! \"to one step smaller\" — the relation \"to\" ends at n4 \"smaller\" which is a standalone nominal. Hmm, \"smaller\" as a nominal head — the rule: \"A nominal whose `head` is the graded word ('a bit longer' with head 'longer') or the lone grading word ('way') fails: L3.\"\n\nHere n4's head is \"smaller\", the graded word! So L3 per that case.\n\nHmm, wait: \"to one step smaller\" — \"one step\" (measure) and \"smaller\" (graded word). The case says a nominal whose head is the graded word fails L3. n4 = nominal head \"smaller\" alone → yes fails.\n\nHmm, but is it a \"grading\" relation? \"one step smaller\" — \"one step\" grades \"smaller\". The case's example \"a bit longer\" with head \"longer\" fails. Here \"smaller\" stands as its own nominal tied by r1 \"to\". So L3.\n\nQuote 'one step smaller'? The words of the message have \"to one step smaller\". Note: \"'smaller' stands as a nominal of its own with head 'smaller', a graded word, instead of a modifier of the clause\".\n\nHmm, alternatively accept? It's the explicit L3 case. Reject