tter\" with deps Grandad, 90. \"Grandad 90 letter\" — the title is \"Grandad 90 letter\"? So the document is titled \"Grandad 90 letter\"? Hmm, the message: \"create a document titled Grandad 90 letter containing \"...\".\" So the title is \"Grandad 90 letter\". Head \"letter\" with deps \"Grandad\", \"90\" — a name of several words with the last word as head: allowed. OK.\n- n=52 — accepted.\n- n=68 — accepted (\"exept\" as nominal head). Hmm, \"exept\" is a misspelt linking word (\"except\"). Held as a nominal head with a relation \"with\" from it. Hmm, \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\" — it's held as a head! Even worse. Hmm. But \"exept\" is misspelt → the misspelt rule. Ehh. Keep accept.\n- n=101 — accepted. \"whatever\" as marker — hmm, \"whatever it was\" tied to \"the latest\" with marker \"whatever\". Hmm, \"whatever\" is not a preposition, but it is a linking word in this relative-like use. Accept.\n- n=109/124 — accepted (JSON blobs).\n- n=116 — accepted (repairs/spares as verbs). Hmm, keep? I decided accept.\n- n=143 — accepted.\n- n=147 — accepted.\n- n=82 — accepted.\n- n=42, 44? 44 rejected.\n- n=59 — accepted (curled as declarative). Hmm, this one is questionable: form declarative for a participle clause. And n=29 has the same first part with nonfinite. Both can't be... well, they can (different messages). Hmm, \"a little black cat curled up near the bench\" as a declarative sentence is grammatical. Accept.\n- n=76 — accepted (the \"instead ... keep\" merge). Hmm, let me reconsider. c1 = \"instead keep the rest as it is\" — imperative keep with modifier instead, complement \"the rest\", r7 \"as\"→c7 (\"it is\"). The word \"instead\" from the message start is in this clause. Which clause holds a discourse word isn't judged; \"instead\" is a modifier here. Hmm, but if held as a modifier, the clause's identity matters. L1 \"Words of one written clause are held in another\": \"instead\" belongs to the first part (\"instead, a small dog down at the bottom\"). Held in the last clause. Hmm, but \"instead\" is a sentential adverb that could attach to the whole message. Its place in a particular clause is like a discourse word. Since which clause holds a discourse word is not judged, and \"instead\" is essentially a discourse-ish word held as a modifier — I'll accept.\n\nHmm, and n=73 also had \"instead\" as a modifier of c1 (along with \"next\" and \"clear\"). Fine, that one I rejected on \"clear\".\n\n- n=64 — accepted.\n- n=66 — accepted.\n- n=94 — accepted (leave as declarative).\n- n=32, 33, 34, 36, 38-40, 46, 48(rejected), 49-53, 55-56, 58, 60-61, 63, 65, 67(rejected), 69-71, 74-75, 77-78, 81-86, 88-89, 92, 95-98, 100-106, 108-111, 113-115, 118-119, 121-122, 125, 128-129, 131-132, 134, 136-138, 142-145, 147-150.\n\nWait, n=67: I rejected as L1 (form interrogative for \"I need the revenue trend legible\"). Let me double check: text \"for the monthly ops review on Friday I need the revenue trend legible from the back of the conference room, so how about line thinner?; also let's have the marker borders thicker...\" and the circuit has c1 form=interrogative verb=need subject=I complement=the revenue trend. Yes — \"I need...\" is declarative, written interrogative → L1. And the contrast with n=115 (the same prefix but declarative) confirms the error. Good.\n\nAlso n=67 has other flaws (\"line\" with dep \"thinner\"), fine.\n\n- n=119 — accepted.\n- n=103 — accepted. Hmm, and n=129/143 similar. OK.\n\nNow, let me reconsider n=41 and a few others once more... I'll go with my decisions.\n\nWait, also n=25: \"no, not that, the branches growing right\" — c1 nonfinite \"growing\" with subject \"the branches\", k1 coordination with c2 (fragment \"not that\"). Hmm, \"not that\" as a fragment with \"not\" as a dependent of \"that\" — \"not\" is a negation word held as a dependent of a nominal. L1: \"An auxiliary or negation word is held in another place\"? Hmm! \"not that\" — the negation negates \"that\". Held as a dependent of \"that\" inside a fragment. Hmm, \"another place\"? The negation belongs to the elliptical clause \"not that [one]\" — held as a dependent of \"that\" in a fragment. That's arguably fine (the fragment \"not that\" is the negation of \"that\").\n\nHmm, \"negation\" is a clause key: `negation` on a clause. In a fragment, \"not\" as a dependent of \"that\"... The rules L1: \"An `auxiliary` or `negation` word is held in another place, or negates the wrong clause.\" Held as a dependent of \"that\" in the same fragment \"not that\" — it's in the right place (with the thing negated). I'll keep the accept.\n\nOK. Also n=45 note: also \"cheers\" held as a nominal. Fine.\n\nLet me reconsider n=34 (cannot). Hmm, \"cannot\" as negation with no auxiliary. Is that like a negation word that should be an auxiliary+negation? The message's word is exactly \"cannot\". Holding it as negation is fine; holding it as auxiliary would also be fine. Accept.\n\nOne more re-check: n=115 vs n=67 and n=112's first part. Fine.\n\nNow also double-check n=132 (accepted with the \"so\" relation direction). Hmm. And n=126 rejected for form + \"borders\" as verb. Let me double check n=126's c2: \"nobody argued with the data, only with how it reads at a glance\" — form=interrogative with verb argued. Yes wrong form → L1. Good.\n\nn=112: c5 \"I'd like the numbers back on the right on the sales chart\" form=interrogative → L1. Good.\n\nAlso check n=49 again — accepted. And n=55 accepted.\n\nLet me reconsider n=35 (blue, blue, green, red). Hmm, \"layer the four blocks in the order blue, blue, green, red\". The circuit: n3 with deps blue, blue, green, head red; r2 \"in\" c1→n2 (the order); r4 n2→n3. So the order nominal is \"blue blue green red\" with head \"red\". A nominal with four colour words where one is the head — that merges four items. Per the settled case about a nominal whose head is a graded word... not applicable. L3: \"two nominals are merged\" — four nominals merged. Keep L3.\n\nHmm, also n=55: \"sections Intro, 2, 3 and Second\" — the circuit made a coordination of Intro, 2, 3, Second (each its own nominal) — good handling of a list. So the formalizer knows how to do lists. In n=35 it merged. That supports rejection.\n\nNow let me also double check n=30/86/104 acceptances vs n=55-style. In 30/86/104 the property lists are chained; accepted. OK.\n\nNow write the file. Notes: for rejections, must quote words of the message in single quotes (checker verifies the quoted substring appears in the message, case-insensitive, whitespace-normalized). Be careful: quotes must be exact substrings. Also note should be one sentence.\n\nI must double check each quoted string is a verbatim substring of the message. Let me write the verdicts programmatically with a validation step: for each rejection, check `text.toLowerCase().includes(quote.toLowerCase())` for the quoted region — I'll just run the checker (which does the same) and fix.\n\nLet me now write out all 150 verdicts. Let me draft the rejections' notes:\n\n7 L3: \"'a medium brown dog' is one of the things to give but is held as the complement of the nonfinite clause of 'standing', which the words attach to the fisherman\".\nCareful: quotes must be substrings. 'a medium brown dog' — the message has \"a medium brown dog\". Good. But also, the checker just needs ONE quoted pair to match. Also avoid other single quotes that might confuse... fine.\n\n10 L3: \"'larger' in 'error bar caps a size larger' is held as a dependent of the caps nominal, so it becomes part of how the caps are found instead of the change asked for\".\nBoth quoted substrings must exist... only one is needed. 'error bar caps a size larger' — message: \"go for error bar caps a size larger on the sales chart\" — yes.\n\n12 L3: \"'next' stands as a dependent of 'block' in 'next est block 1', so a word of order describes the block instead of the instruction\".\n\n13 L2: \"'ahve the boy in the picture stay' is a command whose verb is 'ahve' (for 'have'), but 'ahve' stands in `discourse` and no clause has it as its verb\".\n\n16 L1: \"'face left' is a command, but 'face' is held as the head of a nominal and the command has no clause\".\n\n18 L3: \"'left' in 'one space left' is a dependent of 'space', so it describes the space instead of saying where the block goes\".\n\n19 L3: \"'wider' in 'marker spacing wider' is a dependent of 'spacing', so it describes the spacing instead of stating the change\".\n\n22 L5: \"'est the Shell block into the upper right corner' has no clause: 'est' is held as a modifier and 'keep' is the only verb\".\n\n23 L3: \"'the line medium width' is one nominal with 'width' as head, merging the thing to change with the value it should take\".\n\n27 L3: \"'biggest first' is a nominal whose head is 'first', so a word of order is attached to 'the wines' instead of modifying the clause\".\n\n29 L3: \"'all the way through' is held as dependent words of 'light', so it describes the light instead of the whole scene\".\n\n35 L3: \"'blue, blue, green, red' is one nominal with 'red' as head, merging the four colours of the order\".\n\n37 L3: \"'Proposal tree' is split: 'tree' is held as a modifier of the clause of 'make' while only 'Proposal' is its complement\".\n\n41 L1: \"'Let us' is written as an interrogative clause whose verb is 'Let' and 'us' is held as a complement of 'show'\".\n\n43 L1: \"'blocks' is held as the verb of a declarative clause, though 'and blocks one paragraph' is the third item of the list of what the document has\".\n\n44 L3: \"'the blue block one place right' is merged into the fragment that starts with 'the red block', so 'right' and the second 'one place' also say the red block moves right\".\n\n45 L1: \"'label' is the only verb of 'label number n4' but is held as a dependent of 'number', so the command is lost\".\n\n47 L1: \"'flip the x axis over' is a command, but it is written as an `interrogative` clause\".\n\n48 L1: \"'not' in 'not right' is a negation word held in `discourse`, so the clause says the dog faces 'left' and 'right'\".\n\n54 L3: \"'nothing else of the house' and 'everything else untouched' are merged into one nominal with head 'nothing'\".\n\n57 L3: \"'of' in 'scatter plot of for each region scatter' is a linking word held as a modifier and ties nothing\".\n\n62 L3: \"'to' in 'character 10 to 15' — hmm need exact substring: '10 to 15' (message: \"characters 10 to 15 of Body\"). Note: \"'to' is a linking word held as a dependent of '15', so the range 10 to 15 is not written\". Hmm, the quote 'to' alone is a substring but is it \"words of the message\"? The checker accepts any quoted substring. But rule: quote the words. I'll quote '10 to 15'.\n\n67 L1: \"'I need the revenue trend legible' is a declarative clause written as `interrogative`\".\n\n72 L3: \"'the font smaler' splits the nominal: 'the' stands as a modifier of the clause and 'smaler' is its verb\".\n\n73 L5: \"'clear' in 'next clear the top two' is held as a modifier, so that command has no clause\".\n\n79 L5: \"'halve' is the verb of 'halve teh Shelve label size' but is held as a dependent of 'size'\".\n\n80 L3: \"'for' in '15, 15 for 1 star' is a linking word held as a dependent inside one nominal with head 'star'\".\n\n87 L1: \"'change' is the first word and only verb of the message but is held as a dependent of 'Review', so the command is lost\".\n\n90 L3: \"'then' in 'then the boy' is a dependent of 'boy', so a word of order describes the boy\".\n\n91 L1: \"'rotate' is the verb of the message but stands as a nominal, and the direction word 'dwn' is made the verb\".\n\n93 L1: \"'hollow' in 'the inheritance arrows hollow' is an adjective but is held as the verb of a declarative clause\".\n\n99 L3: \"'then' in 'then a parallel chart' is a dependent of 'chart', so a word of order describes the chart\".\n\n107 L4: \"'make the bars blue' is a command with no subject, but the circuit gives it 'the top six regions' as its subject\".\